DT.AUTHORS = [{"slug":"zeno-of-citium","name":"Zeno of Citium","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 334 – 262 BC","born":"c. 334 BC","died":"c. 262 BC","origin":"Citium, Cyprus","school":"Founder of the Stoa","bio":"A merchant from Cyprus who, after a shipwreck brought him to Athens, turned to philosophy. Around 300 BC he began teaching at the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Porch, which gave the school its name. He taught that virtue is the only true good and that a good life means living in agreement with nature.","life":[{"h":"From merchant to philosopher","p":["Zeno was born around 334 BC in Citium, a harbour town on Cyprus where Greek and Phoenician culture met. His father Mnaseas was a merchant, and Zeno followed him into the trade. Ancient writers describe him as tall, thin and dark-skinned, with a slight stoop and a habit of keeping to himself.","The story of how he became a philosopher was told for centuries. In his early thirties Zeno was carrying a cargo of purple dye from Phoenicia when his ship was wrecked near Piraeus, the port of Athens. Stranded in the city, he sat down in a bookshop and listened to the bookseller read from the second book of Xenophon's memoirs of Socrates. Zeno was so taken that he asked where men like that could be found. At that moment Crates the Cynic happened to walk past, and the bookseller pointed and said: \"Follow that man.\" Zeno later joked that he had made a prosperous voyage on the day he was shipwrecked."]},{"h":"Learning from many teachers","p":["Crates taught him the Cynic way of life: plain clothes, little food, no shame about poverty and a sharp disregard for convention. Zeno admired the toughness but was too modest for some of the Cynic stunts, and Crates is said to have teased him for it. He went on to study logic with Stilpo of Megara and attended the lectures of Polemo, head of Plato's Academy. Polemo complained that Zeno slipped in through the garden gate, stole his doctrines and dressed them up in Phoenician clothes. That was close to the truth: Zeno built something new out of everything he had heard."]},{"h":"The Painted Porch","p":["Around 300 BC Zeno began to teach in the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Porch, a public colonnade on the north side of the Athenian Agora decorated with paintings of the Battle of Marathon. Anyone could walk in and listen. His followers were first called Zenonians and later simply Stoics, after the place where they met.","Zeno divided philosophy into three parts: logic, physics and ethics. The goal of life, he taught, is \"to live in agreement\", which his successors expanded to \"living in agreement with nature\". Virtue is the only true good and vice the only true evil. Health, wealth, reputation and even life itself are \"indifferent\": some are reasonably preferred, but none of them can make a person good or bad. Strong passions such as fear, craving and rage are errors of judgment that a trained mind can correct. The universe is ordered by reason, the logos, and a human being is a small part of that rational whole.","His early book, the Republic, sketched a Cynic-flavoured ideal society without temples, law courts or money, bound together by love and virtue. Later Stoics found it embarrassing and tried to explain it away."]},{"h":"Life in Athens","p":["Zeno lived on bread, figs, honey and water, and his frugality became proverbial: Athenians said someone was \"more self-controlled than Zeno the philosopher\". He disliked crowds and would sit at the end of a couch so that at least one side of him was free. He is credited with the remark that we have two ears and one mouth so that we may listen more and speak less.","King Antigonus II Gonatas of Macedon admired him, attended his lectures and invited him to court. Zeno, by then old, declined and sent his students Persaeus and Philonides instead. Athens honoured him with a public decree, a golden crown and a tomb at public cost, stating that he had made the young men who came to him better people and had lived a life that matched his teaching."]},{"h":"Death and legacy","p":["According to Diogenes Laertius, Zeno died around 262 BC after tripping as he left the school and breaking a toe. He struck the ground with his hand, quoted a line from a tragedy, \"I come, why do you call me?\", and held his breath until he died.","None of his writings survive except short fragments quoted by later authors. Yet every Stoic who followed, from Cleanthes to Marcus Aurelius, built on the house he founded."]}],"quotes":["q-zeno-of-citium-01","q-zeno-of-citium-02","q-zeno-of-citium-03","q-zeno-of-citium-04","q-zeno-of-citium-05","q-zeno-of-citium-06","q-zeno-of-citium-07","q-zeno-of-citium-08","q-zeno-of-citium-09","q-zeno-of-citium-10","q-zeno-of-citium-11","q-zeno-of-citium-12","q-zeno-of-citium-13","q-zeno-of-citium-14","q-zeno-of-citium-15"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust, National Archaeological Museum, Naples. Image: Jeremy Weate from Abuja, Nigeria, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY 2.0</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeno_of_Citium_-_Museo_archeologico_nazionale_di_Napoli.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"cleanthes","name":"Cleanthes","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 330 – 230 BC","born":"c. 330 BC","died":"c. 230 BC","origin":"Assos, Asia Minor","school":"Stoic, second head of the Stoa","bio":"A former boxer who earned his living drawing water at night so he could study with Zeno by day. He succeeded Zeno as head of the school. His Hymn to Zeus, one of the few early Stoic texts to survive complete, praises the reason that orders the world.","life":[{"h":"The boxer with four drachmas","p":["Cleanthes was born around 330 BC in Assos, a town in the Troad on the north-west coast of Asia Minor. Before philosophy he was a boxer. He arrived in Athens, so the story goes, with only four drachmas in his pocket, met Zeno and decided to stay.","To pay for his studies he worked at night drawing water for the city's gardens and kneading dough for a baker, which earned him the nickname Phreantles, \"the Well-Filler\". Because he seemed to have no visible means of support, he was once summoned before the Areopagus council to explain how he lived. He brought the gardener he worked for as his witness. The council was so impressed that it offered him ten minae as a gift, but Zeno told him not to accept it."]},{"h":"The slow student who carried the load","p":["Cleanthes was not quick. Fellow students called him \"the donkey\", and he answered that he alone was strong enough to carry Zeno's burden. He compared himself to a hard writing tablet: difficult to write on, but it keeps what has been written. He was so poor that he took notes on oyster shells and the shoulder blades of oxen because he could not afford papyrus.","He studied under Zeno for nineteen years, and when Zeno died around 262 BC, Cleanthes became head of the Stoa. He led the school for more than thirty years."]},{"h":"The Hymn to Zeus","p":["Cleanthes' most famous work is the Hymn to Zeus, the longest piece of early Stoic writing that survives in full. It praises Zeus not as a bearded god on a mountain but as the reason that runs through the whole universe, steering everything and turning disorder into order. Only the bad, the hymn says, act against this order, and in doing so they harm themselves.","Another short poem of his became one of the best-known prayers in Stoic literature: \"Lead me, Zeus, and you, Destiny, wherever you have ordained for me. I will follow without hesitation; and if I am unwilling and become bad, I will follow anyway.\" Epictetus closed his Handbook with these lines, and Seneca translated them into Latin, adding his own famous line: \"The fates lead the willing and drag the unwilling.\""]},{"h":"Teaching and physics","p":["Cleanthes gave Stoic physics much of its vocabulary. He described the soul and the cosmos as held together by tension, a kind of inner tone, and he saw the sun as the ruling part of the universe. He wrote a commentary on Heraclitus, whose ideas about fire and the logos fed into Stoic thinking. He was also a strict moralist: he held that virtue, once acquired, cannot be lost, and that pleasure is not natural at all. Plutarch reports that he thought Aristarchus of Samos should be charged with impiety for suggesting that the earth moves around the sun.","He was less gifted at logic than at living. His own student Chrysippus is said to have told him: \"Just give me the doctrines, I will find the proofs myself.\""]},{"h":"A quiet end","p":["Cleanthes died around 230 BC in extreme old age; sources give his age as eighty or even ninety-nine. He had a sore on his gum, and his doctors told him to fast for two days. When the sore healed and they allowed him to eat again, he refused, saying that he had already come so far along the road. He kept fasting until he died, and Chrysippus succeeded him as head of the school.","What Cleanthes gave the Stoa was continuity. He was not its most original mind, but his patience, his poverty and his loyalty to Zeno kept the school alive through its first generation."]}],"quotes":["q-cleanthes-01","q-cleanthes-02","q-cleanthes-03","q-cleanthes-04","q-cleanthes-05","q-cleanthes-06","q-cleanthes-07","q-cleanthes-08","q-cleanthes-09","q-cleanthes-10","q-cleanthes-11","q-cleanthes-12"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust, Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek, Copenhagen. Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cleanthes_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN_1795.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"chrysippus","name":"Chrysippus","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 279 – 206 BC","born":"c. 279 BC","died":"c. 206 BC","origin":"Soli, Cilicia","school":"Stoic, third head of the Stoa","bio":"The great systematiser of Stoicism. He is said to have written more than 700 works, especially on logic, and the ancients said that without Chrysippus there would be no Stoa. Only fragments of his writing survive.","life":[{"h":"The runner from Soli","p":["Chrysippus was born around 279 BC in Soli, a Greek city in Cilicia on the south coast of modern Turkey. As a young man he was a long-distance runner. After his inheritance was confiscated, he came to Athens and studied with Cleanthes, and for a time he also attended the Academy, where the sceptics Arcesilaus and Lacydes taught. That training in arguing both sides of a question shaped the rest of his life.","He was small, and a statue of him in the Kerameikos was said to be almost hidden behind a nearby equestrian statue, so the comic poets called him \"Krysippus\", the hidden one. The well-known type of his portrait shows him seated, leaning forward with one hand stretched out, as if counting arguments on his fingers."]},{"h":"The second founder","p":["Chrysippus became head of the Stoa after Cleanthes died around 230 BC. At that point the school was under heavy attack from the sceptical Academy, which argued that the Stoics could not show that any knowledge was certain. Chrysippus answered every objection and rebuilt Stoic teaching into a complete system. Ancient writers said: \"If there had been no Chrysippus, there would have been no Stoa.\" His opponent Carneades later turned the line around: \"If there had been no Chrysippus, there would have been no me.\"","He is said to have written more than 705 books. Almost all of them are lost; we know his views from quotations by friends and enemies, and from charred papyrus rolls found at Herculaneum."]},{"h":"Logic and language","p":["Chrysippus is one of the great logicians of the ancient world. Where Aristotle's logic dealt with terms such as \"all men\" and \"some animals\", Chrysippus worked with whole statements joined by \"if\", \"and\" and \"or\". He set out five basic forms of valid argument, such as \"If it is day, it is light; it is day; therefore it is light\", and showed how more complex arguments can be reduced to them. Modern propositional logic, developed in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, rediscovered much of what he had already worked out."]},{"h":"Fate and responsibility","p":["Stoics believed that everything happens according to fate, a chain of causes running through the universe. Critics asked: if everything is fated, how can anyone be blamed or praised? Chrysippus answered with an image preserved by Cicero. Push a cylinder and it rolls; push a cone and it spins. The push starts the motion, but the shape of the object decides how it moves. Events in the world give us the push, but how we respond depends on our own character and judgment. That is where our responsibility lies."]},{"h":"The passions","p":["Chrysippus also worked out the Stoic account of emotion. Fear, grief, craving and wild delight are not forces that overwhelm reason from outside; they are judgments that something bad or good is happening, followed by an excessive impulse. Because they are judgments, they can be examined and changed. His four books On the Passions became the basis for the Stoic \"therapy of the soul\" that Seneca and Epictetus later practised."]},{"h":"A famous death","p":["Chrysippus died around 206 BC, aged about seventy-three. Diogenes Laertius gives two stories. In one, he drank undiluted wine at a sacrifice, grew dizzy and died a few days later. In the other, he saw a donkey eating his figs, told an old woman to give the animal some neat wine to wash them down, and laughed so hard at his own joke that he died.","Whatever the truth, the Stoicism that Romans like Cato, Seneca and Marcus Aurelius received was, in its structure, the Stoicism of Chrysippus."]}],"quotes":["q-chrysippus-01","q-chrysippus-02","q-chrysippus-03","q-chrysippus-04","q-chrysippus-05","q-chrysippus-06","q-chrysippus-07","q-chrysippus-08","q-chrysippus-09"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust, Roman copy of a Hellenistic original. Image: Unknown artist, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chrysippus_bust.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"zeno-of-tarsus","name":"Zeno of Tarsus","group":"Stoics","dates":"fl. c. 200 BC","born":"unknown","died":"unknown","origin":"Tarsus, Cilicia","school":"Stoic, fourth head of the Stoa","bio":"Pupil and successor of Chrysippus as head of the school. He wrote little but had many students. He is reported to have doubted the Stoic doctrine that the universe is periodically destroyed by fire.","life":[{"h":"A quiet successor","p":["Zeno of Tarsus was the fourth head of the Stoa. He was born in Tarsus in Cilicia, the same region as his teacher Chrysippus; some sources say his family came from Sidon in Phoenicia. His father was named Dioscorides. He came to Athens as a young man, studied under Chrysippus and took over the school when Chrysippus died around 206 BC.","He should not be confused with the founder, Zeno of Citium, who had died more than fifty years earlier. Ancient writers already had to keep apart several philosophers named Zeno, and Diogenes Laertius lists eight of them."]},{"h":"What we know","p":["Very little survives about Zeno of Tarsus. Diogenes Laertius says he wrote only a few books but had a great many students. In other words, he seems to have been a teacher more than an author, someone who kept the school running after the towering output of Chrysippus.","One doctrine is linked with his name. The early Stoics believed that the universe goes through endless cycles: at the end of each one, everything returns to pure fire in a great \"conflagration\", and then the world is reborn exactly as before. Zeno of Tarsus is reported to have suspended judgment on this teaching. He did not reject it outright, but he would not affirm it either. It is the first sign of a more cautious attitude to Stoic physics that later heads of the school, such as Diogenes of Babylon and Panaetius, carried further."]},{"h":"Why there is no portrait","p":["No statue, bust or coin with a reliable identification of Zeno of Tarsus is known. That is why his challenge coin carries the gold Stoa column instead of a face: the column stands for the school itself, which he served as head."]},{"h":"No direct quotes","p":["None of his own words have come down to us. Any line attributed to him today should be treated with suspicion. For the Daily Thoughts app this means his page will mostly serve as a link in the chain from Chrysippus to Diogenes of Babylon, rather than as the source of one-pagers."]},{"h":"His place in the story","p":["Zeno of Tarsus led the Stoa during a period when Athens was no longer a major political power but was still the intellectual capital of the Greek world. Rome was rising in the west and would soon reach into Greece itself. The school he handed on to Diogenes of Babylon was stable, respected and increasingly cautious about its boldest claims. That careful, practical Stoicism is the version that would soon travel to Rome and win over its elite."]}],"quotes":[],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"diogenes-of-babylon","name":"Diogenes of Babylon","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 230 – 150 BC","born":"c. 230 BC","died":"c. 150 BC","origin":"Seleucia on the Tigris","school":"Stoic, head of the Stoa","bio":"Head of the Stoa in the second century BC. In 155 BC he went to Rome with the Athenian embassy of philosophers, and his lectures there helped bring Stoicism to the Romans. He wrote on ethics, language and music.","life":[{"h":"From the Tigris to Athens","p":["Diogenes was born around 230 BC in Seleucia on the Tigris, the great Greek city founded near ancient Babylon, which is why he was called \"the Babylonian\". He came to Athens, studied with Chrysippus and then with Zeno of Tarsus, and succeeded Zeno as head of the Stoa. He led the school for several decades and lived to over eighty."]},{"h":"Language, music and the mind","p":["Diogenes wrote on a wide range of subjects. His handbook On Voice set out the Stoic theory of language: sounds, letters, parts of speech and the difference between a mere noise and a meaningful word. It shaped Greek and Roman grammar for centuries. He also wrote On Music, arguing that music has real effects on character, a claim the Epicurean Philodemus later attacked point by point in a book whose remains were found at Herculaneum. In ethics he refined the Stoic idea of value, explaining how things that are not good in themselves, such as health or money, can still be rationally chosen."]},{"h":"The philosophers' embassy of 155 BC","p":["Diogenes is best remembered for a journey to Rome. In 155 BC Athens was fined 500 talents for raiding the town of Oropus. To appeal, the Athenians sent three of their leading philosophers as ambassadors: Carneades of the Academy, Critolaus of the Peripatetic school and Diogenes for the Stoics.","While they waited for the Senate, the three men gave public lectures, and young Romans flocked to hear them. Carneades caused a scandal by speaking in praise of justice one day and arguing against it the next. Cato the Elder, alarmed that Greek cleverness would corrupt Roman youth, urged the Senate to settle the matter quickly and send the philosophers home. The fine was reduced to 100 talents. Ancient writers praised Diogenes in particular for his sober and restrained style of speaking. For many Romans, this was their first direct encounter with Greek philosophy, and with Stoicism in particular."]},{"h":"The grain merchant of Rhodes","p":["Cicero preserves a famous debate between Diogenes and his student Antipater. A merchant brings a cargo of grain to Rhodes during a famine. He knows that several other grain ships are on their way and will arrive soon. Must he tell the Rhodians, which will lower his price, or may he keep quiet and sell at the famine price?","Diogenes argued that the merchant must not lie, but he is not obliged to volunteer every piece of information that would help the buyer. Antipater argued that honesty requires full disclosure, because we are all members of one human community. The case is still discussed in business ethics today."]},{"h":"His students","p":["Diogenes taught many of the people who would carry Stoicism forward: Antipater of Tarsus, who succeeded him; Panaetius of Rhodes, who brought the school to the Roman elite; and Boethus of Sidon. He died around 150 to 140 BC."]},{"h":"Why there is no portrait","p":["No reliable portrait of Diogenes of Babylon exists, so his coin carries the gold Stoa column. Only fragments of his writings survive, quoted by Cicero, Philodemus and others, so genuine quotations are scarce."]}],"quotes":["q-diogenes-of-babylon-01","q-diogenes-of-babylon-02","q-diogenes-of-babylon-03"],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"antipater-of-tarsus","name":"Antipater of Tarsus","group":"Stoics","dates":"died c. 130 BC","born":"unknown","died":"c. 130 BC","origin":"Tarsus, Cilicia","school":"Stoic, head of the Stoa","bio":"Successor of Diogenes of Babylon and teacher of Panaetius. He is remembered for his debates with the sceptic Carneades and for his discussion of honesty in trade, which Cicero retold in On Duties.","life":[{"h":"Successor of Diogenes","p":["Antipater was born in Tarsus in Cilicia and studied in Athens under Diogenes of Babylon. When Diogenes died, around the middle of the second century BC, Antipater became head of the Stoa. He led it until his death around 130 BC, when his student Panaetius took over."]},{"h":"The pen-shouter","p":["Antipater's great opponent was Carneades, head of the sceptical Academy and one of the most brilliant debaters of the ancient world. Carneades attacked Stoic teaching in public lectures, and Antipater did not dare to face him in open argument. Instead he wrote book after book in reply. For this he was mocked as kalamoboas, \"the pen-shouter\", a man who shouted with his pen instead of his voice. Plutarch tells the story; it shows how much pressure the Stoics were under in this period."]},{"h":"Refining the goal of life","p":["Antipater worked hard on how to state the Stoic goal of life precisely. His versions run roughly: \"to live continually choosing what is in accordance with nature and rejecting what is contrary to it\", or \"to do everything in one's power, continuously and without wavering, to obtain the things that are by nature preferred\".","The point is subtle but important. The Stoic does aim at real things in the world: health, safety for family, success in a task. But what makes the life good is the skill and steadiness of the aiming, not whether the target is actually hit. Cicero later used the image of an archer: the archer does everything possible to hit the mark, yet the goal is to shoot well, not merely to strike the target. This distinction between doing your part and controlling the outcome is at the heart of later Stoic practice."]},{"h":"The grain merchant of Rhodes","p":["In the debate about the merchant who brings grain to Rhodes during a famine, knowing that other ships are coming, Antipater took the strict line against his teacher Diogenes. Because all people belong to one community, he argued, the merchant must tell the buyers everything he knows. Hiding the truth for profit is not compatible with honesty, even if no direct lie is told. Cicero, who reports the case, sides with Antipater."]},{"h":"On marriage","p":["A long fragment of Antipater's essay On Marriage survives in the anthology of Stobaeus. It argues that marriage and raising children are among the most necessary and fitting duties of a good citizen, and describes marriage as a full partnership in which husband and wife share body, soul and property. This warm view of family life was taken up by later Stoics such as Musonius Rufus."]},{"h":"Why there is no portrait","p":["No portrait of Antipater is known, so his coin shows the gold Stoa column. His works survive only in fragments, which means that there are very few direct quotes that can safely be attributed to him."]}],"quotes":["q-antipater-of-tarsus-01","q-antipater-of-tarsus-02","q-antipater-of-tarsus-03"],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"panaetius","name":"Panaetius of Rhodes","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 185 – 110 BC","born":"c. 185 BC","died":"c. 110 BC","origin":"Rhodes","school":"Stoic, head of the Stoa","bio":"Friend of Scipio Aemilianus and a central figure in bringing Stoicism into Roman public life. He softened the school's strict teaching to speak to people making progress rather than only to the perfect sage. Cicero's On Duties draws heavily on his work.","life":[{"h":"A nobleman from Rhodes","p":["Panaetius was born around 185 BC into a leading family of Lindos on the island of Rhodes. His education was wide. He studied in Pergamon with Crates of Mallos, the famous scholar of Homer, and then in Athens under the Stoics Diogenes of Babylon and Antipater of Tarsus."]},{"h":"In Rome with Scipio","p":["In the 140s BC Panaetius went to Rome and became a close friend of Scipio Aemilianus, the general who had destroyed Carthage in 146 BC. He belonged to the circle around Scipio that included the statesman Laelius and the Greek historian Polybius. When Scipio led an embassy to Egypt and the eastern kingdoms around 140 to 139 BC, Panaetius travelled with him.","Through these friendships, Stoicism found a home in the Roman ruling class. Panaetius showed Romans who led armies and governed provinces that Stoic ethics could be a practical guide for public life, not just a doctrine for sages."]},{"h":"Head of the Stoa","p":["When Antipater died around 129 BC, Panaetius became head of the school in Athens and led it for twenty years until his death around 109 BC. Historians see him as the start of the \"Middle Stoa\".","He softened several of the school's harder positions. He doubted the doctrine that the world would end in a great conflagration, rejected most forms of divination and astrology, and spoke with admiration of Plato and Aristotle. Instead of focusing on the perfect sage, who may never exist, he wrote for the person who is making progress: someone who is not yet wise but is trying to do better each day."]},{"h":"Four roles","p":["Panaetius' best-known idea is that each of us plays four roles, or personae. The first is our shared human nature as rational beings. The second is our individual nature: our talents, temperament and strengths. The third is the situation that chance has placed us in, such as wealth, family and political circumstance. The fourth is the role we choose for ourselves, such as a career or a way of life. A good life, he argued, respects all four. We should not try to imitate someone else's nature, but should act well as the particular person we are."]},{"h":"On appropriate action","p":["His most influential book was On Appropriate Action. It dealt with everyday duties: what a person owes to family, friends, country and themselves, and how to decide when these seem to clash. The book itself is lost, but Cicero used it as the model for the first two books of his own De Officiis, written in 44 BC. Through Cicero, Panaetius' practical ethics shaped the moral education of Europe for well over a thousand years."]},{"h":"Students and legacy","p":["Among his students were Posidonius of Apamea, who became the most learned Stoic of his age, and Hecato of Rhodes. Panaetius is said to have declined honorary Athenian citizenship, remarking that one city was enough for a sensible man.","No authentic portrait of Panaetius is known, so his coin carries the gold Stoa column. His words survive mostly in Cicero's Latin retelling rather than as direct quotations."]}],"quotes":["q-panaetius-01","q-panaetius-02","q-panaetius-03"],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"cato-the-younger","name":"Cato the Younger","group":"Stoics","dates":"95 – 46 BC","born":"95 BC","died":"46 BC","origin":"Rome","school":"Stoic statesman","bio":"Roman senator famous for his integrity and his opposition to Julius Caesar. He lived his Stoicism in public life rather than writing it down. After Caesar's victory he took his own life at Utica rather than accept Caesar's pardon, and later Stoics held him up as a model.","life":[{"h":"A stern young Roman","p":["Marcus Porcius Cato was born in 95 BC, the great-grandson of Cato the Elder, the famously severe censor. Orphaned as a child, he grew up in the house of his uncle Marcus Livius Drusus. Plutarch says he was stubborn and serious even as a boy. When the dictator Sulla's men were killing political enemies, the young Cato asked his tutor for a sword so he could free Rome from tyranny.","He studied Stoic philosophy with Antipater of Tyre and later kept the Stoic Athenodorus Cordylion in his own household. He trained himself in hardship: walking bareheaded in heat and rain, travelling on foot while his friends rode, and dressing in a plain dark toga instead of the fashionable purple."]},{"h":"Integrity in office","p":["As military tribune in Macedonia in 67 BC, he shared the work and food of his soldiers. As quaestor in 64 BC he took control of the treasury, refused to pay out false claims and made the clerks who had grown rich on fraud answer for it. He became known as the one man in Rome who could not be bribed.","In December 63 BC the Senate debated what to do with the arrested conspirators of Catiline. Julius Caesar argued for life imprisonment. Cato replied with a speech so forceful that the Senate voted for execution. The historian Sallust later contrasted the two men: Caesar won glory by giving, Cato by refusing."]},{"h":"Against the strong men","p":["For the rest of his life Cato opposed the men who were turning the Republic into a contest of personal power: Pompey, Crassus and above all Caesar. He used every legal tool, including speaking until sunset to block votes. In 58 BC his enemies sent him away to annex Cyprus. He collected the royal treasure and brought nearly 7,000 talents back to Rome, with accounts so careful that he kept two copies. Both were lost on the way, one in a shipwreck and one in a fire."]},{"h":"Civil war and Utica","p":["When civil war broke out in 49 BC, Cato sided with Pompey as the lesser danger. After Caesar's victory at Pharsalus in 48 BC, Cato led the surviving troops on a long march across the Libyan desert on foot to join the republican forces in Africa. He took command of the city of Utica.","In April 46 BC Caesar crushed the republicans at Thapsus. Cato arranged ships for everyone in Utica who wanted to escape, then spent the night reading Plato's Phaedo, the dialogue about the death of Socrates and the immortality of the soul. He stabbed himself with his sword. His doctors found him still alive and bandaged the wound, but he tore it open and died. When Caesar heard, he said: \"Cato, I begrudge you your death, as you begrudged me the saving of your life.\""]},{"h":"A symbol of freedom","p":["Cato left no writings except one speech, but he became the Stoic hero in action. Seneca returned to him again and again as the model of a man who could not be broken. Lucan made him the moral centre of his poem on the civil war. Dante placed him as guardian of the shores of Purgatory. Joseph Addison's play Cato (1713) was a favourite of George Washington, who had it performed for his army at Valley Forge in 1778."]}],"quotes":["q-cato-the-younger-01","q-cato-the-younger-02","q-cato-the-younger-03","q-cato-the-younger-04","q-cato-the-younger-05","q-cato-the-younger-06"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Bronze bust from Volubilis, Archaeological Museum, Rabat. Image: Laci3, <a href=\"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC0</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_de_Cat%C3%B3n_el_Joven_-_Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_de_Rabat_(1).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"seneca","name":"Seneca","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. 4 BC – AD 65","born":"c. 4 BC","died":"AD 65","origin":"Corduba, Hispania","school":"Stoic","bio":"Philosopher, playwright and adviser to the emperor Nero. His Letters to Lucilius and essays such as On the Shortness of Life are among the most widely read Stoic writings. Accused of conspiracy, he was ordered by Nero to take his own life.","life":[{"h":"From Corduba to Rome","p":["Lucius Annaeus Seneca was born around 4 BC in Corduba, in the Roman province of Hispania, today Córdoba in Spain. His father, Seneca the Elder, was a wealthy writer on rhetoric. One of his brothers, Gallio, appears in the Acts of the Apostles as the governor before whom Paul was brought in Corinth.","Seneca was taken to Rome as a child. He suffered from poor health all his life, probably asthma or a lung disease, and spent several years recovering in Egypt with his aunt. As a young man he studied philosophy with the Stoic Attalus and with Sotion and Papirius Fabianus of the Sextii, a short-lived Roman school that combined Stoic and Pythagorean ideas. For a while he even gave up meat."]},{"h":"Exile on Corsica","p":["Seneca made his name as an orator and senator. According to later writers, the emperor Caligula was so jealous of his speaking that he nearly had him killed. In AD 41 the new emperor Claudius exiled him to Corsica, accused of adultery with Julia Livilla, the emperor's niece. He spent eight years on the island and wrote consolations to his mother Helvia and to the court official Polybius, both meditations on how to bear misfortune."]},{"h":"Tutor and adviser to Nero","p":["In AD 49 Agrippina, Claudius' new wife, recalled Seneca to tutor her son Nero. When Nero became emperor in 54 at the age of sixteen, Seneca and the praetorian prefect Burrus guided the government. The first five years of Nero's reign were later remembered as a time of good rule.","Then came compromise. Nero had his mother murdered in 59, and Seneca wrote the letter to the Senate justifying it. He also became enormously rich, with a fortune Tacitus put at 300 million sesterces, and critics called him a hypocrite who preached poverty from a palace. Seneca answered in On the Happy Life: the wise person may own wealth, as long as wealth does not own them."]},{"h":"Retreat and writing","p":["After Burrus died in 62, Seneca's position became dangerous. He asked to retire, offered Nero his fortune, and withdrew from public life. In these last years he wrote his greatest work, the 124 Letters to Lucilius, along with Natural Questions. Earlier works include On Anger, On the Shortness of Life, On Tranquility of Mind and On Providence. He also wrote tragedies, such as Medea, Thyestes and Phaedra, which later shaped Elizabethan drama."]},{"h":"Death","p":["In AD 65 a plot to kill Nero, the Pisonian conspiracy, was discovered. Seneca was probably not involved, but Nero ordered him to die. Tacitus describes the scene. Seneca told his weeping friends that he was leaving them the one thing he still had, the pattern of his life. He opened his veins, but because he was old and thin the blood flowed slowly. He took poison, which did not work, and finally was carried into a hot bath, where he died. His wife Paulina tried to die with him but was saved on Nero's orders."]},{"h":"Legacy","p":["Seneca is the most readable of the Stoics. His letters are short, practical and personal, full of everyday scenes and striking lines. Early Christian writers admired him so much that a fake correspondence between Seneca and Saint Paul circulated for centuries. Montaigne, Shakespeare's contemporaries and modern readers have all returned to him for practical advice on time, anger, grief and fear."]}],"quotes":["q-seneca-01","q-seneca-02","q-seneca-03","q-seneca-04","q-seneca-05","q-seneca-06","q-seneca-07","q-seneca-08","q-seneca-09","q-seneca-10","q-seneca-11","q-seneca-12","q-seneca-13","q-seneca-14","q-seneca-15","q-seneca-16","q-seneca-17","q-seneca-18","q-seneca-19","q-seneca-20","q-seneca-21","q-seneca-22","q-seneca-23","q-seneca-24","q-seneca-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble herm, Antikensammlung Berlin. Image: Calidius, <a href=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seneca-berlinantikensammlung-2_copy.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"musonius-rufus","name":"Musonius Rufus","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. AD 30 – 101","born":"c. AD 30","died":"c. AD 101","origin":"Volsinii, Etruria","school":"Stoic","bio":"Roman Stoic teacher, exiled several times by emperors, and the teacher of Epictetus. His lectures, preserved by his students, stress practice over theory and argue that women are just as capable of philosophy as men.","life":[{"h":"A Roman knight who taught in Greek","p":["Gaius Musonius Rufus was born before AD 30 in Volsinii, an old Etruscan town in central Italy, today Bolsena. He belonged to the equestrian order, the class of Roman knights just below the senators. He taught philosophy in Rome, in Greek, and became so respected that later writers called him \"the Roman Socrates\"."]},{"h":"Exile under Nero","p":["In AD 65, after the Pisonian conspiracy against Nero, Musonius was exiled to Gyara, a barren, waterless island in the Aegean used as a prison for political enemies. People still sailed out to visit him and hear him teach. A later story says he discovered a spring on the island, making it more livable. He was recalled after Nero's death in 68.","In 69, the year of the four emperors, armies were marching on Rome. Musonius joined a delegation of senators sent to meet the troops of Vespasian's general outside the city. He walked among the soldiers lecturing on the blessings of peace and the dangers of war. Tacitus says the soldiers mocked him and he barely escaped being beaten.","In 70 he prosecuted Publius Egnatius Celer, a Stoic who had given false testimony that led to the death of the senator Barea Soranus, and won. When Vespasian expelled philosophers from Rome in 71, Musonius was at first allowed to stay, but he was later exiled again and returned only under the emperor Titus."]},{"h":"Practice above theory","p":["Musonius wrote nothing himself. His teaching survives through notes taken by a student named Lucius, preserved as some twenty-one short lectures, along with sayings quoted by later writers. They are practical and direct. Philosophy, he said, is useless unless it changes how you live, just as medicine is useless unless it heals. Virtue is not only known but trained, through hardship, simple food, cold and heat, and steady practice."]},{"h":"Women, marriage and family","p":["Musonius held views that were unusual in Rome. He argued that women have the same capacity for reason and virtue as men and should study philosophy. Daughters should receive the same education as sons. Marriage should be a full partnership of two people who share everything and care for each other. He taught that men as well as women should limit sex to marriage, opposed the common practice of abandoning unwanted newborns, and praised large families."]},{"h":"Simple living","p":["He recommended plain food, mostly vegetables, grains, fruit and cheese, prepared simply. He thought clothes and houses should protect the body, not display wealth, and that farming was a fitting occupation for a philosopher, because it kept body and mind healthy and left time to teach."]},{"h":"His students","p":["His most famous student was Epictetus, who heard him while still a slave and quotes him with deep respect in the Discourses. Another was the orator Dio Chrysostom. Through Epictetus, Musonius' style of practical, demanding Stoicism reached Marcus Aurelius.","No reliable portrait of Musonius is known, so his coin carries the gold Stoa column. The lecture notes of Lucius do give us his ideas in some detail, so short quotations from them can be used for one-pagers."]}],"quotes":["q-musonius-rufus-01","q-musonius-rufus-02","q-musonius-rufus-03","q-musonius-rufus-04","q-musonius-rufus-05","q-musonius-rufus-06","q-musonius-rufus-07","q-musonius-rufus-08","q-musonius-rufus-09","q-musonius-rufus-10","q-musonius-rufus-11","q-musonius-rufus-12","q-musonius-rufus-13","q-musonius-rufus-14","q-musonius-rufus-15","q-musonius-rufus-16"],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"epictetus","name":"Epictetus","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. AD 50 – 135","born":"c. AD 50","died":"c. AD 135","origin":"Hierapolis, Phrygia","school":"Stoic","bio":"Born a slave, he studied under Musonius Rufus and was later freed. After philosophers were expelled from Rome he taught at Nicopolis in Greece. His student Arrian recorded his teaching in the Discourses and the Handbook, built around one idea: separate what is up to us from what is not.","life":[{"h":"Born a slave","p":["Epictetus was born around AD 50 in Hierapolis, a city in Phrygia in modern Turkey. He was born into slavery, and his name simply means \"acquired\". His mother was a slave, and we do not know his birth name. As a boy he came to Rome as the property of Epaphroditus, a powerful freedman who served as secretary to the emperor Nero.","Epictetus was lame. One story, told by the Christian writer Origen, says his master once twisted his leg to test him. Epictetus calmly warned that the leg would break, and when it did, he said only: \"Didn't I tell you it would break?\" Another source says the lameness came from rheumatism. Either way, it became part of his legend."]},{"h":"Student of Musonius","p":["While still a slave, Epictetus was allowed to attend the lectures of Musonius Rufus, the leading Stoic teacher in Rome. Musonius trained him hard. Epictetus later recalled how Musonius would point out his faults so precisely that each student felt the lecture was aimed at him alone. At some point after Nero's death in 68, Epictetus was freed and began teaching philosophy himself."]},{"h":"The school at Nicopolis","p":["Around 93 or 94 the emperor Domitian expelled all philosophers from Rome. Epictetus moved to Nicopolis, a city on the west coast of Greece, and opened a school that attracted young men from across the empire, many from wealthy Roman families. The emperor Hadrian is said to have visited him.","He lived with very little: a bed, a mat and a lamp. When his iron lamp was stolen, he bought a clay one and said the thief would have to become a thief again to take the next one. Late in life he took in a child whose parents were going to abandon it, and asked a woman to help him raise it."]},{"h":"The Discourses and the Handbook","p":["Epictetus wrote nothing. A student, the historian Arrian of Nicomedia, took notes on his lectures and published them as the Discourses. Four of the original eight books survive. Arrian also produced a short summary, the Enchiridion or Handbook, meant to be kept close at hand.","The Handbook opens with the idea at the core of his teaching: \"Some things are up to us and others are not.\" Our judgments, our desires, our choices and our actions are up to us. Our body, possessions, reputation and position are not. Freedom and peace come from caring about the first group and accepting the second. What disturbs us, he said, is not events but our judgments about them."]},{"h":"Influence","p":["Marcus Aurelius was introduced to Epictetus' notes by his teacher Junius Rusticus and quotes him throughout the Meditations. Christian monks adapted the Handbook as a guide for monastic life. In the twentieth century, the psychologist Albert Ellis cited Epictetus as an inspiration for cognitive therapy. James Stockdale, a US Navy pilot held as a prisoner of war in Hanoi for over seven years, said Epictetus' teaching kept him alive.","Epictetus died around AD 135 in Nicopolis. The portrait on his coin is an imaginary one from an engraving printed in Oxford in 1715, since no portrait made from life exists."]}],"quotes":["q-epictetus-01","q-epictetus-02","q-epictetus-03","q-epictetus-04","q-epictetus-05","q-epictetus-06","q-epictetus-07","q-epictetus-08","q-epictetus-09","q-epictetus-10","q-epictetus-11","q-epictetus-12","q-epictetus-13","q-epictetus-14","q-epictetus-15","q-epictetus-16","q-epictetus-17","q-epictetus-18","q-epictetus-19","q-epictetus-20","q-epictetus-21","q-epictetus-22","q-epictetus-23","q-epictetus-24","q-epictetus-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Engraved frontispiece, Oxford 1715 (an imagined portrait). Image: William Sonmans / Michael Burghers, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epicteti_Enchiridion_Latinis_versibus_adumbratum_(Oxford_1715)_frontispiece.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"junius-rusticus","name":"Junius Rusticus","group":"Stoics","dates":"c. AD 100 – 170","born":"c. AD 100","died":"c. AD 170","origin":"Rome","school":"Stoic statesman","bio":"Stoic philosopher, twice consul and Prefect of Rome, and the teacher Marcus Aurelius thanks most warmly in Book 1 of the Meditations. It was Rusticus who lent the young Marcus his own copy of Epictetus.","life":[{"h":"A family of Stoic senators","p":["Quintus Junius Rusticus was born around AD 100 into a Roman senatorial family with a strong Stoic tradition. He was very probably the grandson of Arulenus Rusticus, a Stoic senator who was executed by the emperor Domitian in 93 for writing a book in praise of Thrasea Paetus, another Stoic who had died resisting Nero. For the Rustici, Stoicism was not an academic subject but a family inheritance, tied to the memory of men who had paid with their lives for speaking freely."]},{"h":"Teacher of Marcus Aurelius","p":["Rusticus became the most important philosophy teacher of the young Marcus Aurelius. In the first book of the Meditations, where Marcus thanks the people who shaped him, the entry for Rusticus is one of the longest. From him, Marcus writes, he learned:","that his character needed training and correction; not to be carried away by the showy rhetoric of the sophists, nor to write treatises on abstract topics or give moralising little speeches; not to parade about the house in formal dress; to write letters simply, like the one Rusticus wrote to Marcus' mother from Sinuessa; to be ready to forgive and be reconciled with those who had offended him as soon as they wanted to make peace; to read carefully and not be satisfied with a rough idea of a thing; and not to agree too quickly with people who talk a lot.","Most important of all, Rusticus lent Marcus his own copy of the notes of Epictetus' lectures. That gift set the direction of Marcus' inner life, and Epictetus is the philosopher Marcus quotes most often."]},{"h":"A career in government","p":["Rusticus was not only a teacher. He held high offices of state, serving as consul for the second time in 162, an honour Marcus gave to very few, and as urban prefect of Rome, in charge of order in the capital, from about 162 to 168. Marcus consulted him on both public and private matters.","As urban prefect, Rusticus presided over the trial of the Christian philosopher Justin Martyr and several companions around 165. The record of the trial, the Acts of Justin, preserves their exchange. Justin refused to sacrifice to the gods, and Rusticus, following the law as it stood, sentenced him to death. The episode is a reminder that a Stoic magistrate could still enforce the harsh laws of his time."]},{"h":"Honours after death","p":["Rusticus died around 170. According to a later biography of Marcus, the emperor asked the Senate to set up statues in his honour and showed him a respect he gave to few others."]},{"h":"No portrait, no writings","p":["No portrait of Junius Rusticus is known, so his coin carries the gold Stoa column. He left no writings that have survived. What we know of his teaching comes almost entirely from Marcus' grateful memory, so any one-pager for Rusticus would start from Meditations 1.7 rather than his own words."]}],"quotes":[],"credit":"No authentic ancient portrait survives, so the coin carries the Stoa column of the Stoic Ranger seal.","portrait":false},{"slug":"marcus-aurelius","name":"Marcus Aurelius","group":"Stoics","dates":"AD 121 – 180","born":"AD 121","died":"AD 180","origin":"Rome","school":"Stoic emperor","bio":"Roman emperor from 161 to 180 and the last of the Five Good Emperors. He spent most of his reign on campaign along the Danube while the Antonine Plague swept the empire. His private notebook, the Meditations, was written in Greek and never meant for publication.","life":[{"h":"A boy noticed by an emperor","p":["Marcus Annius Verus was born in Rome on 26 April AD 121 into a wealthy family of Spanish origin. His father died when he was about three, and he was raised by his mother Domitia Lucilla and his grandfather. The emperor Hadrian took an interest in the serious, honest boy and nicknamed him Verissimus, \"the most truthful\", a play on his family name.","In 138, shortly before his death, Hadrian adopted Antoninus Pius as his heir, on the condition that Antoninus in turn adopt the seventeen-year-old Marcus and the young Lucius Verus. From that day Marcus was being prepared for the throne."]},{"h":"Education and the turn to philosophy","p":["Marcus had the best teachers of his age. Marcus Cornelius Fronto, the most famous orator in Rome, taught him rhetoric, and their letters survive. But in his twenties Marcus turned more and more to philosophy. His Stoic teacher Junius Rusticus gave him the notes of Epictetus' lectures, and the first book of the Meditations thanks a long list of people, from family members to philosophers, for the qualities he learned from each.","In 145 he married Faustina, the daughter of Antoninus Pius. They had at least thirteen children, many of whom died young."]},{"h":"Emperor in hard times","p":["When Antoninus died in 161, Marcus became emperor and insisted that Lucius Verus rule with him as co-emperor. Almost at once, the empire was at war with Parthia in the east. The returning army brought back a devastating epidemic, the Antonine Plague, which killed millions across the empire over the following years.","From around 166, Germanic and Sarmatian peoples, among them the Marcomanni and Quadi, broke across the Danube frontier and even reached northern Italy. In 169 Lucius Verus died, and Marcus faced the crisis alone. With the treasury empty, he auctioned off palace treasures, including gold and crystal tableware, silk garments and jewels from the imperial household, in the Forum of Trajan rather than raise taxes on the provinces. This is the event behind the one-pager on Meditations 5.20."]},{"h":"Writing at the front","p":["Marcus spent most of the 170s with his armies on the Danube, at bases such as Carnuntum and Sirmium. There, in camp, he wrote private notes to himself in Greek. Later generations called them the Meditations; the oldest title is simply \"To Himself\". One book ends with the note \"among the Quadi, on the Granua\"; another, \"at Carnuntum\". They were never meant for publication. They are reminders to himself: to do the work in front of him, to be patient with difficult people, to remember that everything passes, and to act for the common good.","In 175 the general Avidius Cassius, believing a false rumour that Marcus had died, declared himself emperor. The revolt collapsed within months. Marcus burned Cassius' letters unread so he would not learn which friends had been involved."]},{"h":"Death and legacy","p":["Marcus died on 17 March 180, near the Danube front, probably at Vindobona (Vienna) or Sirmium. His son Commodus succeeded him and quickly abandoned his father's wars and values.","Marcus is remembered as the last of the \"Five Good Emperors\", a phrase popularised by Machiavelli and later by Edward Gibbon. His bronze equestrian statue survived in Rome because people believed it showed Constantine, the first Christian emperor. The Meditations survived in a single manuscript tradition and were first printed in 1559. Today they are the most widely read work of Stoic philosophy, and the motto of Stoic Ranger, \"Impedimentum fit via\", comes from them."]}],"quotes":["q-marcus-aurelius-01","q-marcus-aurelius-02","q-marcus-aurelius-03","q-marcus-aurelius-04","q-marcus-aurelius-05","q-marcus-aurelius-06","q-marcus-aurelius-07","q-marcus-aurelius-08","q-marcus-aurelius-09","q-marcus-aurelius-10","q-marcus-aurelius-11","q-marcus-aurelius-12","q-marcus-aurelius-13","q-marcus-aurelius-14","q-marcus-aurelius-15","q-marcus-aurelius-16","q-marcus-aurelius-17","q-marcus-aurelius-18","q-marcus-aurelius-19","q-marcus-aurelius-20","q-marcus-aurelius-21","q-marcus-aurelius-22","q-marcus-aurelius-23","q-marcus-aurelius-24","q-marcus-aurelius-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust, Glyptothek Munich. Image: Bibi Saint-Pol (own work, 2007), Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcus_Aurelius_Glyptothek_Munich.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"socrates","name":"Socrates","group":"Other authors","dates":"c. 470 – 399 BC","born":"c. 470 BC","died":"399 BC","origin":"Athens","school":"Classical Greek philosopher","bio":"The Athenian who wrote nothing and questioned everyone. Through his students, Plato above all, he became the model of the examined life. Condemned to death for impiety and corrupting the young, he drank the hemlock rather than give up philosophy. The Stoics saw him as their great forerunner.","life":[{"h":"A stonemason's son in Athens","p":["Socrates was born around 470 BC in Athens, in the district of Alopeke. His father Sophroniscus was a stonemason or sculptor, and his mother Phaenarete was a midwife. Socrates later described himself as a midwife of ideas: he did not give birth to wisdom himself, but helped others bring their own thoughts into the light and test whether they were sound.","He was famously ugly by Athenian standards, with a snub nose, bulging eyes and a thick neck, and he walked barefoot in all weathers. He married Xanthippe, who became proverbial as a difficult wife, and they had three sons."]},{"h":"A soldier","p":["Socrates served as a hoplite, a heavy infantryman, in the Peloponnesian War. At the siege of Potidaea he saved the life of the young Alcibiades and then gave up the prize for bravery to him. Alcibiades later described how Socrates stood still in thought from one dawn to the next, and how he endured the bitter northern winter in his ordinary cloak. During the retreat after the defeat at Delium in 424 BC, Socrates walked away calmly, so composed that no enemy dared attack him."]},{"h":"The oracle and the questions","p":["A friend, Chaerephon, asked the oracle at Delphi whether anyone was wiser than Socrates. The answer was: no one. Socrates was puzzled, because he knew he was not wise. He went around questioning politicians, poets and craftsmen, people with a reputation for wisdom, and found that they thought they knew things they did not. He concluded that his only advantage was that he knew he did not know.","His method was to ask questions. What is courage? What is justice? What is a good life? He would take a person's answer, draw out its consequences and show that it led to contradictions, leaving both of them to search further. He said that the unexamined life is not worth living for a human being."]},{"h":"Standing firm","p":["Twice Socrates risked his life by refusing to do wrong. In 406 BC, when the Assembly wanted to try a group of generals together illegally, he alone refused to put the motion to a vote. In 404 BC the oligarchic regime of the Thirty ordered him and four others to arrest an innocent man, Leon of Salamis. The others went; Socrates simply went home."]},{"h":"Trial and death","p":["In 399 BC Socrates was charged with impiety and corrupting the young. His defence, as Plato records it in the Apology, refused to beg for mercy. He was convicted by a narrow margin. Asked to propose a penalty, he suggested free meals at public expense, as a reward for his service to the city. The jury sentenced him to death.","Friends arranged an escape, but Socrates refused, arguing in Plato's Crito that he would not break the laws of the city that had raised him. In prison he spent his last day discussing the immortality of the soul, then drank the hemlock calmly. His last words were: \"Crito, we owe a cock to Asclepius. Pay it and do not forget.\""]},{"h":"Legacy","p":["Socrates wrote nothing. We know him through Plato, Xenophon and the comic poet Aristophanes. For the Stoics he was the closest thing to a real sage. Epictetus quotes him more than any other philosopher, and Zeno's path to philosophy began with a book about him."]}],"quotes":["q-socrates-01","q-socrates-02","q-socrates-03","q-socrates-04","q-socrates-05","q-socrates-06","q-socrates-07","q-socrates-08","q-socrates-09","q-socrates-10","q-socrates-11","q-socrates-12","q-socrates-13","q-socrates-14","q-socrates-15","q-socrates-16","q-socrates-17","q-socrates-18","q-socrates-19","q-socrates-20"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble head, Louvre. Image: Copy of Lysippos (?), Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"plato","name":"Plato","group":"Other authors","dates":"c. 428 – 348 BC","born":"c. 428 BC","died":"c. 348 BC","origin":"Athens","school":"Founder of the Academy","bio":"Student of Socrates and founder of the Academy in Athens. His dialogues, with Socrates as the main speaker, range over justice, love, knowledge and the soul, and have shaped Western thought ever since. The four cardinal virtues the Stoics adopted are set out in his Republic.","life":[{"h":"A young aristocrat","p":["Plato was born around 428 BC in Athens into one of the city's oldest aristocratic families. Ancient tradition says his real name was Aristocles and that \"Plato\", meaning \"broad\", was a nickname, perhaps for his shoulders, his forehead or his style. As a young man he may have wrestled and written poetry. Two of his relatives, Critias and Charmides, were among the Thirty, the oligarchs who ruled Athens by terror in 404 BC. Their violence turned him away from a political career."]},{"h":"Socrates and his death","p":["Plato became one of the young men around Socrates. Socrates' execution in 399 BC, when Plato was about twenty-eight, was the turning point of his life. It convinced him that no city could be just until philosophers became rulers or rulers became philosophers. Almost all of his writings give Socrates the leading role."]},{"h":"Travels and Sicily","p":["After Socrates' death Plato left Athens and travelled, to Megara, perhaps to Egypt, and to southern Italy, where he met Pythagorean thinkers such as Archytas of Tarentum. Around 388 BC he visited the court of Dionysius I, tyrant of Syracuse. According to a later story, the visit ended badly and Plato was sold into slavery on the island of Aegina, until a man named Anniceris bought his freedom.","Later in life he went to Syracuse twice more, in 367 and 361 BC, at the urging of his friend Dion, hoping to turn the young ruler Dionysius II into a philosopher-king. Both attempts failed, and Plato was lucky to get home."]},{"h":"The Academy","p":["Around 387 BC Plato founded a school in a grove sacred to the hero Akademos, just outside the walls of Athens. The Academy taught mathematics, astronomy, politics and philosophy, and it lasted in some form until Sulla's army sacked Athens in 86 BC. Aristotle studied there for twenty years. Zeno, the founder of Stoicism, attended lectures there under Polemo."]},{"h":"The dialogues","p":["Plato wrote philosophy as drama: conversations in which characters argue their way toward the truth. All of his roughly thirty-five dialogues have survived, among them the Apology, Crito and Phaedo on Socrates' trial and death, the Symposium on love, the Republic on justice and the ideal city, the Timaeus on the creation of the world, and the Laws, his last and longest work.","His central ideas include the theory of Forms, the belief that behind the changing things we see stand perfect, unchanging realities such as Justice and Beauty; the division of the soul into reason, spirit and appetite; and the four cardinal virtues of wisdom, courage, moderation and justice. The Stoics took over these four virtues as the core of their ethics. His allegory of the cave, in which prisoners mistake shadows for reality, is one of the most famous images in philosophy."]},{"h":"Death and legacy","p":["Plato died around 348 BC at about eighty and was buried in the Academy. The philosopher Alfred North Whitehead later wrote that the whole European philosophical tradition is a series of footnotes to Plato. The Stoics disagreed with him on many points, but they argued in a world he had shaped."]}],"quotes":["q-plato-01","q-plato-02","q-plato-03","q-plato-04","q-plato-05","q-plato-06","q-plato-07","q-plato-08","q-plato-09","q-plato-10","q-plato-11","q-plato-12","q-plato-13","q-plato-14","q-plato-15","q-plato-16","q-plato-17","q-plato-18","q-plato-19","q-plato-20","q-plato-21","q-plato-22","q-plato-23","q-plato-24","q-plato-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble head after Silanion, Capitoline Museums. Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY 2.5</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"aristotle","name":"Aristotle","group":"Other authors","dates":"384 – 322 BC","born":"384 BC","died":"322 BC","origin":"Stagira, Macedonia","school":"Founder of the Lyceum","bio":"Student of Plato, tutor of Alexander the Great and founder of the Lyceum. He wrote on nearly every field of knowledge, from logic and biology to ethics and politics. His Nicomachean Ethics describes virtue as a habit and the good life as human flourishing.","life":[{"h":"A doctor's son from Stagira","p":["Aristotle was born in 384 BC in Stagira, a small Greek town in the Chalcidice peninsula of northern Greece. His father Nicomachus was the court physician of King Amyntas III of Macedon, the grandfather of Alexander the Great. Both parents died when he was young, and he was raised by a guardian."]},{"h":"Twenty years in the Academy","p":["At seventeen Aristotle went to Athens and joined Plato's Academy. He stayed for twenty years, first as a student and then as a teacher, until Plato's death in 347 BC. Plato is said to have called him \"the mind of the school\". Aristotle admired his teacher, but came to reject the theory of Forms; in a famous line, he said that Plato was dear to him, but truth was dearer."]},{"h":"Travels and Alexander","p":["After leaving Athens, Aristotle spent several years in Assos, at the court of the ruler Hermias, whose niece Pythias he married, and then on the island of Lesbos. There, with his friend Theophrastus, he made close studies of marine animals, careful observations that made him the founder of biology as a science.","In 343 BC King Philip II of Macedon invited him to tutor his thirteen-year-old son Alexander. Aristotle taught him for several years at Mieza, a quiet sanctuary of the Nymphs. How much of Aristotle's thinking rubbed off on the future conqueror is debated, but Alexander is said to have slept with a copy of the Iliad annotated by his teacher."]},{"h":"The Lyceum","p":["In 335 BC Aristotle returned to Athens and founded his own school in the Lyceum, a gymnasium sacred to Apollo Lyceius. He liked to teach while walking along its covered walkways, and his followers became known as the Peripatetics, \"those who walk around\". The school collected books, gathered information on the constitutions of 158 Greek cities and organised research in almost every field."]},{"h":"Writings","p":["The works that survive are mostly his lecture notes, which is why they can be dense and dry. They cover an astonishing range: logic in the Organon, nature in the Physics, first principles in the Metaphysics, the soul in On the Soul, animals, politics, rhetoric and poetry. His Nicomachean Ethics argues that the goal of life is eudaimonia, flourishing, achieved through virtues of character built up by habit, each of them a mean between two extremes, such as courage between cowardice and recklessness.","Here the Stoics parted ways with him. Aristotle held that a good life also needs some external goods, such as health, friends and a measure of wealth. The Stoics insisted that virtue alone is enough."]},{"h":"Flight and death","p":["When Alexander died in 323 BC, anti-Macedonian feeling exploded in Athens and Aristotle was charged with impiety. Remembering Socrates, he left the city, saying he would not let the Athenians sin twice against philosophy. He died the following year, in 322 BC, at Chalcis on the island of Euboea, aged about sixty-two."]},{"h":"Legacy","p":["His works were edited in the first century BC by Andronicus of Rhodes, preserved by Arab and Byzantine scholars and rediscovered in Western Europe in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. Medieval writers simply called him \"the Philosopher\". For nearly two thousand years, logic, natural science and ethics were studied in the framework he built."]}],"quotes":["q-aristotle-01","q-aristotle-02","q-aristotle-03","q-aristotle-04","q-aristotle-05","q-aristotle-06","q-aristotle-07","q-aristotle-08","q-aristotle-09","q-aristotle-10","q-aristotle-11","q-aristotle-12","q-aristotle-13","q-aristotle-14","q-aristotle-15","q-aristotle-16","q-aristotle-17","q-aristotle-18","q-aristotle-19","q-aristotle-20","q-aristotle-21","q-aristotle-22","q-aristotle-23","q-aristotle-24","q-aristotle-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust after Lysippos, Palazzo Altemps, Rome. Image: After Lysippos, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"heraclitus","name":"Heraclitus","group":"Other authors","dates":"c. 535 – 475 BC","born":"c. 535 BC","died":"c. 475 BC","origin":"Ephesus, Ionia","school":"Pre-Socratic philosopher","bio":"Known as the Obscure for his riddling sayings. He taught that everything is in flux, held together by a hidden order he called the logos. The Stoics took over his logos and his fire as the basis of their physics.","life":[{"h":"An aristocrat of Ephesus","p":["Heraclitus lived around 535 to 475 BC in Ephesus, a rich Greek city on the coast of Ionia in modern Turkey. He came from an old aristocratic family that held the hereditary title of \"king\", a priestly office. According to Diogenes Laertius, Heraclitus gave the title up in favour of his brother.","He was proud, solitary and contemptuous of nearly everyone. He mocked Homer, Hesiod and Pythagoras, and said that learning many things does not teach understanding. When the people of Ephesus exiled his friend Hermodorus, Heraclitus declared that all the adult Ephesians should hang themselves and leave the city to the boys. One story says he withdrew from public life to play dice with children at the temple of Artemis, telling the citizens that this was better than taking part in their politics."]},{"h":"The obscure one","p":["Heraclitus wrote a single book, which he is said to have deposited in the great temple of Artemis at Ephesus. It is lost, but around 130 short fragments survive, quoted by later writers. They are written in compressed, riddling sentences, which earned him the nickname \"the Obscure\". Socrates is said to have remarked that what he understood of the book was excellent, and what he did not understand was probably excellent too, but it would take a diver from Delos to get to the bottom of it."]},{"h":"Logos, fire and change","p":["Heraclitus taught that there is a single law or reason, the logos, which governs everything, though most people do not notice it, living as if asleep. The world is like an ever-living fire, kindling and going out in measures. Everything is in constant change, and the world holds together through the tension of opposites, like a bow or a lyre. \"The road up and the road down are one and the same.\" Plato summed up his view with the famous image that you cannot step into the same river twice, because new waters are always flowing.","He also wrote that \"character is a person's fate\", a line Stoics would have agreed with."]},{"h":"His influence on the Stoics","p":["The Stoics took more from Heraclitus than from almost any other early thinker. Zeno and Cleanthes adopted his ideas of a rational logos running through the universe and of fire as its basic element. Cleanthes wrote a commentary on his book. Marcus Aurelius quotes him several times in the Meditations, including the warning not to act and speak like people asleep."]},{"h":"Death and image","p":["Diogenes Laertius tells that Heraclitus fell ill with dropsy, buried himself in cow dung in the hope that its warmth would draw out the fluid, and died. The story is probably invented, like many ancient philosophers' deaths.","Later tradition paired him with Democritus as \"the weeping philosopher\", who mourned the folly of the world, while Democritus laughed at it. The portrait on his coin comes from a painting of this weeping Heraclitus by the Dutch artist Johannes Moreelse, made around 1630. No portrait made in his lifetime survives."]}],"quotes":["q-heraclitus-01","q-heraclitus-02","q-heraclitus-03","q-heraclitus-04","q-heraclitus-05","q-heraclitus-06","q-heraclitus-07","q-heraclitus-08","q-heraclitus-09","q-heraclitus-10","q-heraclitus-11","q-heraclitus-12","q-heraclitus-13","q-heraclitus-14","q-heraclitus-15","q-heraclitus-16","q-heraclitus-17","q-heraclitus-18","q-heraclitus-19","q-heraclitus-20","q-heraclitus-21","q-heraclitus-22","q-heraclitus-23","q-heraclitus-24","q-heraclitus-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Painting by Johannes Moreelse, c. 1630. Image: Johannes Moreelse, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_Moreelse_-_Heraclitus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"miyamoto-musashi","name":"Miyamoto Musashi","group":"Other authors","dates":"c. 1584 – 1645","born":"c. 1584","died":"1645","origin":"Harima Province, Japan","school":"Swordsman and strategist","bio":"Japan's most famous swordsman, said to have been undefeated in more than sixty duels. In his last years he retired to a cave and wrote The Book of Five Rings on strategy and self-discipline. He was also an accomplished painter and calligrapher.","life":[{"h":"A young swordsman","p":["Miyamoto Musashi was born around 1584 in western Japan, probably in Harima or Mimasaka province. His childhood name was Bennosuke, and his family was connected to the Shinmen clan. He grew up at the end of more than a century of civil war in Japan, when skill with the sword could still decide a man's future.","By his own account in The Book of Five Rings, he fought his first duel at thirteen, defeating a samurai named Arima Kihei. At sixteen he beat a strong opponent from Tajima. He may have fought at the great Battle of Sekigahara in 1600, though which side he was on is unclear."]},{"h":"The Yoshioka and Ganryū-jima","p":["Around 1604 Musashi went to Kyoto and challenged the Yoshioka family, famous sword teachers to the Ashikaga shoguns. According to tradition he defeated the head of the school and his brother, and then a large group of Yoshioka students who tried to ambush him.","His most famous duel took place in 1612 on Funashima, a small island in the strait between Honshu and Kyushu, now known as Ganryū-jima. His opponent was Sasaki Kojirō, a celebrated swordsman known for an unusually long blade. As the story is usually told, Musashi arrived late, having carved a wooden sword from a boat's oar on the way, and killed Kojirō with a single blow. Many details were added by later storytellers, but the duel made Musashi a legend.","He wrote that he fought more than sixty duels by the age of thirty and never lost."]},{"h":"Two swords and the way of strategy","p":["Musashi developed a style of fighting with two swords at once, the long sword and the short one, which he called Niten Ichi-ryū, the \"two heavens as one\" school. But he insisted that strategy was more than swordsmanship. The same principles of timing, rhythm, observation and adapting to circumstances applied to a duel between two men and a battle between armies, and to any craft practised seriously."]},{"h":"Artist and teacher","p":["In later life Musashi became an accomplished artist. His ink paintings, such as Shrike on a Withered Branch and Hotei Watching a Cockfight, are treasured in Japan for their spare, decisive brushwork. He also produced calligraphy and wood carvings. He took part in the suppression of the Shimabara Rebellion in 1637 to 1638, where he was injured by a stone.","In 1640 the lord Hosokawa Tadatoshi invited him to Kumamoto on the island of Kyushu. In 1643 Musashi withdrew to a cave called Reigandō in the nearby mountains to write."]},{"h":"The Book of Five Rings","p":["The result was Go Rin no Sho, The Book of Five Rings, organised into five scrolls named Earth, Water, Fire, Wind and Void. It is a manual of strategy, but also a guide to discipline and clear perception. Its list of basic principles opens with \"Do not think dishonestly\" and \"The Way is in training\". A week before his death in 1645 he wrote the Dokkōdō, \"The Way of Walking Alone\", twenty-one short precepts for self-discipline, among them \"Accept everything just the way it is\" and \"Do not regret what you have done\".","Musashi has no historical link to the Stoics, but his focus on training, self-command and acceptance of what cannot be changed makes him a natural companion to them. The portrait on his coin is traditionally regarded as a self-portrait."]}],"quotes":["q-miyamoto-musashi-01","q-miyamoto-musashi-02","q-miyamoto-musashi-03","q-miyamoto-musashi-04","q-miyamoto-musashi-05","q-miyamoto-musashi-06","q-miyamoto-musashi-07","q-miyamoto-musashi-08","q-miyamoto-musashi-09","q-miyamoto-musashi-10","q-miyamoto-musashi-11","q-miyamoto-musashi-12","q-miyamoto-musashi-13","q-miyamoto-musashi-14","q-miyamoto-musashi-15","q-miyamoto-musashi-16","q-miyamoto-musashi-17","q-miyamoto-musashi-18","q-miyamoto-musashi-19","q-miyamoto-musashi-20","q-miyamoto-musashi-21","q-miyamoto-musashi-22","q-miyamoto-musashi-23","q-miyamoto-musashi-24","q-miyamoto-musashi-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Self-portrait, ink and colour on paper. Image: Miyamoto Musashi, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miyamoto_Musashi_Self-Portrait.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"sun-tzu","name":"Sun Tzu","group":"Other authors","dates":"6th – 5th century BC","born":"traditionally 544 BC","died":"traditionally 496 BC","origin":"State of Qi, China","school":"Military strategist","bio":"The general traditionally credited with The Art of War. Whether one man wrote it or it grew over generations is debated, but its lessons on preparation, deception and winning without fighting are still studied by soldiers and business leaders.","life":[{"h":"A general of Wu","p":["According to tradition, Sun Tzu, \"Master Sun\", was a man named Sun Wu who lived around 544 to 496 BC, at the end of China's Spring and Autumn period. He is said to have come from the state of Qi and to have served King Helü of Wu, a rising state in the lower Yangtze region, as a general."]},{"h":"The palace women","p":["The earliest account of his life comes from the historian Sima Qian, writing around 94 BC, several centuries later. In it, King Helü has read Sun Wu's thirteen chapters and asks whether he can test the theory on the palace women. Sun Wu divides 180 women into two companies, appoints the king's two favourite concubines as their captains, and explains the drill. When he beats the drum, the women only laugh. He explains again; they laugh again. Sun Wu declares that if orders are clear and not obeyed, the fault lies with the officers, and has the two captains executed, despite the king's protests. After that, the women carry out every command in perfect silence. The king, though grieving, made him a general.","Sima Qian adds that under Sun Wu's command Wu defeated the powerful state of Chu, captured its capital Ying in 506 BC, and made its name feared among the other states."]},{"h":"Man or legend?","p":["Historians debate whether Sun Wu existed as described. Older Chinese records of the period do not mention him, and the text of The Art of War, as we have it, seems to reflect warfare of the later Warring States period, the fifth and fourth centuries BC, with large conscript armies and professional generals. Many scholars think the book grew over time as a collection of teachings attached to a famous name.","In 1972 archaeologists opening a Han dynasty tomb at Yinqueshan in Shandong found bamboo strips from around 140 BC carrying a large part of the text, along with a separate work by Sun Bin, a later strategist said to be his descendant. The find showed that the book was already established in the form we know more than two thousand years ago."]},{"h":"The Art of War","p":["The Art of War is short, just thirteen chapters, written in brief, memorable lines. War, it says, is a matter of life and death for the state and must be studied with care. Its most famous ideas include:","that the best victory is to defeat the enemy without fighting at all; that all warfare is based on deception; that one should know the enemy and know oneself, and so not be in danger in a hundred battles; that speed, surprise and the shape of the terrain decide outcomes; and that a skilled commander first makes himself invincible and then waits for the enemy to become vulnerable."]},{"h":"Influence","p":["The book was studied by generals and statesmen across East Asia for centuries. It reached Europe in 1772 through a French translation by the Jesuit missionary Jean Joseph Marie Amiot. The British sinologist Lionel Giles published the classic English translation in 1910, which is now in the public domain. Today it is read by business leaders, athletes and coaches as much as by soldiers.","Like the Stoics, Sun Tzu teaches calm judgment under pressure and the importance of preparing what is in your control before acting. No portrait from his lifetime exists; the image on his coin is a modern statue of Sun Tzu in Yurihama, Japan."]}],"quotes":["q-sun-tzu-01","q-sun-tzu-02","q-sun-tzu-03","q-sun-tzu-04","q-sun-tzu-05","q-sun-tzu-06","q-sun-tzu-07","q-sun-tzu-08","q-sun-tzu-09","q-sun-tzu-10","q-sun-tzu-11","q-sun-tzu-12","q-sun-tzu-13","q-sun-tzu-14","q-sun-tzu-15","q-sun-tzu-16","q-sun-tzu-17","q-sun-tzu-18","q-sun-tzu-19","q-sun-tzu-20","q-sun-tzu-21","q-sun-tzu-22","q-sun-tzu-23","q-sun-tzu-24","q-sun-tzu-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Modern bronze statue, Yurihama, Japan. Image: 663highland, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY 2.5</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enchoen27n3200.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"niccolo-machiavelli","name":"Niccolò Machiavelli","group":"Other authors","dates":"1469 – 1527","born":"1469","died":"1527","origin":"Florence","school":"Political philosopher","bio":"Florentine diplomat and official who, after losing office and being tortured, wrote The Prince and the Discourses on Livy in exile. He described politics as it is rather than as it should be, which made his name a byword for cunning.","life":[{"h":"A Florentine of modest means","p":["Niccolò Machiavelli was born on 3 May 1469 in Florence, the son of Bernardo Machiavelli, a lawyer with a small income and a large love of books. Niccolò received a humanist education in Latin and read the Roman historians closely, above all Livy. He grew up in the Florence of Lorenzo de' Medici and saw the city's fortunes turn after Lorenzo's death in 1492."]},{"h":"Servant of the Republic","p":["In 1494 the Medici were driven out, and for four years the preacher Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florentine politics, until he was executed in 1498. Within weeks, the twenty-nine-year-old Machiavelli was appointed Second Chancellor of the Republic and secretary to the Ten of War, the committee in charge of foreign affairs and defence.","For fourteen years he worked as a diplomat and administrator. He was sent on missions to King Louis XII of France, to the Emperor Maximilian, to Pope Julius II and, most memorably, to Cesare Borgia in 1502 and 1503. He watched Borgia carve out a state in central Italy with speed, cunning and ruthlessness, and later used him as a model in The Prince. Machiavelli also persuaded Florence to raise its own militia instead of relying on mercenaries, and in 1509 that militia helped recapture Pisa."]},{"h":"Fall, torture and exile","p":["In 1512 Spanish troops restored the Medici to power and the Republic collapsed. Machiavelli lost his post. Early in 1513 his name was found on a list of suspected conspirators against the Medici. He was arrested and tortured with the strappado, being hoisted by his wrists tied behind his back and dropped. He admitted nothing. Weeks later, when Giovanni de' Medici became Pope Leo X, an amnesty set him free.","He retired to his small farm at Sant'Andrea in Percussina outside Florence. In a famous letter to his friend Francesco Vettori, dated 10 December 1513, he described his days: trapping birds, arguing with woodcutters, playing cards at the inn. In the evening, he wrote, he took off his muddy clothes, put on the robes of court, and entered the company of the ancient writers, who answered him kindly. In those hours he wrote The Prince."]},{"h":"The writings","p":["The Prince, finished in 1513 and published only in 1532, after his death, is a short handbook on how to gain and keep power. It argues that a ruler must be willing to act against conventional morality when the survival of the state demands it, and that it is safer to be feared than loved, if one cannot be both. His longer Discourses on Livy praise republics and the civic virtue of ancient Rome. He also wrote The Art of War, a comedy called The Mandrake that is still performed, and the Florentine Histories, commissioned by Cardinal Giulio de' Medici."]},{"h":"Fortune and skill","p":["A theme of The Prince speaks directly to Stoic readers. Machiavelli writes that fortune is the arbiter of perhaps half our actions, but leaves the other half to us. Fortune is like a flooding river: it cannot be stopped once it rises, but in calm times a wise person builds dykes and embankments. What matters is virtù, energy, skill and readiness to act."]},{"h":"Death","p":["In 1527 the Medici were driven out again and the Republic was restored, but the new government did not trust Machiavelli because he had worked for the Medici. He died a few weeks later, on 21 June 1527, and was buried in the church of Santa Croce in Florence. The portrait on his coin is by Santi di Tito, painted later in the sixteenth century."]}],"quotes":["q-niccolo-machiavelli-01","q-niccolo-machiavelli-02","q-niccolo-machiavelli-03","q-niccolo-machiavelli-04","q-niccolo-machiavelli-05","q-niccolo-machiavelli-06","q-niccolo-machiavelli-07","q-niccolo-machiavelli-08","q-niccolo-machiavelli-09","q-niccolo-machiavelli-10","q-niccolo-machiavelli-11","q-niccolo-machiavelli-12","q-niccolo-machiavelli-13","q-niccolo-machiavelli-14","q-niccolo-machiavelli-15","q-niccolo-machiavelli-16","q-niccolo-machiavelli-17","q-niccolo-machiavelli-18","q-niccolo-machiavelli-19","q-niccolo-machiavelli-20","q-niccolo-machiavelli-21","q-niccolo-machiavelli-22","q-niccolo-machiavelli-23","q-niccolo-machiavelli-24","q-niccolo-machiavelli-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Portrait by Santi di Tito. Image: Santi di Tito, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Niccol%C3%B2_Machiavelli_by_Santi_di_Tito.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"epicurus","name":"Epicurus","group":"Other authors","dates":"341 – 270 BC","born":"341 BC","died":"270 BC","origin":"Samos","school":"Founder of the Garden","bio":"Founder of the Garden in Athens, the great rival school of the Stoa. He taught that pleasure, understood as freedom from pain and fear, is the goal of life, and that it is best found in simple living and friendship. Seneca quotes him often and with respect.","life":[{"h":"Born on Samos","p":["Epicurus was born in 341 BC on the island of Samos, where his Athenian parents had settled as colonists. His father Neocles was a schoolteacher. According to one story, Epicurus turned to philosophy at fourteen, when his teacher could not explain to him what the \"chaos\" was from which, according to Hesiod, everything came.","He studied with Nausiphanes, a follower of Democritus' atomic theory, and at eighteen went to Athens for his military service. When the Athenian colonists were expelled from Samos in 322 BC, his family moved to Colophon on the coast of Asia Minor, and Epicurus joined them."]},{"h":"The Garden","p":["Epicurus began teaching in Mytilene on Lesbos and in Lampsacus on the Hellespont. Around 307 BC he moved to Athens and bought a house with a garden outside the city walls, where he lived and taught for the rest of his life. The school became known simply as the Garden.","Unusually for the time, the Garden welcomed women and slaves. Among its members were the courtesan and philosopher Leontion and Themista of Lampsacus, as well as Epicurus' slave Mys. The community lived simply and valued friendship above almost everything else. Over the gate, a later writer says, was the invitation: \"Stranger, here you will do well to stay; here the highest good is pleasure.\""]},{"h":"Pleasure, rightly understood","p":["Epicurus taught that pleasure is the goal of life, which his enemies twisted into a doctrine of indulgence. In fact, he meant something close to the opposite. The highest pleasure, he argued, is the absence of pain in the body and of disturbance in the mind, ataraxia. Most of our troubles come from desires that are neither natural nor necessary, such as fame, luxury and power. He lived mostly on bread and water and once wrote to a friend: \"Send me a little pot of cheese, so that I can have a feast whenever I like.\"","His teaching was summed up later as the \"four-part cure\": the gods are not to be feared; death is nothing to fear; what is good is easy to get; what is terrible is easy to endure."]},{"h":"Atoms and the fear of death","p":["Following Democritus, Epicurus held that everything is made of atoms moving in empty space. The soul too is made of atoms and scatters at death. \"Death is nothing to us,\" he wrote, \"since when we exist, death is not present, and when death is present, we no longer exist.\" The gods exist, but live in perfect calm and take no interest in human affairs."]},{"h":"Writings","p":["Epicurus is said to have written about three hundred works. Almost all are lost. Diogenes Laertius preserved three of his letters, to Herodotus, Pythocles and Menoeceus, and his Principal Doctrines, forty key sayings. Another collection, the Vatican Sayings, was found in a manuscript in 1888. His Roman follower Lucretius set out his philosophy in the great Latin poem On the Nature of Things."]},{"h":"Death","p":["Epicurus died in 270 BC after a long, painful illness from kidney stones. In his last letter he wrote that on this happy day, which was also the last of his life, his pain was extreme, but against it he set the joy of remembering his conversations with his friends."]},{"h":"Rival and teacher of the Stoics","p":["The Stoics and Epicureans were the great rival schools of the Hellenistic world: one built life on virtue, the other on pleasure. Yet Seneca quotes Epicurus in many of his early letters to Lucilius, saying he is happy to borrow good ideas from the enemy camp, and Marcus Aurelius also cites him with respect."]}],"quotes":["q-epicurus-01","q-epicurus-02","q-epicurus-03","q-epicurus-04","q-epicurus-05","q-epicurus-06","q-epicurus-07","q-epicurus-08","q-epicurus-09","q-epicurus-10","q-epicurus-11","q-epicurus-12","q-epicurus-13","q-epicurus-14","q-epicurus-15","q-epicurus-16","q-epicurus-17","q-epicurus-18","q-epicurus-19","q-epicurus-20","q-epicurus-21","q-epicurus-22","q-epicurus-23","q-epicurus-24","q-epicurus-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust, Capitoline Museums. Image: Jamie Heath, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Epicurus_cropped_noBG.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"plutarch","name":"Plutarch","group":"Other authors","dates":"c. AD 46 – 120","born":"c. AD 46","died":"after AD 119","origin":"Chaeronea, Boeotia","school":"Platonist, priest at Delphi","bio":"Greek biographer, essayist and priest at Delphi. His Parallel Lives pairs famous Greeks and Romans to draw out their character, and his Moralia collects essays on how to live. Much of what we know about Cato the Younger comes from him.","life":[{"h":"A man from Chaeronea","p":["Plutarch was born around AD 46 in Chaeronea, a small town in Boeotia in central Greece, where Philip II of Macedon had once defeated the Athenians and Thebans. His family was prosperous and long established there. Plutarch loved his hometown and chose to live there most of his life, joking that he stayed so that it would not become smaller by one more citizen."]},{"h":"Education and travels","p":["Around AD 66 he studied in Athens with the Platonist philosopher Ammonius, and Platonism remained his own philosophy for life. He travelled to Egypt and several times to Rome, where he lectured and made influential friends. Through one of them, the consular Lucius Mestrius Florus, he received Roman citizenship and the name Lucius Mestrius Plutarchus.","He married Timoxena, and they had at least four sons and a daughter. When their two-year-old daughter, also called Timoxena, died while Plutarch was away, he wrote his wife a letter of consolation that survives. It is one of the most moving documents of ancient family life, gentle and practical, reminding her of the joy the child brought them and urging her not to let grief cancel it."]},{"h":"Priest at Delphi","p":["For roughly the last thirty years of his life Plutarch served as one of the two priests of Apollo at Delphi, the most famous oracle of the Greek world. He took the duty seriously, helped restore the sanctuary at a time when its importance was fading, and wrote several dialogues about the oracle and its meaning. The bust shown on his coin was found at Delphi and is traditionally identified as Plutarch, though that identification is not certain."]},{"h":"The Parallel Lives","p":["Plutarch's most famous work is the Parallel Lives, biographies of famous Greeks and Romans arranged in pairs, such as Alexander and Julius Caesar, or Demosthenes and Cicero. Twenty-three pairs survive, along with four single lives. His aim was not history for its own sake but character: he said that a small act, a word or a joke often reveals more about a person than battles in which thousands die. His Life of Cato the Younger is our main source for Cato's death at Utica."]},{"h":"The Moralia","p":["Plutarch also wrote a large collection of essays and dialogues now called the Moralia, around seventy-eight works. They include On Tranquility of Mind, On the Control of Anger, How to Tell a Flatterer from a Friend, On Listening, and On Moral Progress. Several essays attack the Stoics and Epicureans on points of doctrine. Even so, his practical advice on anger, calm and self-improvement often sounds very close to Stoic teaching."]},{"h":"Legacy","p":["Plutarch died some time after AD 119. In 1579 Thomas North published an English translation of the Lives, based on a French version by Jacques Amyot. Shakespeare used North's Plutarch as the source for Julius Caesar, Antony and Cleopatra and Coriolanus, sometimes copying whole passages into verse. Montaigne called Plutarch his favourite author, and the Lives were read closely by the American founders and many later statesmen."]}],"quotes":["q-plutarch-01","q-plutarch-02","q-plutarch-03","q-plutarch-04","q-plutarch-05","q-plutarch-06","q-plutarch-07","q-plutarch-08","q-plutarch-09","q-plutarch-10","q-plutarch-11","q-plutarch-12","q-plutarch-13","q-plutarch-14","q-plutarch-15","q-plutarch-16","q-plutarch-17","q-plutarch-18","q-plutarch-19","q-plutarch-20","q-plutarch-21","q-plutarch-22","q-plutarch-23","q-plutarch-24"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Marble bust believed to show Plutarch, Archaeological Museum of Delphi. Image: Original uploader was Odysses, Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_a_philosopher_-_Archaeological_Museum_of_Delphi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"confucius","name":"Confucius","group":"Other authors","dates":"551 – 479 BC","born":"551 BC","died":"479 BC","origin":"Lu, China","school":"Founder of Confucianism","bio":"Teacher and minor official in the state of Lu whose sayings, collected by his students as the Analects, became the foundation of Chinese ethics. He taught self-cultivation, respect within relationships and governing by virtuous example.","life":[{"h":"A poor boy in Lu","p":["Confucius, in Chinese Kongzi or Kong Fuzi, \"Master Kong\", was born in 551 BC near Qufu in the small state of Lu, in today's Shandong province. His personal name was Kong Qiu. His father, Shuliang He, was an old soldier who died when Confucius was about three, and the boy grew up in poverty with his mother, Yan Zhengzai.","He later said that because he was poor and of humble station in his youth, he became skilled in many menial things. As a young man he worked as a keeper of granaries and of livestock for a noble family. He married at nineteen and had a son and a daughter. Meanwhile he taught himself the rites, music, history and poetry of the early Zhou dynasty, which he regarded as a golden age of good order."]},{"h":"In government","p":["Confucius began to take students and became known for his learning. According to tradition, around the age of fifty he entered the service of the Duke of Lu and rose to high office, possibly as Minister of Crime. At a diplomatic meeting with the more powerful state of Qi at Jiagu in 500 BC, his insistence on correct ritual is said to have shamed Qi into returning territory it had taken from Lu.","His career did not last. Traditional accounts say that Qi, worried by Lu's growing strength, sent the duke a gift of dancing girls and fine horses, and the duke neglected government to enjoy them. Around 497 BC Confucius left Lu in disappointment."]},{"h":"Fourteen years of wandering","p":["For the next fourteen years Confucius travelled from state to state with a small band of disciples, looking for a ruler who would put his ideas into practice. None did. He was threatened, mistaken for an enemy and at one point nearly starved when he and his followers were trapped between the states of Chen and Cai. When a disciple asked whether a gentleman ever finds himself in such need, Confucius answered that a gentleman holds firm in hardship, while a petty person in hardship loses all restraint."]},{"h":"Teacher of the ages","p":["Around 484 BC, aged nearly seventy, Confucius returned to Lu. He spent his last years teaching and, according to tradition, editing the classic books of poetry, history and ritual. He is said to have had three thousand students, of whom seventy-two mastered his teaching. He outlived his son and his favourite disciple, Yan Hui, whose death left him crying out that heaven had destroyed him. Confucius died in 479 BC."]},{"h":"The Analects","p":["Confucius wrote no book of his own ideas. His sayings and conversations were collected by his disciples and their students in the Analects. They describe the ideal of the junzi, the exemplary person, who cultivates ren, humaneness or goodness, and li, ritual propriety, and who leads by moral example rather than force. Asked for a single word to guide one's whole life, he offered \"reciprocity\" and explained: \"Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself.\"","He summed up his own life in a famous passage: at fifteen he set his heart on learning, at thirty he stood firm, at forty he had no doubts, at fifty he knew the will of heaven, at sixty his ear was attuned, and at seventy he could follow his heart's desire without overstepping what was right."]},{"h":"Legacy","p":["Under the Han dynasty in the second century BC, his teachings became the official philosophy of the Chinese state, and they shaped education and government in China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam for two thousand years. His stress on self-cultivation, duty and steady character has much in common with Stoic ethics. The image on his coin is based on a portrait attributed to the Tang painter Wu Daozi."]}],"quotes":["q-confucius-01","q-confucius-02","q-confucius-03","q-confucius-04","q-confucius-05","q-confucius-06","q-confucius-07","q-confucius-08","q-confucius-09","q-confucius-10","q-confucius-11","q-confucius-12","q-confucius-13","q-confucius-14","q-confucius-15","q-confucius-16","q-confucius-17","q-confucius-18","q-confucius-19","q-confucius-20","q-confucius-21","q-confucius-22","q-confucius-23","q-confucius-24","q-confucius-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Stone rubbing after Wu Daozi, Tang dynasty. Image: Wu Daozi, 685-758, Tang Dynasty., Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius_Tang_Dynasty,_black_print.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true},{"slug":"friedrich-nietzsche","name":"Friedrich Nietzsche","group":"Other authors","dates":"1844 – 1900","born":"1844","died":"1900","origin":"Röcken, Prussia","school":"Philosopher","bio":"German philologist turned philosopher. In books such as Thus Spoke Zarathustra and Beyond Good and Evil he attacked conventional morality and called on people to create their own values. His idea of amor fati, loving one's fate, is close to the Stoic acceptance of what happens.","life":[{"h":"A pastor's son","p":["Friedrich Wilhelm Nietzsche was born on 15 October 1844 in Röcken, a village in Prussian Saxony. His father, a Lutheran pastor, died in 1849, when Friedrich was four, and the family moved to Naumburg, where he grew up in a household of women: his mother, his sister Elisabeth, his grandmother and two aunts.","He was a brilliant student at Schulpforta, one of the best boarding schools in Germany, and went on to study theology and then classical philology at Bonn and Leipzig."]},{"h":"Professor at twenty-four","p":["His teacher Friedrich Ritschl thought so highly of him that in 1869, at only twenty-four and before he had even completed his doctorate, Nietzsche was appointed professor of classical philology at the University of Basel in Switzerland. During the Franco-Prussian War of 1870 he volunteered as a medical orderly. He caught dysentery and diphtheria while caring for wounded soldiers, and his health never fully recovered.","In these years he became a close friend of the composer Richard Wagner. His first book, The Birth of Tragedy (1872), celebrated Wagner's music as a rebirth of the Greek tragic spirit. The friendship later collapsed, and Nietzsche became one of Wagner's sharpest critics."]},{"h":"The wanderer","p":["Nietzsche suffered from severe migraines, eye problems and stomach illness. In 1879 he resigned his professorship with a small pension. For the next ten years he lived a wandering life in rented rooms, spending summers in Sils Maria in the Swiss Alps and winters in Genoa, Nice and Turin. He walked for hours each day and wrote in short bursts, often in aphorisms.","In this decade he wrote almost all of his major works: Human, All Too Human; The Gay Science, in which a madman announces that \"God is dead\"; Thus Spoke Zarathustra; Beyond Good and Evil; On the Genealogy of Morality; Twilight of the Idols, which contains the line \"What does not kill me makes me stronger\"; and Ecce Homo, his strange, proud autobiography. Few people read his books during his lifetime."]},{"h":"Nietzsche and the Stoics","p":["Nietzsche admired and mocked the Stoics in equal measure. In Beyond Good and Evil he made fun of the Stoic ideal of \"living according to nature\", arguing that nature is wasteful and indifferent, and that the Stoics were really imposing their own ideals on it. Yet his own central formula, amor fati, love of fate, sounds deeply Stoic: \"I want to learn more and more to see as beautiful what is necessary in things.\" He wanted not just to accept what happens but to love it, and to want nothing to be different, not forward, not backward, not in all eternity."]},{"h":"Collapse and afterlife","p":["In January 1889, in Turin, Nietzsche suffered a complete mental breakdown. A famous but unverified story says it began when he threw his arms around a horse being beaten in the street. He never recovered. He was cared for first by his mother and then by his sister Elisabeth, and died in Weimar on 25 August 1900.","Elisabeth, an ardent nationalist and antisemite, took control of his papers, edited them to suit her own views and later welcomed the Nazis, who used a distorted version of his work for their purposes. After the Second World War, scholars restored the original texts. Today Nietzsche is recognised as one of the most influential thinkers of the modern age. The photograph on his coin was taken in Naumburg in 1882 by Gustav Schultze."]}],"quotes":["q-friedrich-nietzsche-01","q-friedrich-nietzsche-02","q-friedrich-nietzsche-03","q-friedrich-nietzsche-04","q-friedrich-nietzsche-05","q-friedrich-nietzsche-06","q-friedrich-nietzsche-07","q-friedrich-nietzsche-08","q-friedrich-nietzsche-09","q-friedrich-nietzsche-10","q-friedrich-nietzsche-11","q-friedrich-nietzsche-12","q-friedrich-nietzsche-13","q-friedrich-nietzsche-14","q-friedrich-nietzsche-15","q-friedrich-nietzsche-16","q-friedrich-nietzsche-17","q-friedrich-nietzsche-18","q-friedrich-nietzsche-19","q-friedrich-nietzsche-20","q-friedrich-nietzsche-21","q-friedrich-nietzsche-22","q-friedrich-nietzsche-23","q-friedrich-nietzsche-24","q-friedrich-nietzsche-25"],"credit":"Coin portrait: Photograph by Gustav Schultze, 1882. Image: Gustav-Adolf Schultze (d. 1897), Public domain, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nietzsche1882.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.","portrait":true}];
DT.QUOTES = [{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.4","q":["I made a prosperous voyage","when I suffered shipwreck."],"c":["Zeno said this about the accident that changed his life. A merchant carrying a cargo of purple dye, he was shipwrecked near Athens, wandered into a bookshop and discovered philosophy. Without the wreck there would have been no Zeno the philosopher, and no Stoa."],"e":{"when":"c. 312 BC · Piraeus and Athens","title":"The shipwreck","text":"Zeno's ship, carrying purple dye from Phoenicia, was wrecked near the port of Athens. In a bookshop he heard the second book of Xenophon's memoirs of Socrates read aloud and asked where such men could be found. The bookseller pointed at Crates the Cynic, who happened to be passing: \"Follow that man.\" Zeno did."},"p":"Think of a setback that later turned out to be a turning point. Ask whether today's setback might become one too.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-01","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.23","q":["We have two ears and one mouth,","so that we may listen more and speak less."],"c":["Zeno was known for his quiet manner and for short, sharp replies. He disliked long speeches and preferred to listen. This line, one of his best known, reminds us that we learn far more by listening than by talking."],"e":null,"p":"In your next conversation, listen twice as much as you speak.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-02","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.26","q":["Well-being is achieved little by little,","and yet it is no little thing."],"c":["Zeno's saying contains a play on words in Greek: \"little by little\" and \"no little thing\". Happiness is not a single event or sudden breakthrough. It is built slowly through many small good choices, each one modest, but together great."],"e":null,"p":"Make one small good choice today and count it as part of the larger work.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-03","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.87","q":["The goal is to live in agreement with nature,","which is to live according to virtue, for nature leads us towards virtue."],"c":["Zeno was the first to give this definition of the Stoic goal, in his book On Human Nature. To live \"in agreement\" means a life that is consistent, not divided against itself. Human nature is rational, so a life in agreement with nature is one guided by reason and virtue."],"e":null,"p":"Where today is your life out of agreement with itself, saying one thing and doing another? Bring one small part back into line.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-04","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.23","q":["A friend is another self."],"c":["Asked what a friend is, Zeno gave this short answer. A true friend shares your aims and values so closely that what is good for them is good for you, and your concern for them becomes as natural as concern for yourself."],"e":null,"p":"Do something for a friend today with the same care you would give yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-05","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.21","q":["Your ears have slid down into your tongue."],"c":["Zeno said this to a young man who talked too much. It is a typically sharp, funny Zeno reply: someone who speaks constantly has stopped using their ears. Zeno's school was a place to listen and think, not to show off."],"e":null,"p":"Notice when you are talking just to fill silence today. Let the silence stay.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-06","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.22","q":["Nothing is more unbecoming than arrogance,","especially in the young."],"c":["Zeno often corrected his young students' manners. He thought arrogance was especially harmful in youth, when there is most to learn and the least experience to justify pride."],"e":null,"p":"Ask someone younger or less experienced than you for their view today, and learn from it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-07","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.23","q":["\"It was fated that I should steal.\"","\"Yes, and that you should be beaten for it.\""],"c":["Zeno was beating a slave who had stolen from him. The slave, who had heard the Stoic doctrine of fate, tried to use it as an excuse. Zeno's reply shows the Stoic view: fate does not cancel responsibility. Actions have consequences, and those too are part of the chain."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one excuse you use today: \"That's just how I am.\" Ask what consequence it brings.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-08","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.26","q":["It is better to trip with the feet","than with the tongue."],"c":["A fall with the feet hurts for a moment. A slip of the tongue, an insult, a lie or a careless remark, can do harm that lasts years. Zeno valued words chosen with care."],"e":null,"p":"Before saying something sharp today, pause. Would it be better left unsaid?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-09","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.24","q":["Tell the king that there is someone in Athens","who knows how to keep silent."],"c":["At a dinner given for ambassadors of a king, perhaps Ptolemy, Zeno said nothing all evening. As they left, the envoys asked what they should report about him to their master. This was his answer. In a culture that admired speeches, Zeno saw silence as a sign of self-command."],"e":{"when":"c. 270 BC · Athens and Pella","title":"Zeno and King Antigonus","text":"King Antigonus II Gonatas of Macedon admired Zeno, attended his lectures when in Athens and invited him to court. Zeno, by then over eighty, declined because of his age and sent two students, Persaeus and Philonides, in his place. When Zeno died, Antigonus is said to have remarked that he had lost the audience of his life."},"p":"In a meeting or conversation today, choose not to speak once when you would normally jump in.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-10","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Stobaeus, Anthology 2.7.6","q":["Happiness is a good flow of life."],"c":["Zeno defined happiness, eudaimonia, as euroia biou, \"a smooth flow of life\". The image is of a river running steadily, without blockage or turbulence. A life is happy when it runs consistently, guided by reason, and not when it is filled with exciting highs."],"e":null,"p":"Look for one place where your day keeps getting stuck. Clear it so the day can flow.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-11","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 83.9","q":["No one entrusts a secret to a drunkard; but one entrusts a secret to a good person.","So the good person will not be a drunkard."],"c":["Zeno liked to make his points through short logical arguments. Seneca quotes this one about drunkenness and then criticises it as too clever. But it shows Zeno's method: think clearly, step by step, and draw conclusions about how to live."],"e":null,"p":"Reason through one decision today in steps, as Zeno would: what follows from what?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-12","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Cicero, Orator 113","q":["Logic is like a closed fist;","rhetoric is like an open hand."],"c":["Zeno demonstrated this with gestures. Clenching his fist, he called it dialectic: tight, compact argument. Opening his hand, he called it rhetoric: the same reason, spread out and made persuasive. Both have their place; good thinking needs both."],"e":null,"p":"Today, try to state one argument tightly, in a single sentence, and then explain it generously.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-13","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.24","q":["Crates, the right way to take hold of philosophers is by the ears.","Persuade me, then, and drag me by them. If you use force, my body will be with you, but my mind with Stilpo."],"c":["Crates the Cynic tried to pull Zeno away from the lectures of Stilpo, another teacher, by grabbing his cloak. This was Zeno's reply. Minds can only be won by argument, not force. It is a principle for teachers and leaders everywhere."],"e":null,"p":"If you want to change someone's mind today, use reasons, not pressure.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-14","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Plutarch, On the Fortune of Alexander 329A","q":["We should not live divided into cities and peoples, each with its own laws,","but think of all human beings as fellow citizens, with one life and one order, like a herd grazing together under a common law."],"c":["Plutarch summarises this vision from Zeno's lost Republic. It is one of the first statements of the idea of a world community bound by reason rather than by borders. Later Stoics, from Seneca to Marcus Aurelius, built on it with their idea of the citizen of the world."],"e":null,"p":"Treat one stranger today as if you belonged to the same city.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-zeno-of-citium-15","a":"zeno-of-citium"},{"ref":"Epictetus, Handbook 53","q":["Lead me, Zeus, and you, Destiny, wherever you have assigned me.","I will follow without hesitation. And if I become bad and unwilling, I will follow all the same."],"c":["These lines by Cleanthes became one of the best-known prayers of the ancient world. Epictetus ended his Handbook with them, and Seneca translated them into Latin. They express the Stoic attitude to fate: we can follow willingly, or be dragged, but we will go either way."],"e":null,"p":"Name one thing today that you cannot change. Say to yourself: \"I will follow without hesitation.\"","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-01","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Hymn to Zeus, lines 1–5","q":["Most glorious of the immortals, called by many names, ever all-powerful,","Zeus, origin of nature, steering all things with law: hail to you."],"c":["Cleanthes' Hymn to Zeus is the longest piece of early Stoic writing to survive complete. It addresses Zeus not as a mythical figure but as the reason that orders the universe. For Cleanthes, praising God and understanding the rational order of nature were the same activity."],"e":null,"p":"Take a moment today to notice something ordered and beautiful in nature, and let it put your worries in proportion.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-02","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Hymn to Zeus, lines 15–17","q":["Nothing happens on earth without you, nor in the heavens, nor in the sea,","except what bad people do in their folly."],"c":["The hymn faces the problem of evil directly. Cleanthes believed that the universe is ordered by reason, but also that people can act against it through ignorance. He continues: \"But you know how to make the crooked straight and to order the disorderly.\" Even wrongdoing is woven back into the whole."],"e":null,"p":"When you see something go wrong today, ask: what could be made straight from this?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-03","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Hymn to Zeus, lines 22–25","q":["Unhappy people, always longing for the possession of good things,","they neither see nor hear God's universal law. If they obeyed it with understanding, they would have a good life."],"c":["Cleanthes describes the people who chase reputation, money or pleasure and never find peace. The good life is available, but they miss it because they are looking in the wrong direction. Happiness comes from understanding and following the order of things, not from possessing more."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one thing you are chasing today. Ask whether it is the good itself, or something you think will bring it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-04","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.168","q":["I don't only draw water. I dig the ground, I water gardens,","and I do everything for the sake of philosophy."],"c":["Cleanthes worked through the nights to pay for his studies. He reportedly said this to people who wondered how he made a living. Philosophy, for him, was worth any amount of hard, humble work."],"e":{"when":"c. 280 BC · Athens","title":"Before the Areopagus","text":"Because Cleanthes was always at Zeno's lectures but seemed to have no income, he was summoned before the Areopagus, the city's ancient council, to explain how he lived. He brought the gardener for whom he drew water at night and the woman for whom he kneaded dough as witnesses. The councillors were so impressed that they voted him a gift of ten minae; Zeno would not let him accept it."},"p":"Do one humble task today well and without complaint, as part of something bigger you care about.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-05","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.171","q":["To an old man","who has grey hair but no sense."],"c":["Someone once overheard Cleanthes scolding himself and asked who he was talking to. This was his answer. It is a funny line, but it reflects a serious practice: Stoics examined and corrected themselves constantly, even into old age."],"e":null,"p":"Have an honest word with yourself today about one thing you know you should do better.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-06","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.171","q":["That is why I make so few mistakes."],"c":["When someone mocked Cleanthes for being timid and overly cautious, he answered in this way. What others saw as weakness, he saw as a source of good judgment. Caution, used well, is not cowardice but care."],"e":null,"p":"Before one decision today, take an extra moment of caution. See if it saves you a mistake.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-07","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.170","q":["Yes, I am a donkey,","but I alone am strong enough to carry Zeno's load."],"c":["Fellow students called Cleanthes \"the donkey\" because he was slow to learn. He turned the insult around. He was not quick, but he was strong and patient, and his steady devotion made him Zeno's successor as head of the school."],"e":null,"p":"If someone mocks you today, look for the strength hidden in what they call weakness.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-08","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.37","q":["I am like a hard writing tablet:","difficult to write on, but I keep what is written."],"c":["Cleanthes compared himself to a stiff wax tablet. He learned slowly, but what he learned stayed with him. Quick minds often forget quickly; patient learners build lasting understanding."],"e":null,"p":"Spend extra time today truly understanding one thing, rather than skimming many.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-09","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 108.10","q":["As breath sounds clearer when forced through a narrow trumpet,","so the strict form of verse makes our thought sharper."],"c":["Seneca reports this image from Cleanthes, who wrote some of his teaching in poetry. Compressed into a few words, an idea strikes harder and stays longer. That is why Stoics loved short sayings and maxims."],"e":null,"p":"Write one principle you live by in no more than ten words.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-10","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Stobaeus, Anthology 2.7.6","q":["The goal is to live in agreement with nature."],"c":["Zeno said the goal of life was \"to live in agreement\". Cleanthes, according to Stobaeus, added the words \"with nature\", making explicit what that agreement is with: the rational order of the universe and our own rational nature. It became the classic Stoic formula."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself today: is this action in agreement with what I really am, a reasoning and social being?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-11","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.176","q":["I have already come so far along the road."],"c":["In old age Cleanthes had a sore on his gum, and his doctors told him to fast for two days. When the sore healed and they told him to eat again, he refused with these words, and continued to fast until he died. He chose his end as calmly as he had lived."],"e":null,"p":"Look back at how far you have come on some long road. Let it give you calm for what remains.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cleanthes-12","a":"cleanthes"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.179","q":["Just give me the doctrines.","I will find the proofs myself."],"c":["Chrysippus said this to his teacher Cleanthes. He was confident in his ability to argue, and later he supplied proofs for almost the whole of Stoic teaching. It became a famous line about bold self-confidence, and about the difference between knowing what is true and being able to show it."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one belief you hold. Can you give three good reasons for it? Find them today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-01","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.182","q":["If I had paid attention to the many,","I would not have become a philosopher."],"c":["Someone criticised Chrysippus for not going to the crowded lectures of the popular teacher Aristo. This was his answer. Following the crowd is easy; finding your own way requires some independence from what everyone else is doing."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one thing you do today only because everyone does it. Decide for yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-02","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.87","q":["To live virtuously is the same as to live","in accordance with experience of what happens by nature."],"c":["Chrysippus refined the Stoic definition of the goal of life. We are parts of the whole universe, he argued, so our nature is part of its nature. Living well means understanding how things actually work and fitting our lives to that understanding."],"e":null,"p":"Learn one thing today about how something actually works: in nature, in people, in your own habits.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-03","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Fate 42–43","q":["Just as a person who pushes a cylinder gives it the beginning of motion, but not the ability to roll,","so an impression strikes us from outside, but our assent is in our own power."],"c":["Cicero reports Chrysippus' famous image. Push a cylinder and it rolls; push a cone and it spins. The push starts things, but the object's own shape decides how it moves. Events give us impressions; our character decides how we respond. That is where our responsibility lies."],"e":null,"p":"When something pushes you today, notice that the push is not the response. How will your shape roll with it?","s":"Image reported by Cicero, On Fate 42–43; wording here is a summary of Cicero's account.","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-04","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Galen, On the Doctrines of Hippocrates and Plato 4.2","q":["When someone walks as they choose, they can stop when they want.","But when someone runs, the motion carries them beyond their choice."],"c":["Chrysippus used this image to explain the passions. A healthy impulse is like walking: we can stop. An excessive impulse, like anger or panic, is like running downhill: once started, it carries us further than we meant. That is why we should catch it early."],"e":null,"p":"Catch one strong emotion early today, while you are still walking, and slow down.","s":"Image reported by Galen, On the Doctrines of Hippocrates and Plato 4.2.","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-05","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 9.14","q":["The wise person lacks nothing, yet needs many things.","The fool needs nothing, for he does not know how to use anything, yet he lacks everything."],"c":["Seneca quotes this paradox of Chrysippus. The wise use friends, tools and resources well, but they are not destroyed by their loss. The foolish could have everything and still feel empty."],"e":null,"p":"Use something well today, a skill, a friendship, a resource, without treating it as something you could not live without.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-06","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Duties 3.42","q":["Someone running a race should strive with all their strength to win,","but must not trip a rival or push them aside with their hand."],"c":["Cicero quotes this saying of Chrysippus on fair competition. It is right to seek your own advantage, Chrysippus argues, but not at the cost of wronging others. Ambition is allowed; cheating is not."],"e":null,"p":"Compete hard today at something, but cleanly.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-07","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Seneca, On Benefits 2.17.3","q":["Giving and receiving a kindness is like a game of catch:","the ball must be thrown so that it can be caught, and caught so that it can be thrown back."],"c":["Seneca reports Chrysippus' image of the ball game. A good gift is given in a way the other person can accept, and a good recipient receives it in a way that lets the exchange continue. Generosity is a skill shared by both sides."],"e":null,"p":"Give something today in a way that is easy to receive. And receive something today with real gratitude.","s":"Image reported by Seneca, On Benefits 2.17.3; wording here is a summary of Seneca's account.","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-08","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 7.1","q":["Nature did not create disease on purpose,","but it came as a by-product of the good things nature made."],"c":["Gellius reports Chrysippus' answer to the question of why there is suffering in a world governed by reason. The human skull, Chrysippus said, had to be made of thin bones for the sake of the brain, and therefore it can be broken. Some evils are the necessary side effects of real goods."],"e":null,"p":"Notice a frustration today that is the flip side of something good in your life. See them as one.","s":"View reported by Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 7.1; wording here is a summary.","t":"","id":"q-chrysippus-09","a":"chrysippus"},{"ref":"Seneca, On Anger 3.38","q":["I am not angry.","But I am not sure that I ought not to be."],"c":["Seneca tells how Diogenes, while lecturing on anger, was spat on in the face by an insolent young man. Diogenes wiped his face, stayed calm and gave this mild, ironic answer. He refused to let the insult take over, and still made his point."],"e":{"when":"155 BC · Rome","title":"The philosophers' embassy","text":"Athens sent three philosophers to the Roman Senate to appeal a heavy fine: Carneades of the Academy, Critolaus the Peripatetic and Diogenes for the Stoics. While they waited, they lectured to crowds of young Romans. Carneades argued for justice one day and against it the next, and Cato the Elder urged the Senate to send them home quickly. Ancient writers praised Diogenes for his sober, restrained manner."},"p":"If someone provokes you today, answer calmly, and if needed, with a little humour.","s":"Embassy: Cicero, On the Orator 2.155; Plutarch, Cato the Elder 22.","t":"","id":"q-diogenes-of-babylon-01","a":"diogenes-of-babylon"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Duties 3.51","q":["I have brought my grain here; I have offered it for sale; I sell it no dearer than others.","Where is the wrong in that, if I tell no lie?"],"c":["This is how Cicero has Diogenes answer the case of the grain merchant who arrives in Rhodes during a famine, knowing more ships are on the way. Diogenes argues that honesty forbids lying, but does not require you to volunteer every fact that would lower your price. His student Antipater disagreed. The debate is still discussed in business ethics."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself in one deal or conversation today: am I just avoiding lies, or am I being truly open?","s":"Speech as reconstructed by Cicero, On Duties 3.50–53.","t":"","id":"q-diogenes-of-babylon-02","a":"diogenes-of-babylon"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 7.88","q":["The goal of life is to reason well","in the selection and rejection of things according to nature."],"c":["Diogenes gave this formulation of the Stoic goal. We cannot control outcomes, but we can choose well among the options life presents: health over sickness, safety over danger, where appropriate. The good lies in the quality of the choosing."],"e":null,"p":"For one choice today, focus on choosing well rather than on guaranteeing the outcome.","s":"Formula reported by Diogenes Laertius 7.88 and Stobaeus 2.7.6.","t":"","id":"q-diogenes-of-babylon-03","a":"diogenes-of-babylon"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Duties 3.52","q":["You must take thought for others and serve the community of humankind.","That is the law you were born under, the nature you must follow."],"c":["Cicero has Antipater argue against his teacher Diogenes in the case of the grain merchant at Rhodes. If the merchant hides the fact that more grain ships are coming, he is placing his own profit above the community to which all people belong. Antipater insists that honesty means full disclosure."],"e":null,"p":"In one situation today where you could stay silent for your own advantage, choose to share what you know.","s":"Speech as reconstructed by Cicero, On Duties 3.50–53.","t":"","id":"q-antipater-of-tarsus-01","a":"antipater-of-tarsus"},{"ref":"Stobaeus, Anthology 2.7.6","q":["The goal is to do everything in your power, continuously and without wavering,","to obtain the things that are by nature preferred."],"c":["Antipater refined the Stoic goal of life in debates with the sceptic Carneades. The Stoic aims at real things, health, safety, success, but what makes the life good is the steady effort, not the result. It is the basis of the image of the archer, who does all that can be done to hit the target, though the wind is not his to command."],"e":{"when":"c. 140 BC · Athens","title":"The pen-shouter","text":"Carneades of the Academy was the most feared debater of his time. Antipater did not dare to face him in live debate, so he wrote book after book in reply. He was mocked as \"kalamoboas\", the man who shouts with his pen. His careful written answers kept the Stoic school alive under Carneades' attack."},"p":"Choose a goal today. Do everything in your power towards it, and let the result be what it will be.","s":"Pen-shouter: Plutarch, On Talkativeness 514D.","t":"","id":"q-antipater-of-tarsus-02","a":"antipater-of-tarsus"},{"ref":"Stobaeus, Anthology 4.22 (On Marriage)","q":["For a well-born young man who wishes to serve his city,","marriage is among the first and most necessary of fitting duties."],"c":["A long fragment of Antipater's essay On Marriage survives. He describes marriage as a full partnership in which husband and wife share body, soul and property, so close that they are blended like wine and water. He argues that a good citizen should marry and raise children as part of their duty to the community."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that strengthens a bond of family or close friendship.","s":"Fragment preserved by Stobaeus; wording here is a free rendering.","t":"","id":"q-antipater-of-tarsus-03","a":"antipater-of-tarsus"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Duties 1.110","q":["Each person must hold firmly to their own nature,","as long as it is not wicked, so that they keep the fitting character that is proper to them."],"c":["Cicero bases this passage on Panaetius' theory of the four roles we play: our shared human nature, our individual nature, our circumstances and our chosen path. We should not fight our own talents and temperament to imitate someone else. Act well as the particular person you are."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that plays to your real strengths, instead of imitating someone else's.","s":"Based on Panaetius' On Appropriate Action, as adapted by Cicero, On Duties 1.107–117.","t":"","id":"q-panaetius-01","a":"panaetius"},{"ref":"Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 13.28","q":["The life of people who live among affairs and want to be useful to themselves and others","is like the stance of a pancratiast: always on guard, alert to the blows that come from every side."],"c":["Gellius quotes this from Panaetius' book On Appropriate Action. The pankration was a brutal Greek combat sport mixing boxing and wrestling. A person active in public life, Panaetius says, must be just as watchful, ready to block and respond to dangers coming from all directions."],"e":{"when":"c. 140 BC · Egypt and the East","title":"The embassy with Scipio","text":"Panaetius travelled with his friend Scipio Aemilianus, the destroyer of Carthage, on a Roman embassy to Egypt, Syria and the eastern kingdoms. It was a journey through a world of courts, intrigue and power, the kind of life where his advice about staying on guard was not theory."},"p":"Before an important conversation today, take a moment to prepare: where could it go wrong, and how will you respond?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-panaetius-02","a":"panaetius"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Duties 1.90","q":["Just as horses that have become wild from fighting are handed to trainers,","so people who have become unruly through success should be led into the training ground of reason."],"c":["Cicero tells us that Panaetius used to report this saying of his friend Scipio Africanus. Success can make people arrogant and careless. Like a horse made skittish by battle, they need to be retrained, through reflection, to stay calm and humble."],"e":null,"p":"After any success today, take a moment to come back down and look at it soberly.","s":"Saying of Scipio reported by Panaetius, via Cicero, On Duties 1.90.","t":"","id":"q-panaetius-03","a":"panaetius"},{"ref":"Plutarch, Life of Cato the Younger 4","q":["I begin to speak only when I am sure","that what I have to say is not better left unsaid."],"c":["As a young man Cato was criticised for being silent. His answer shows his discipline. He did not lack opinions; he was careful about when to share them. Later in life his speeches in the Senate became famous for their force, because people knew he did not speak lightly."],"e":null,"p":"Before speaking today, ask whether what you're about to say is better than silence.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-01","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Plutarch, Life of Cato the Younger 3","q":["Why does no one kill this man?"],"c":["As a boy of fourteen, Cato was taken to the house of the dictator Sulla, where he saw the heads of executed men being brought in. He asked his tutor why no one killed Sulla. \"Because they fear him more than they hate him,\" the tutor replied. \"Then give me a sword,\" said the boy, \"so that I can free my country from slavery.\" His tutor kept a close eye on him after that."],"e":{"when":"82–81 BC · Rome","title":"The proscriptions of Sulla","text":"After winning a civil war, Lucius Cornelius Sulla made himself dictator and published lists of enemies whose lives and property were forfeit. Thousands were killed, many for their wealth alone. The young Cato grew up in this terror; it shaped his lifelong hatred of one-man rule."},"p":"Notice one wrong around you that people tolerate because they fear to speak. Decide what small thing you can do.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-02","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Sallust, The War with Catiline 52","q":["It was not by arms that our ancestors made this republic great,","but by hard work at home, just rule abroad, and a mind free in counsel, not enslaved to crime or passion."],"c":["Sallust gives this speech to Cato in the Senate debate on the Catilinarian conspirators. The words are Sallust's reconstruction, but they reflect what Cato stood for: a republic founded on effort, honesty and self-control, now threatened by luxury and greed."],"e":{"when":"5 December 63 BC · Temple of Concord, Rome","title":"The Catiline debate","text":"The Senate debated the fate of the arrested conspirators of Catiline, who had planned to seize power. Julius Caesar argued for life imprisonment. Cato, then only a tribune-elect, answered with a speech so forceful that the Senate voted for execution. The conspirators were strangled that night."},"p":"In one decision today, ask whether you are acting out of effort and integrity or out of comfort.","s":"Speech as written by Sallust, The War with Catiline 52.","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-03","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Seneca, On Anger 2.32","q":["I do not remember being struck."],"c":["Seneca tells that Cato was once struck in the public baths by a man who did not recognise him. When the man later came to apologise, Cato replied with these words. It was better, Seneca comments, not to acknowledge an injury than to forgive it."],"e":null,"p":"Let go of one small offence today so completely that you do not even need to forgive it.","s":"Anecdote reported by Seneca, On Anger 2.32.","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-04","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Plutarch, Life of Cato the Younger 66","q":["I do not want to be indebted to a tyrant for his lawless acts.","He has no right to save, as master, those over whom he has no right to rule."],"c":["After Caesar's victory at Thapsus, Cato's friends urged him to ask Caesar for mercy. Cato refused. Accepting Caesar's pardon would mean accepting that Caesar had the right to give it. He would not recognise the power of a man who had seized the state."],"e":{"when":"April 46 BC · Utica, North Africa","title":"Cato's last night","text":"With Caesar's army approaching, Cato arranged ships for everyone in Utica who wished to escape, debated with friends that only the good are free, and read Plato's Phaedo twice. Then he stabbed himself. Caesar, on hearing it, said: \"Cato, I begrudge you your death, as you begrudged me the saving of your life.\""},"p":"Is there an advantage you are offered today at the cost of your principles? Weigh it honestly.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-05","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Plutarch, Life of Cato the Younger 70","q":["Now I am my own master."],"c":["Plutarch says Cato spoke these words on his last night, after he had seen his friends off to the ships. Whatever we think of his decision, the line expresses the Stoic idea that, whatever else is taken away, no one can take away one's control over one's own will."],"e":null,"p":"In the middle of pressure today, remember the part that is still fully yours: your choice of response.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-cato-the-younger-06","a":"cato-the-younger"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 1.1","q":["Claim yourself for yourself.","Gather and guard the time that until now was taken from you, stolen or simply slipped away."],"c":["These are the first lines of the 124 letters Seneca wrote in the last years of his life to his friend Lucilius, a Roman official in Sicily. He opens not with theory but with time: most of it, he says, is taken by others, some is stolen, and the largest part simply drifts away while we are not paying attention.","\"Hold every hour in your grasp,\" he continues. \"You will depend less on tomorrow if you lay hold of today.\""],"e":{"when":"AD 62 · Rome","title":"Seneca asks to retire","text":"After his ally Burrus died, Seneca asked Nero for permission to withdraw from court and offered to hand over his vast fortune. Nero refused the money but Seneca withdrew anyway, avoided Rome and spent his time reading and writing. The letters to Lucilius were written in this hard-won free time."},"p":"Track where your hours go today. At the end of the day, mark which ones were taken, which were stolen, and which you actually chose.","s":"Retirement: Tacitus, Annals 14.53–56.","t":"","id":"q-seneca-01","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On the Shortness of Life 1.3","q":["It is not that we have a short time to live,","but that we waste a great deal of it."],"c":["Seneca wrote this essay to Paulinus, an official in charge of Rome's grain supply, who was buried in work. Life is long enough, he argues, and generous enough for the greatest achievements, if the whole of it is well invested. We do not receive a short life; we make it short.","He lists the ways: chasing other people's approval, endless business, pointless busyness, idle pleasure. People guard their money carefully but give away their time to anyone who asks."],"e":null,"p":"Find one thing today you do out of habit that you would not choose if you noticed it. Give that time to something you value.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-02","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 13.4","q":["There are more things that frighten us than things that actually crush us.","We suffer more often in imagination than in reality."],"c":["Seneca writes to Lucilius about fear. Many of the things we dread never arrive; others are smaller than we pictured; others are still far off. Yet we suffer them all in advance. Before you give in to fear, he advises, ask whether the danger is certain, whether it is near, and whether it is really as bad as it looks."],"e":null,"p":"Take your biggest worry today and ask three questions: is it certain? is it near? is it as bad as I imagine?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-03","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 7.8","q":["People learn while they teach."],"c":["In this letter Seneca warns Lucilius against crowds, which bring vices home with them, and recommends spending time with people who can make him better, or whom he can make better. The help goes both ways. Explaining something to someone else is one of the best ways to understand it yourself."],"e":null,"p":"Explain one idea you care about to someone today. Notice what you learn by trying.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-04","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 2.6","q":["It is not the one who has too little who is poor,","but the one who wants more."],"c":["Seneca often borrowed lines from other philosophers, even rival schools, to close his letters. Here he makes a point the Stoics and Epicureans agreed on: poverty is not measured by what you have but by the gap between what you have and what you want. Close the gap and you are rich."],"e":null,"p":"Before buying anything today that you don't need, wait. Ask whether you want the thing, or only the feeling of getting it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-05","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 116.8","q":["\"I can't\" is the excuse.","\"I won't\" is the reason."],"c":["Lucilius had suggested that some passions are too strong to resist. Seneca disagrees. We say we cannot because it is easier than admitting that we do not want to. We defend our faults, he says, because we love them, and we would rather excuse them than cure them."],"e":null,"p":"Catch one \"I can't\" in your thoughts today. Honestly rename it \"I won't\" and see whether that changes your answer.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-06","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 28.1","q":["You need to change your mind, not your sky."],"c":["Lucilius had travelled to escape his low mood and was surprised it came with him. Seneca replies with a remark of Socrates: why wonder that travel does not help you, when you always take yourself along? Wherever you go, your problems travel with you. A new place can refresh, but it cannot cure what lives in the mind."],"e":null,"p":"If you are tempted to fix a bad mood by changing your surroundings today, first ask what thought you are carrying with you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-07","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On Anger 3.36","q":["Every day the mind should be called to account.","What bad habit have you cured today? What fault have you resisted? In what way are you better?"],"c":["Seneca describes the evening practice he learned from his teacher Quintus Sextius. After the lamp was put out and his wife had fallen silent, Seneca went through his whole day, examining what he had done and said, hiding nothing from himself. Then, he says, he slept peacefully."],"e":{"when":"c. 40 BC – AD 10 · Rome","title":"The school of the Sextii","text":"Quintus Sextius turned down a seat in the Senate offered by Julius Caesar to found a philosophical school in Rome. It combined Stoic ethics with Pythagorean habits like vegetarianism and nightly self-examination. It did not outlast its founders, but through Seneca, who studied with Sextius' followers, its evening review became one of the best-known Stoic exercises."},"p":"Tonight, before sleep, ask Sextius' three questions about your day. Be honest, and be kind to yourself in the answer.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-08","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On Providence 5.10","q":["Fire tests gold,","hardship tests the brave."],"c":["In On Providence Seneca answers the old question: why do bad things happen to good people? His answer is that hardship is a kind of training. Just as athletes need strong opponents, a person needs difficulty to show and grow their strength. The good are not spared trouble; they are tested by it."],"e":{"when":"AD 41–49 · Corsica","title":"Eight years of exile","text":"Accused of adultery with the emperor's niece, Seneca was banished to Corsica, a rough, malarial island he described with dislike. He was in his forties, cut off from his career and his family. Yet there he wrote consolations to his mother and to others, and deepened the philosophy that would shape his later works."},"p":"When something hard comes up today, think of it as the fire, not the end. What is it testing in you?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-09","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 18.5","q":["Set aside some days on which you are content with very little and very plain food, with rough and coarse clothing,","and say to yourself: \"Is this what I was afraid of?\""],"c":["Seneca wrote this letter in December, during the Saturnalia, when Rome gave itself over to feasting. He did not recommend avoiding the party completely. But he did recommend practising poverty now and then, so that the fear of losing comfort loses its grip. Once you have lived with little, you discover how little you need."],"e":{"when":"December · Rome","title":"The Saturnalia","text":"The Saturnalia was Rome's winter festival in honour of Saturn, celebrated from 17 December. Shops closed, people exchanged gifts, gambling was allowed and slaves were served by their masters. Seneca wrote that \"the whole city is in a sweat\" and used the noise of the festival as the setting for his advice on simplicity."},"p":"Try one small voluntary discomfort today: a cold shower, a simple meal, a walk instead of a ride. Then ask: is this what I was afraid of?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-10","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 47.1","q":["\"They are slaves.\" No, they are human beings.","\"They are slaves.\" No, they are housemates. \"They are slaves.\" No, they are humble friends."],"c":["Seneca does not call for abolishing slavery, which almost no one in the ancient world did. But he insists that slaves are human beings, born of the same seed, under the same sky. He criticises masters who eat in silence while slaves stand hungry and are beaten for coughing. Treat those below you, he says, as you would want to be treated by those above you."],"e":{"when":"AD 61 · Rome","title":"The slaves of Pedanius Secundus","text":"When the city prefect Pedanius Secundus was murdered by one of his slaves, Roman law demanded that all the slaves in the household be executed, around four hundred people. Crowds protested, but the Senate voted for the old law and the sentence was carried out under military guard. It is the world in which Seneca's letter was written."},"p":"Notice how you treat someone today who serves you: a cashier, a waiter, a colleague below you. Treat them the way you would like to be treated.","s":"Pedanius: Tacitus, Annals 14.42–45.","t":"","id":"q-seneca-11","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 91.4","q":["Nothing should be unexpected by us.","We must send our minds ahead to meet everything, and consider not what usually happens but what can happen."],"c":["Seneca wrote this letter after hearing that a whole city had burned in a single night. A friend from that city was devastated. Seneca's advice is the Stoic exercise of premeditation: imagine in calm times the misfortunes that could come, so they do not take you by surprise."],"e":{"when":"AD 64 or 65 · Lugdunum (Lyon)","title":"The fire of Lyon","text":"Lugdunum, capital of Roman Gaul, burned to the ground in a single night. \"There was one night between a great city and none,\" Seneca wrote. The city had just sent a large sum to Rome after its own great fire. Nero sent money in return to help rebuild."},"p":"Take one plan you are counting on and briefly imagine it failing. What would you do? Then go ahead, calmer.","s":"Fire: Seneca, Letters 91; Tacitus, Annals 16.13.","t":"","id":"q-seneca-12","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 71.3","q":["If you do not know which harbour you are heading for,","no wind is the right wind."],"c":["Lucilius had asked Seneca for advice on a specific decision. Seneca replies that individual decisions are hard to make without first knowing the goal of your whole life. Without that, every piece of advice is random. Once you know where you are going, decisions become easier."],"e":null,"p":"Write one sentence describing where you are trying to go this year. Use it to make one decision today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-13","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On Anger 3.12","q":["The greatest remedy for anger is delay."],"c":["Seneca's book On Anger, written for his brother Novatus, describes anger as a brief madness. Its first rush is the strongest. If you can give it time, it passes, and judgment returns. Seneca recommends asking anger for a delay, not for forgiveness: it does not need to be cancelled, just postponed."],"e":null,"p":"When you feel anger rise today, give it a delay: ten breaths, ten minutes, or until tomorrow. Then decide.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-14","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 107.11","q":["The fates lead the willing,","and drag the unwilling."],"c":["Seneca translates the prayer of Cleanthes into Latin verse and adds this final line, which became one of the most quoted sentences in Stoic writing. Events happen whether we agree or not. The only choice is whether to walk with them, or be dragged and complain."],"e":null,"p":"Name one thing today that is going to happen whether you like it or not. Choose to walk with it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-15","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 3.2","q":["Think for a long time about whether to admit someone to your friendship.","But once you have decided, welcome them with all your heart, and speak with them as boldly as with yourself."],"c":["Lucilius had called someone a \"friend\" while saying he could not trust him with everything. Seneca objects. You should judge carefully before friendship, he says, and trust fully after. Many people do the reverse: trust quickly and then judge."],"e":null,"p":"Think of one friend you trust fully. Tell them something you have been holding back.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-16","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 24.20","q":["We die every day.","Every day a part of life is taken from us."],"c":["Death, Seneca argues, is not a single event ahead of us. It is a process that began at birth. Every hour that passes is already gone. The thought is not meant to depress but to change perspective: the last day does not bring death; it only completes it."],"e":null,"p":"Look at today as a part of your life you are spending. What do you want to buy with it?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-17","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On the Happy Life 26.1","q":["The wise person does not love wealth, but prefers it.","They keep it in their house, not in their heart."],"c":["Seneca was one of the richest men in Rome, and critics called him a hypocrite. In On the Happy Life he answers them. A philosopher need not be poor. What matters is whether you can lose your wealth without losing yourself. If it leaves, it takes only itself with it."],"e":{"when":"AD 58 · Rome","title":"The attack of Suillius","text":"Publius Suillius Rufus, a prosecutor under the previous emperor, attacked Seneca in public: what philosophy, he asked, had allowed Seneca to collect 300 million sesterces in four years of royal friendship? Suillius was exiled, but the accusation stuck, and Seneca defended his position in writing."},"p":"Name one thing you own that you would struggle to lose. Imagine losing it, and remind yourself you would still be you.","s":"Suillius: Tacitus, Annals 13.42.","t":"","id":"q-seneca-18","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"On the Shortness of Life 7.3","q":["It takes a whole life to learn how to live,","and, what may surprise you more, it takes a whole life to learn how to die."],"c":["Seneca observes that many skills can be learned quickly, even by the young. But living well cannot be mastered once and filed away; it is a lifelong study. Many of the greatest people, he says, gave up wealth, pleasure and duties so they could focus on this single thing until the end."],"e":null,"p":"What is one thing about living well you are still learning? Practise it once today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-19","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 24.13","q":["Not only from people, but from things too, the mask must be removed,","and their true face restored."],"c":["Seneca describes how children are frightened by masks worn by people they love. Adults are no different. Swords, fire, prison, pain and death look terrible when dressed up by fear. Look at them plainly, he says, and see what they really are: often less than their reputation."],"e":null,"p":"Name something you dread. Take off its mask by describing it in plain words.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-20","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 33.11","q":["Those who went before us are not our masters","but our guides."],"c":["Lucilius asked for more quotations from great philosophers. Seneca warns him against simply collecting famous lines. The truth is open to all and has not yet been fully discovered. Use the ancients as guides, not as masters to be blindly followed, and add something of your own."],"e":null,"p":"Take one rule or saying you follow without thinking, and ask whether you agree with it after all.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-21","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 95.52","q":["We are limbs of one great body."],"c":["Seneca argues that all human beings are bound together by nature. Born from the same source and for the same purpose, we owe each other help. Like stones in an arch, he adds, we would fall if we did not hold each other up."],"e":null,"p":"Do one small thing today for someone who has no way to repay you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-22","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 101.10","q":["Begin to live at once,","and count each separate day as a separate life."],"c":["Seneca writes after the sudden death of a friend, Senecio, who had been busy making plans for the future. We keep postponing life, he notes, waiting for the next thing. Instead, treat each day as complete in itself."],"e":null,"p":"Live today as a whole life: start something, do something well, end the day in peace.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-23","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Letters to Lucilius 56.5","q":["There may be uproar outside,","as long as there is none within."],"c":["Seneca wrote this letter while living above a public bath. He describes the noise in detail: weightlifters grunting, masseurs slapping, a man who loved the sound of his own voice in the bath, sellers of cakes and sausages. His point is that true quiet does not come from silence outside, but from a mind at rest."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 63 · Seneca's lodgings","title":"Above the bathhouse","text":"Roman public baths were loud, crowded social centres where people exercised, bathed, ate and gossiped. Seneca, rich enough to live anywhere, found himself in rooms directly above one and used it as a test of his calm, listing every noise with obvious amusement."},"p":"Next time noise distracts you today, treat it as training: return your attention to your task without blaming the noise.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-seneca-24","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Tacitus, Annals 15.62","q":["Since I am not allowed to repay your services,","I leave you the one thing I still have, and the finest: the pattern of my life."],"c":["When the order came from Nero to die, Seneca asked for writing tablets to make his will. The centurion refused. Seneca turned to his friends and spoke these words, as Tacitus reports. If they remembered it, he said, they would win a reputation for virtue and loyal friendship."],"e":{"when":"April AD 65 · Near Rome","title":"The death of Seneca","text":"After the Pisonian conspiracy against Nero was discovered, Seneca was ordered to take his own life. He opened his veins, but the blood flowed slowly; he took poison, which failed; and he was finally placed in a warm bath, where he died. He dictated his last thoughts to secretaries until the end."},"p":"What would someone learn from the pattern of your life today, if they only saw that?","s":"Tacitus, Annals 15.60–64. Seneca's last words reported by Tacitus.","t":"","id":"q-seneca-25","a":"seneca"},{"ref":"Lectures 5","q":["Practice is more important than theory.","Theory teaches how to act; practice is what makes us able to act."],"c":["Musonius asked his students which was stronger, habit or argument. A doctor who has studied medicine but never treated a patient, or a pilot who knows the theory of sailing but has never held a rudder, is of little use. Ethics, he said, is the same. Knowing what is right is only the beginning; doing it repeatedly is what forms character."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one principle you already know is right. Practise it three times today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-01","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 3","q":["Women have received from the gods the same reason as men.","Why then should it be fitting for men to seek and consider how to live well, which is what philosophy is, but not for women?"],"c":["In a culture where women were rarely educated beyond the household, Musonius argued that women have the same senses, the same capacity for reason and the same natural inclination to virtue. A woman who studies philosophy, he said, will be a better manager of her home, a better partner and more just and self-controlled."],"e":null,"p":"Think of someone whose view you usually overlook. Ask for it today, and take it seriously.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-02","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 4","q":["Daughters should be educated in the same way as sons."],"c":["Musonius pressed the argument further than most of his contemporaries. If the virtues of men and women are the same, then their training should be too. Courage, justice and self-control are not male or female; they are human. He did allow that some practical tasks might be divided by strength, but not the education of the mind."],"e":null,"p":"Look at how you encourage the young people around you. Are you expecting the same courage and judgment from all of them?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-03","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 6","q":["Since a human being is neither soul alone nor body alone, but a combination of the two,","whoever is in training must care for both, the soul more, but the body too."],"c":["Musonius taught that philosophy requires physical training as well as mental. We should get used to cold, heat, thirst, hunger, plain food and a hard bed. This toughening does not make us better in itself, but it makes the soul steadier, and the body ready to carry out what the mind decides."],"e":null,"p":"Do something today that is physically uncomfortable but good for you, and notice how it steadies your mind.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-04","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 7","q":["People endure hard work and pain to win a lover or to make money.","Is it not absurd that we will not endure the same for the sake of goodness?"],"c":["Musonius points out how much effort people are willing to make for things of little value: athletes who train for a crown, merchants who risk their lives at sea, soldiers who bear wounds for pay. Virtue is worth far more, and the effort it costs is usually far less."],"e":null,"p":"Compare the effort you put into your job or hobby with the effort you put into becoming a better person. Shift some of it today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-05","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 8","q":["A king must study philosophy,","for no one needs to know better what is just and good than the one who rules over others."],"c":["This lecture is addressed to a king from Syria who came to hear Musonius. Musonius tells him that a ruler should be a model of self-control and justice, should judge fairly and should not be ruled by anger or pleasure. Philosophy is not a private hobby for a ruler but a duty owed to all his subjects."],"e":{"when":"AD 69 · Outside Rome","title":"The lecture to the army","text":"During the civil war that brought Vespasian to power, Musonius went with a peace delegation to meet the advancing troops. He walked among the soldiers, lecturing them on the blessings of peace and the dangers of war. Many mocked him, others found him tiresome, and Tacitus says he had to stop before being beaten. He spoke anyway."},"p":"If you lead anyone today, at home or at work, ask: what would a just and self-controlled leader do here?","s":"Tacitus, Histories 3.81.","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-06","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 9","q":["Exile does not take away anything that is truly good.","The exile is not prevented from being brave, just or self-controlled."],"c":["Musonius speaks from experience. He was exiled more than once and argued that exile deprives us only of things like home, friends and status, none of which can make us good or bad. Many famous people, he says, have done their best work far from home."],"e":{"when":"AD 65 · Gyara, Aegean Sea","title":"The island prison","text":"After the conspiracy against Nero, Musonius was banished to Gyara, a rocky and waterless island used for political prisoners. Visitors came by sea to hear him teach. One later account says he found a spring on the island, making it easier to live on. He returned to Rome after Nero's death."},"p":"If you feel cut off from something today, ask what part of your character it has actually taken away.","s":"Gyara: Tacitus, Annals 15.71; Philostratus, Life of Apollonius 7.16.","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-07","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 10","q":["To accept an injury without savagery, without being implacable towards the one who did it,","to be instead a source of good hope for them, is the mark of a civilised and gentle character."],"c":["Musonius was asked whether a philosopher should take someone to court for an insult. His answer was no. Being slapped or mocked is not a real harm; what we lose is only an imagined dignity. Instead of revenge, he recommends patience, and even being a source of hope for the person who offended."],"e":null,"p":"If someone slights you today, let it go without needing to win.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-08","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 13A","q":["In marriage there must be complete companionship and mutual care between husband and wife,","in health and sickness and at all times."],"c":["Musonius described marriage as a partnership in which both partners share everything, body, possessions and life, and each tries to outdo the other in care. A marriage where each looks only to their own interest falls apart, however beautiful the house. It was a high view of marriage for his time, and an equal one."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today for your partner, or a close friend, that puts their needs first.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-09","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 15","q":["To have many brothers and sisters is a far greater good","than to have many possessions."],"c":["Musonius opposed the common practice of abandoning unwanted newborns, especially among the wealthy who wanted fewer heirs. Children, brothers and sisters are a greater good than wealth, he argued. A family is a resource no fortune can buy."],"e":null,"p":"Spend time today with family or friends, not as a duty, but as the wealth it is.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-10","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 16","q":["If your father forbids you to study philosophy, you need not obey him,","for you would be disobeying him only to obey a better father: reason, which is common to all."],"c":["Musonius asks whether children must always obey their parents. In most things, yes. But obedience means following good advice; if a parent commands something wrong, refusing is the deeper form of respect. A son who becomes good, he says, honours his father more than one who simply does what he is told."],"e":null,"p":"Is there a rule you follow only because someone told you to? Ask whether reason supports it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-11","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 18A","q":["We should eat in order to live, not live in order to eat."],"c":["Musonius taught that food is the first test of self-control, because we face it several times every day. He recommended simple food that needs little preparation, such as grains, vegetables, fruit, milk and cheese, and warned against gluttony, which makes people resemble pigs and dogs more than humans."],"e":null,"p":"At one meal today, eat simply and slowly, and stop when you have had enough.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-12","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 19","q":["We should use clothing and shelter to protect the body, not to impress others."],"c":["Musonius argued that houses and clothes should serve the needs of the body. Expensive marble, gilded ceilings and fine robes do not make anyone happier, and keeping them up costs time and freedom. A simple house is easier to keep, and leaves money for helping others."],"e":null,"p":"Before buying or wearing something today, ask: is this for my needs, or for others' eyes?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-13","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Lectures 11","q":["Is not working the land a better and more fitting way of life for a philosopher","than living in the city and depending on others?"],"c":["Musonius praised farming as a livelihood for a philosopher. It keeps the body strong, provides an honest living and allows teaching in the open, as students work alongside the teacher and see virtue in action. It is a vision of philosophy lived in daily work rather than in lecture halls alone."],"e":null,"p":"Do something with your hands today, and let it be part of your thinking time.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-14","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 16.1","q":["If you do something good with effort, the effort passes, but the good remains.","If you do something shameful with pleasure, the pleasure passes, but the shame remains."],"c":["Aulus Gellius heard this saying as a young man in Athens and attributes it to Musonius. It shows in two sentences how differently effort and pleasure behave over time. One leaves something lasting behind; the other leaves only what you would rather forget."],"e":null,"p":"When faced with a choice today, ask what will remain once the effort or pleasure has passed.","s":"Saying attributed to Musonius by Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 16.1.","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-15","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Epictetus, Discourses 3.23.29","q":["If you have time to praise me, I am speaking to no purpose."],"c":["Epictetus remembered his teacher's lectures as uncomfortable. Musonius spoke so directly, Epictetus says, that each listener thought someone had told him about their faults. A philosopher's lecture room, Epictetus added, is a doctor's surgery: you should leave feeling pain, not pleasure. Applause meant the medicine had not worked."],"e":null,"p":"Ask someone you trust to tell you one thing you could do better, and resist the urge to defend yourself.","s":"Saying reported by Epictetus, Discourses 3.23.29.","t":"","id":"q-musonius-rufus-16","a":"musonius-rufus"},{"ref":"Handbook 1","q":["Some things are up to us, and some are not.","Up to us are our judgments, our impulses, our desires and aversions: in a word, whatever is our own doing."],"c":["These are the first words of the Handbook, the short summary of Epictetus' teaching made by his student Arrian. Everything else follows from this division. Our body, property, reputation and position are not fully up to us; they can be taken away. Our own judgments and choices are. Peace comes from caring most about the second group."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 93 · Rome","title":"Domitian expels the philosophers","text":"The emperor Domitian, suspicious of philosophers who praised the old Stoic martyrs, banished them all from Rome and Italy. Epictetus, by then a free man and a respected teacher, moved to Nicopolis on the coast of north-west Greece and opened a school there. Exile, which he could not control, became the place where his greatest work was done."},"p":"Make two lists for today's worries: up to me, not up to me. Spend your energy only on the first.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-01","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 5","q":["People are disturbed not by things,","but by their judgments about things."],"c":["Epictetus gives the most extreme example: death. Death is not terrible, he says, or it would have seemed so to Socrates. What is terrible is the judgment that death is terrible. So when we are troubled, we should not blame others but our own judgments. An uneducated person blames others; someone beginning to learn blames themselves; an educated person blames no one."],"e":null,"p":"When you're upset today, finish this sentence: \"I am upset because I judge that...\" Then look at the judgment.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-02","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 8","q":["Do not ask for things to happen as you wish.","Wish for them to happen as they do happen, and your life will go well."],"c":["This is one of the hardest lines in Stoic teaching. Epictetus does not say we should not try to change things. He says we should not make our peace depend on events going our way. Do your part, then accept the outcome as the place where your next action begins."],"e":{"when":"Before AD 68 · Rome","title":"The broken leg","text":"A story told by the early Christian writer Origen says that Epictetus' master Epaphroditus once twisted his leg as a test. Epictetus calmly warned him that it would break. When it did, he said only: \"Didn't I tell you it would break?\" Whether true or legend, the story became a symbol of how he lived what he taught."},"p":"Pick one thing today that did not go your way, and practise wanting it exactly as it is, as the starting point for what you do next.","s":"Story of the leg: Origen, Against Celsus 7.53. Another ancient source says he was lame from illness.","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-03","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 3","q":["With everything that delights you, or is useful, or that you love, remind yourself what kind of thing it is, starting with the smallest.","If you are fond of a jug, say: \"I am fond of a jug.\" Then if it breaks, you will not be upset."],"c":["Epictetus goes on to apply this to the people we love most, reminding ourselves that they are mortal. It sounds cold, but it is the opposite. Knowing that what we love is fragile keeps us from taking it for granted, and keeps loss from shattering us when it comes."],"e":null,"p":"With something or someone you love today, pause for a moment and remember that it is not permanent. Then enjoy it more fully.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-04","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 11","q":["Never say of anything, \"I have lost it\", but rather, \"I have given it back\"."],"c":["Has your child died? Given back. Your wife? Given back. Your property was taken? That too was given back. Epictetus reminds his students that everything we have is on loan. While it is ours, we should care for it like travellers caring for an inn: not as our own, but as something entrusted to us for a while."],"e":null,"p":"Think of something you lost recently. Instead of \"I lost it\", say \"I gave it back\", and see how that changes the feeling.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-05","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 17","q":["Remember that you are an actor in a play, whose character the author has chosen.","Your task is to play well the part you are given. Choosing the part belongs to another."],"c":["You may be given the part of a beggar, a king, someone disabled or an ordinary citizen. Epictetus, a former slave who was lame, knew this well. The part is not up to us. How well we play it is."],"e":null,"p":"Name the role you are playing today: parent, colleague, friend, leader. Play it as well as you can.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-06","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 20","q":["Remember that it is not the one who insults or strikes you who wrongs you,","but your judgment that they are wronging you."],"c":["When someone provokes you, Epictetus says, know that it is your own judgment that has provoked you. So the first task is not to be carried away by the impression. Gain some time and delay, and you will more easily master yourself."],"e":null,"p":"When someone annoys you today, pause and say: \"This is an impression, not a fact.\" Then decide how to respond.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-07","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 33","q":["Be silent for the most part, or say only what is necessary, and in few words."],"c":["In this chapter Epictetus gives a set of rules for daily conduct. Avoid long talk about gladiators, horse races, athletes, food and drink, and above all about other people, whether praising, blaming or comparing them. Speak when there is a reason; let your words be fewer than your actions."],"e":null,"p":"In one conversation today, speak less than usual. Notice what you learn by listening.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-08","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 46","q":["Never call yourself a philosopher, and do not talk much among ordinary people about your principles.","Act on them instead."],"c":["Sheep do not bring their grass to the shepherd to show how much they have eaten, Epictetus says. They digest it and produce wool and milk. In the same way, do not show off your principles; show the actions that come from digesting them."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that shows your values, without mentioning the values.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-09","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 48","q":["Signs of someone making progress: they blame no one, praise no one, find fault with no one, accuse no one.","They say nothing about themselves as if they were someone or knew something."],"c":["Epictetus describes the student who is truly improving. They are quiet about themselves, slow to blame, unbothered by praise or criticism. If they are praised, they laugh to themselves; if they are blamed, they do not defend themselves. They watch over themselves, Epictetus says, like an enemy lying in wait."],"e":null,"p":"For one day, try not to blame anyone, including yourself. Just observe and adjust.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-10","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 51","q":["How long will you wait before you think yourself worthy of the best,","and never go against the decision of reason?"],"c":["Epictetus urges his students not to keep postponing their own improvement. You have learned the principles, he says; you are no longer a child. If you keep putting it off, you will live and die as an ordinary person. Socrates became Socrates by paying attention to nothing but reason in everything he did."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 108 · Nicopolis","title":"Arrian takes notes","text":"A young man from Nicomedia, Lucius Flavius Arrianus, studied with Epictetus and wrote down his lectures as nearly word for word as he could, \"to preserve the thoughts and frankness of his speech\". Arrian later became a Roman consul, a governor and the historian of Alexander the Great. Without his notebook, Epictetus' teaching would be lost."},"p":"Is there something you keep saying you will start \"someday\"? Start today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-11","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 13","q":["If you want to improve, be content to be thought foolish and simple in external matters."],"c":["Progress often looks odd to others. If you stop chasing what others value, they may think you are naive. Epictetus says to accept that. You cannot keep your inner choice in line with nature and also secure everything outside you; caring for one means neglecting the other."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that is right but might look foolish to others.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-12","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.1","q":["I must die. Must I die groaning as well?","I must be put in chains. Must I complain as well? I must go into exile. Does anyone stop me from going with a smile, cheerful and calm?"],"c":["Epictetus is describing what is up to us and what is not. Death, chains and exile can come from outside. How we meet them is our own. He tells the story of Paconius Agrippinus to show it."],"e":{"when":"AD 66 · Rome","title":"\"Let's have lunch at Aricia\"","text":"The senator Paconius Agrippinus was on trial before the Senate under Nero. When a messenger came to say his case was being heard, Agrippinus went on with his exercise and bath. When told he had been condemned to exile, he asked only: \"Where? To Aricia?\" and said, \"Then let's go and have lunch there.\" Aricia was a town a short ride from Rome."},"p":"When something unwelcome comes today, ask: this I cannot change, but must I add complaint to it?","s":"Agrippinus: Epictetus, Discourses 1.1.28–30; Tacitus, Annals 16.28–33.","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-13","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.2","q":["\"Then I will kill you.\" \"When did I ever tell you I was immortal?","You will do your part, and I will do mine. Yours is to kill; mine is to die without trembling.\""],"c":["Epictetus tells this as the exchange between the emperor Vespasian and the senator Helvidius Priscus, a Stoic who refused to stay away from the Senate or keep quiet there. He uses it to show what it means to keep to one's own role, whatever the cost."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 75 · Rome","title":"Helvidius Priscus","text":"Helvidius Priscus, son-in-law of the Stoic martyr Thrasea Paetus, openly criticised Vespasian and refused to address him by his imperial titles. He was exiled and then executed. For later Stoics he was a model of frank speech, praised by Epictetus and remembered with honour by Marcus Aurelius."},"p":"Is there something true that you are avoiding saying because it is uncomfortable? Find the right way to say it.","s":"Helvidius: Suetonius, Vespasian 15; Marcus Aurelius, Meditations 1.14.","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-14","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.15","q":["Nothing great comes into being all at once; not even a bunch of grapes or a fig.","If you tell me you want a fig now, I will answer: it needs time. Let it first blossom, then bear fruit, then ripen."],"c":["A student asked Epictetus how to stop being angry with his brother. Epictetus answers that a change of character is like ripening fruit. It cannot be done in an hour. Patience with yourself is part of the work."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one habit you are trying to change. Instead of judging today's progress, ask only whether the fruit is a little riper.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-15","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 2.18","q":["Every habit and ability is strengthened by its corresponding actions: walking by walking, running by running.","If you want to do something, make a habit of it. If you do not want to, do not do it, and get used to doing something else instead."],"c":["Epictetus explains that anger, desire and every vice grow stronger each time we give in to them, just as muscles grow with use. The good news is that the reverse is also true. Count the days you have not been angry, he advises: every other day, then every third, then every fourth."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one bad habit and count today as day one without it. Choose one good habit and do it once.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-16","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 3.23","q":["First tell yourself what you want to be,","then do what you have to do."],"c":["This is the same in every craft, Epictetus says. The athlete first decides what kind of athlete to be, then trains accordingly. The same applies to life. Unless we decide what we want to become, our actions will be random, and we will act without direction."],"e":null,"p":"Write one sentence: \"I want to be someone who...\" Then do one thing today that follows from it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-17","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 4.1","q":["Freedom is not won by satisfying your desires,","but by removing them."],"c":["Discourses 4.1 is the longest lecture on freedom. Epictetus, a former slave, insists that freedom is something deeper than legal status. Many free people are slaves to wealth, approval and fear. Whoever wants nothing that others control cannot be controlled."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one desire today that makes you depend on someone else's approval. Loosen your grip on it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-18","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.18","q":["I lost my lamp because the thief was better at staying awake than I was.","But he paid for that lamp: for a lamp, he became a thief; for a lamp, he lost his good faith."],"c":["Epictetus uses a real loss from his own house to show how to think about being wronged. The thief gained a lamp but lost something far more valuable: his honesty. Epictetus lost only an object. Who has really suffered the loss?"],"e":{"when":"After AD 135 · Rome","title":"The lamp of Epictetus","text":"According to the satirist Lucian, after Epictetus' death someone bought his simple clay lamp for three thousand drachmas, hoping that by reading at night by its light he would gain Epictetus' wisdom. Lucian mocks the buyer: wisdom cannot be bought along with a lamp."},"p":"If something is taken from you today, ask what the other person paid for it.","s":"Lamp: Lucian, The Ignorant Book-Collector 13.","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-19","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.24","q":["It is difficulties that show what people are.","When a difficulty comes, remember that God, like a trainer, has matched you with a tough young opponent."],"c":["Why? asks Epictetus. So that you can become an Olympic victor; and that cannot happen without sweat. Hardship is not a sign that something has gone wrong, but a training partner."],"e":{"when":"9 September 1965 · North Vietnam","title":"Stockdale ejects","text":"US Navy pilot James Stockdale was shot down over North Vietnam. As he floated down by parachute, he later said, he told himself he was \"leaving the world of technology and entering the world of Epictetus\". He spent seven and a half years as a prisoner of war, much of it in solitary confinement and under torture, and credited Epictetus' teaching with helping him survive."},"p":"Treat today's hardest moment as a training partner. What is it training you in?","s":"Stockdale: J. B. Stockdale, Courage under Fire (1993).","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-20","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 2.17","q":["It is impossible for someone to begin to learn what they think they already know."],"c":["Epictetus begins this lecture by observing that everyone thinks they know what is good and bad, just and unjust. Because we think we already know, we never examine it. Real learning starts with admitting how much we have assumed."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one opinion you hold strongly. Spend five minutes honestly asking whether you might be wrong.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-21","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 1.9","q":["If what philosophers say about the kinship of God and humans is true,","what is left for us but to do as Socrates did, and when asked where we are from, never to say \"Athens\" or \"Corinth\", but \"I am a citizen of the world\"."],"c":["Epictetus teaches that all rational beings share in reason and are therefore a single community. National and city identities are real but secondary. A person who understands this will not be humiliated by exile or lowly status, because no place on earth is foreign to them."],"e":null,"p":"Treat someone today as you would a fellow citizen, even if you seem to have nothing in common.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-22","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Handbook 43","q":["Everything has two handles, one by which it can be carried and one by which it cannot.","If your brother wrongs you, do not take it by the handle \"he wrongs me\". Take it by the other: \"he is my brother, raised with me\"."],"c":["The same situation can be picked up in different ways. One handle leads to anger and a broken relationship; the other leads to patience and repair. The facts stay the same; the grip changes."],"e":null,"p":"In one conflict today, look for the other handle. What else is true about this person or situation?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-23","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 17.19","q":["Endure and refrain."],"c":["The Roman writer Aulus Gellius reports that Epictetus summed up his teaching in two words in Greek: anechou kai apechou, \"bear and forbear\". Endure what must be endured; hold back from what you should not do. Two simple words, almost impossible to fully live by."],"e":null,"p":"Today, endure one discomfort without complaint, and refrain from one thing you know you should not do.","s":"Saying reported by Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 17.19.","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-24","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Discourses 4.12","q":["When you let your attention slip for a moment, do not imagine you can get it back whenever you like.","Remember this: because of today's slip, everything that follows will be worse."],"c":["Epictetus warns against the thought \"I'll focus tomorrow\". Carelessness becomes a habit, he says, and each lapse makes the next one easier. Is there any part of life where inattention does not make things worse? The carpenter, the helmsman and the student all do worse work when they drift."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one task today and give it your full attention from start to finish.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epictetus-25","a":"epictetus"},{"ref":"Meditations 5.20","q":["What blocks the action moves the action forward.","What stands in the way becomes the way."],"c":["Marcus wrote this note to himself, in Greek, during the long wars on the Danube frontier. The full passage says that people can block his actions, but they cannot block his intentions or his disposition, because the mind can turn whatever obstructs it into material for its own work.","The obstacle is not something to get past before the real task starts. Dealing with it well is the task. Patience, fairness or courage can only be practised on the things that test them."],"e":{"when":"AD 169 · Forum of Trajan, Rome","title":"The palace auction","text":"Plague had thinned the legions, Germanic tribes had crossed the Danube, and the treasury was empty. Rather than raise new taxes on the provinces, Marcus put the palace treasures up for public sale, including gold cups, his wife's silk and jewelled robes, and statues. The auction is said to have run for two months and paid for the war. The empty treasury became the way to fund it."},"p":"When something blocks you today, ask one question before you get annoyed: what does this let me practise right now?","s":"Auction: Historia Augusta, Life of Marcus 17; Eutropius 8.13.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-01","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 2.1","q":["Say to yourself at daybreak: today I will meet people who are meddling, ungrateful, arrogant, deceitful, jealous and unsociable.","None of them can harm me, for none can drag me into what is shameful."],"c":["This is how Book 2 of the Meditations opens. Marcus prepares himself each morning for the difficult people he knows he will meet in council, at court and in the army. He goes on to say that they act this way because they do not know good from bad, and that they are still his kin, made for working together like hands, feet and eyelids.","The point is not to expect the worst out of gloom. It is to remove the surprise, so that rudeness meets a prepared mind instead of a raw nerve."],"e":{"when":"AD 171–173 · Carnuntum, on the Danube","title":"Notes from the camp","text":"Book 2 carries the heading \"Written at Carnuntum\", a legionary fortress east of modern Vienna. Marcus spent winters there directing the war against the Marcomanni and Quadi, hearing embassies, judging cases and dealing with generals and petitioners from dawn on. The difficult people of 2.1 were not abstractions; he met them every day."},"p":"Before the day starts, name the one person or situation most likely to annoy you, and decide now how you will meet it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-02","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 4.3","q":["People seek retreats for themselves in the country, by the sea, in the mountains.","But nowhere can you retreat into more quiet and freedom from trouble than into your own mind."],"c":["Wealthy Romans dreamed of villas on the Bay of Naples. Marcus, who could have had any of them, reminds himself that the only retreat that is always available costs nothing and needs no travel. A few minutes of returning to his own principles renew him, he writes, and send him back to his work without resentment.","The retreat is not escape. It is a short, deliberate pause to remember what matters, taken in the middle of a busy life rather than instead of one."],"e":null,"p":"Take two minutes today, wherever you are, to step back into your own head and remind yourself what you are actually trying to do.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-03","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 4.7","q":["Take away the opinion, and you take away the complaint \"I have been harmed\".","Take away the complaint \"I have been harmed\", and the harm is gone."],"c":["This is one of the shortest entries in the Meditations and one of the most Stoic. Marcus distinguishes between what happened and the story he tells himself about it. An insult, a setback or a loss is an event; \"I have been wronged\" is a judgment added on top.","He is not saying that nothing bad ever happens. He is saying that the part that eats at us afterwards is usually the judgment, and that part is ours to change."],"e":null,"p":"When you catch yourself replaying a slight today, separate the plain facts from the verdict you added. Drop the verdict.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-04","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 6.6","q":["The best way to take revenge is not to become like the one who wronged you."],"c":["Marcus held absolute power and had every means of revenge. In the Meditations he repeatedly warns himself against answering wrong with wrong. Responding in kind, he says, only adds a second bad act to the first, and makes him what he disliked in the other person."],"e":{"when":"AD 175 · Syria and the Danube","title":"The revolt of Avidius Cassius","text":"Avidius Cassius, governor of Syria, declared himself emperor on a false report that Marcus had died. When Cassius was killed by his own officers three months later, Marcus showed no pleasure. He is said to have regretted losing the chance to pardon him, spared his family and burned his correspondence unread, so he would not have to punish the friends who had written to him."},"p":"If someone treats you badly today, decide in advance the one thing you will not do in reply: become like them.","s":"Cassius: Cassius Dio 72.27–31; Historia Augusta, Life of Avidius Cassius.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-05","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 5.1","q":["At dawn, when you are reluctant to get up, have this thought ready: I am rising to do the work of a human being.","Why then complain, if I am going out to do what I was born for?"],"c":["The emperor of Rome admits that he did not want to get out of bed. He argues with himself: was he made to lie under the blankets and keep warm? Plants, birds, ants and bees all do their part in the order of the world. Should a human being refuse to do theirs?","Motivation is not waiting for a feeling. It is remembering what you are for."],"e":null,"p":"Tomorrow morning, before you check your phone, say one sentence to yourself about the work you are getting up to do.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-06","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 2.11","q":["You could leave life right now.","Let that decide what you do and say and think."],"c":["Marcus lived surrounded by death: the Antonine Plague, the war, the loss of most of his children. He did not use the thought of death to make himself gloomy, but to make himself honest. If this were the last day, which of today's petty grudges and postponements would still seem worth it?"],"e":{"when":"AD 165–180 · Across the empire","title":"The Antonine Plague","text":"Soldiers returning from the Parthian war brought back an epidemic, probably smallpox, that swept the empire for fifteen years. Ancient writers report that at its height in Rome around two thousand people died in a day. Marcus himself is thought by some historians to have died of it, near the Danube front in 180."},"p":"Pick one thing you have been postponing that would matter if today were your last. Do it, or at least start it, today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-07","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 4.49","q":["Be like the headland, on which the waves break continually.","It stands firm, and the seething water around it falls quiet."],"c":["Marcus goes on: \"It is my bad luck that this happened to me.\" No, he corrects himself: \"It is my good luck that, although this happened, I remain free from pain, neither crushed by the present nor afraid of the future.\" The same thing could have happened to anyone, but not everyone would have borne it so well.","The headland does not fight the waves or run from them. It simply stays what it is, and the sea calms around it."],"e":null,"p":"When pressure comes today, don't push back and don't give way. Hold your position and let it break around you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-08","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 7.8","q":["Do not let the future disturb you.","If you have to, you will meet it with the same reason that you use for the present."],"c":["Anxiety is mostly lived in advance. Marcus reminds himself that whatever comes, he will face it with the same tools he has now: judgment, courage, patience. He does not need to solve tomorrow's problems with today's worry."],"e":{"when":"AD 172 · Territory of the Quadi","title":"The rain miracle","text":"During a campaign north of the Danube, a Roman force was surrounded by the Quadi, cut off from water and suffering in the summer heat. A sudden storm broke, giving the soldiers water while lightning and hail struck the enemy. The Romans won. The event is carved on the Column of Marcus Aurelius in Rome, and pagans and Christians later argued over whose prayers had brought the rain."},"p":"Write down the one future worry that is taking up the most space. Next to it, write what you will do if it happens. Then put it away.","s":"Rain miracle: Cassius Dio 72.8–10; Column of Marcus Aurelius, Rome.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-09","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 8.47","q":["If you are distressed by anything outside you, it is not the thing that disturbs you, but your own judgment about it.","And it is in your power to wipe that judgment out now."],"c":["Marcus continues: if your own character is what troubles you, who stops you from correcting it? And if you are distressed because you are not doing something that seems right, why not do it instead of being distressed? There is a decision hidden inside most distress."],"e":{"when":"AD 169 · Rome","title":"The death of Annius Verus","text":"Marcus' seven-year-old son Annius Verus died after surgery for a tumour behind his ear. According to his biography, Marcus mourned him for only five days, comforted the doctors, and then returned to public business, letting the games in honour of Jupiter go on so the public festival would not be interrupted."},"p":"When something weighs on you today, ask: is this the event, or my verdict on it? And is there a decision hiding inside this distress?","s":"Annius Verus: Historia Augusta, Life of Marcus 21.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-10","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 10.16","q":["Stop talking about what a good person is like.","Be one."],"c":["This is perhaps the shortest instruction in the Meditations. Marcus spent his life reading and discussing philosophy, and he knew the danger: talking about virtue can feel like practising it. By the time he wrote this he had little patience with theory that did not change behaviour."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one thing you believe a good person would do today, and do it without telling anyone.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-11","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 6.30","q":["See that you are not turned into a Caesar, that you are not dyed in the purple. It happens.","Keep yourself simple, good, pure, serious, unpretentious, a friend of justice, reverent, kind, affectionate and strong for your proper work."],"c":["Marcus knew that absolute power changes people. The phrase \"dyed in the purple\" refers to the imperial colour: he feared becoming the role instead of a person playing it. In the same entry he holds up his adoptive father Antoninus Pius as the model: patient, fair, steady, never in a hurry, never vain about small things."],"e":{"when":"AD 161 · Rome","title":"Two emperors","text":"When Antoninus Pius died, the Senate offered the throne to Marcus alone. He refused to accept unless his adoptive brother Lucius Verus was made co-emperor with equal rank. For the first time, Rome had two emperors at once. Marcus could have kept all the power; he chose to share it."},"p":"Notice one moment today when your title, role or status is doing the talking. Step out of it and speak as yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-12","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 4.17","q":["Do not act as if you had ten thousand years to live.","Death hangs over you. While you live, while you can, be good."],"c":["The Stoics did not dwell on death to frighten themselves but to wake up. Time is the one resource that cannot be replaced. Marcus reminds himself that \"later\" is not guaranteed, so the good he intends to do should not be put off."],"e":{"when":"AD 169 · Altinum, northern Italy","title":"Death of Lucius Verus","text":"Returning from the northern front, Lucius Verus, Marcus' co-emperor and adoptive brother, suffered a stroke in the carriage they shared and died a few days later, aged thirty-nine. Overnight Marcus became sole ruler of an empire at war and in the grip of plague."},"p":"Is there something kind, honest or brave you have been saving for later? Do it today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-13","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 3.10","q":["Each of us lives only in the present, this brief moment.","All the rest has already been lived, or is uncertain."],"c":["Marcus reminds himself how small the present moment is, how small the corner of the earth where he lives, and how short even the longest fame. That might sound depressing, but his conclusion is freeing: you cannot lose the past or the future, because you do not have them. You only ever have now."],"e":null,"p":"Several times today, when your mind drifts to yesterday or tomorrow, bring it back with one word: \"now\".","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-14","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 7.59","q":["Dig within. Within is the spring of good,","and it can always bubble up, if you keep digging."],"c":["The Stoics believed that everything needed for a good life is already present in a rational mind. It does not have to be bought, borrowed or approved by others. But it has to be worked at, like a well that silts up if left alone."],"e":{"when":"AD 176 · Athens","title":"Chairs of philosophy","text":"Returning from the East, Marcus visited Athens, was initiated into the Eleusinian Mysteries, and founded four state-paid professorships in philosophy, one each for the Platonists, Aristotelians, Stoics and Epicureans. Even rival schools received support: he believed people should have access to the springs of wisdom."},"p":"Spend ten quiet minutes today with a question that matters to you, without input from anyone else.","s":"Chairs: Cassius Dio 72.31; Philostratus, Lives of the Sophists 2.2.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-15","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 5.16","q":["Your mind will take on the character of what you often think about,","for the soul is dyed by its thoughts."],"c":["Marcus uses the image of cloth soaking in dye. Whatever we return to again and again, resentment, envy, worry or gratitude, slowly colours the whole mind. He continues: dye it, then, with thoughts like these: wherever it is possible to live, it is possible to live well."],"e":null,"p":"Notice what your mind returns to most often today. If you don't like the colour, choose a different thought to soak it in.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-16","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 9.6","q":["Your present judgment, grasping what is real;","your present action, done for the common good;","your present attitude, content with whatever comes from outside you. That is enough."],"c":["This short entry sums up the whole Stoic programme in three lines: see clearly, act well for others, accept what is not in your control. Marcus calls it \"enough\". Nothing else is required for a good life in this moment."],"e":null,"p":"At three points today, check all three: am I seeing this clearly, acting for the good of others, and accepting what I can't change?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-17","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 6.21","q":["If anyone can show me that I am wrong in thought or deed, I will gladly change.","I seek the truth, and no one was ever harmed by the truth."],"c":["For someone with absolute power, admitting error is rare and costly. Marcus makes it a principle: being corrected is a gain, not a defeat. What does harm us, he adds, is staying in our own ignorance and self-deception."],"e":{"when":"AD c.176 · Rome","title":"The emperor goes to school","text":"According to later writers, Marcus as an older man still attended the lectures of the philosopher Sextus of Chaeronea. When someone asked where he was going, he answered that it is good even for an old man to learn, and he was going to Sextus to learn what he did not yet know."},"p":"Ask someone today where you might be wrong about something you care about. Listen to the answer without defending yourself.","s":"Sextus: Philostratus, Lives of the Sophists 2.1.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-18","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 6.48","q":["When you want to cheer yourself up, think of the good qualities of the people around you:","one person's energy, another's modesty, another's generosity."],"c":["In Book 1 Marcus did exactly this: he listed what he had learned from each of his family members, teachers and friends. Here he recommends it as daily practice. Few things lift the mind so reliably as seeing the virtues of the people we live with."],"e":null,"p":"Name three people you will see today and one good quality in each. Tell at least one of them.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-19","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 12.4","q":["I have often wondered how it is that everyone loves themselves more than anyone else,","yet values their own opinion of themselves less than the opinion of others."],"c":["Marcus notes the paradox: we would never let anyone read our thoughts for even a day, yet we care desperately what others think of us. If we really respected ourselves, we would care first about our own judgment of our actions."],"e":null,"p":"Before you decide something today, ask what you would think of it, not what others will think.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-20","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 7.69","q":["The perfection of character is this:","to live each day as if it were your last, without frenzy, without sluggishness, without pretence."],"c":["Living as if each day were the last is often misread as a call to excess. Marcus means the opposite: no frantic rushing, no dull drifting, no acting a part. Just doing the day's work well and honestly, because there may not be another chance."],"e":{"when":"AD 180 · Near the Danube","title":"The last days","text":"When Marcus fell gravely ill, he called his friends and spoke to them calmly, more concerned about the empire than himself. According to his biography, when they wept he said: \"Why weep for me, and not rather think about the plague and the death of so many others?\" He died on 17 March 180."},"p":"Pick one task today and do it the way you would if you knew it was the last time you would do it.","s":"Last days: Historia Augusta, Life of Marcus 28.","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-21","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 9.42","q":["When you are offended by someone's shamelessness, ask yourself at once:","is it possible for there to be no shameless people in the world? It is not."],"c":["So do not demand the impossible, Marcus continues. This person is one of the shameless people who must exist in the world. The same applies to the dishonest and the faithless. Expecting the world to be without them is the real mistake.","He also reminds himself that nature has given us a virtue for each fault in others: gentleness against rudeness, for example."],"e":null,"p":"When someone behaves badly today, tell yourself: of course such people exist. Then ask which virtue this situation is asking of you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-22","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 8.36","q":["Do not let the picture of your whole life overwhelm you.","Do not try to take in all the troubles that may come. Ask of each present one: what is unbearable here?"],"c":["A whole life of difficulty, imagined all at once, can crush anyone. Taken moment by moment, almost everything is bearable. Marcus reminds himself that neither past nor future weighs on him, only the present, and the present gets smaller the more closely you look at it."],"e":null,"p":"If a big project or problem feels too heavy today, ask only: what is the next step, and can I bear that one?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-23","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 6.13","q":["When meat is in front of you, think: this is the dead body of a fish, a bird, a pig.","And this purple robe is sheep's wool dyed with the blood of a shellfish."],"c":["Marcus practised describing things in plain physical terms to strip away their glamour. Fine wine is fermented grape juice; the imperial purple is wool and shellfish dye. Seeing things as they are, he says, cuts through the stories that make us crave or fear them."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one thing you crave or are impressed by today and describe it to yourself in the plainest possible words.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-24","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Meditations 11.18","q":["Kindness is invincible, if it is genuine and not a fake smile or an act.","What can the most aggressive person do to you if you remain kind to them?"],"c":["This entry is part of a list of nine things Marcus reminds himself of when someone has offended him. Real kindness, he argues, disarms people. It is not weakness, because it does not depend on the other person's response."],"e":null,"p":"Respond to one difficult person today with genuine kindness, and notice what it does to the conversation.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-marcus-aurelius-25","a":"marcus-aurelius"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 38a","q":["The unexamined life","is not worth living for a human being."],"c":["Socrates said this at his trial, after being found guilty. He had been asked to propose a penalty, and someone suggested he could go into exile and keep quiet. He refused. To stop questioning himself and others, he said, would be to stop living as a human being at all."],"e":{"when":"399 BC · The People's Court, Athens","title":"The trial of Socrates","text":"Socrates, then seventy, was accused by Meletus, Anytus and Lycon of not recognising the city's gods, introducing new ones and corrupting the young. A jury of about five hundred citizens heard the case in a single day. He was found guilty by a margin of around sixty votes and sentenced to death."},"p":"Set aside ten minutes today to examine one thing you do automatically. Why do you do it?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-01","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 21d","q":["I seem to be wiser than this man in just this small way:","that what I do not know, I do not think I know."],"c":["Socrates explains how he came to question people. After the oracle said no one was wiser than he, he went to a politician with a reputation for wisdom and found that the man knew very little, but thought he knew a great deal. Socrates did not know either, but at least he was aware of it."],"e":{"when":"c. 430 BC · Delphi","title":"The oracle","text":"Socrates' friend Chaerephon, an impulsive man, went to the oracle of Apollo at Delphi and asked whether anyone was wiser than Socrates. The priestess answered: no one. Socrates, puzzled, set out to prove the oracle wrong and ended by understanding what it meant."},"p":"In one conversation today, say \"I don't know\" when you don't. Notice how it opens the conversation.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-02","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 30b","q":["Virtue does not come from money,","but from virtue come money and all other good things for human beings, both private and public."],"c":["Socrates tells the jury what he has been doing for years: going around Athens urging young and old not to care for their bodies or money before caring for the state of their souls. Character comes first; everything else depends on it."],"e":null,"p":"Before one decision today, ask which option makes you a better person, not only a richer or more comfortable one.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-03","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 41d","q":["No evil can happen to a good person,","either in life or after death."],"c":["These words come from Socrates' final speech to the jurors who had voted for his acquittal. He tells them to be of good hope about death. Whatever happens to the body, the good person's character cannot be harmed by others. That is the only thing truly theirs."],"e":null,"p":"When you fear losing something today, ask: can this loss make me worse, or only make things harder?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-04","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 29d","q":["Men of Athens, I respect and love you, but I will obey the god rather than you.","As long as I draw breath and am able, I will never stop doing philosophy."],"c":["Socrates imagines the jury offering to let him go if he promises to stop questioning people. He refuses. He sees his questioning as a duty given to him, and he would rather die than abandon it."],"e":null,"p":"Name one thing you would not give up even under pressure. Live by it today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-05","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 30e","q":["The god has attached me to the city like a gadfly to a large, well-bred horse","that is sluggish because of its size and needs to be stirred up."],"c":["Socrates compares himself to an insect that stings a lazy horse. Athens is great but complacent; he is the irritating presence that keeps it awake. If they kill him, he warns, they will not easily find another."],"e":null,"p":"Is there someone whose criticism annoys you but keeps you sharp? Thank them, at least in your mind.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-06","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 42a","q":["Now it is time to go: I to die, and you to live.","Which of us goes to the better thing is unclear to everyone except the god."],"c":["These are the last words of Socrates' speech at his trial. He ends without bitterness, with the calm of someone who has lived by his principles and accepts the consequences."],"e":null,"p":"End your day today without bitterness. Whatever happened, let it go before you sleep.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-07","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Crito 49b","q":["We must never do wrong,","nor, when wronged, do wrong in return, as most people think."],"c":["In prison, his friend Crito urges Socrates to escape, since he was condemned unjustly. Socrates refuses. Escaping would mean breaking the laws, and doing wrong in return for wrong is still doing wrong. This was a radical idea in a culture where taking revenge was considered honourable."],"e":{"when":"399 BC · Prison, Athens","title":"The escape plan","text":"Because a sacred ship had gone to Delos and no executions could take place until it returned, Socrates spent about a month in prison. His friends prepared an escape, with money ready to bribe the guards and a safe home in Thessaly. Socrates refused to leave."},"p":"If someone wrongs you today, refuse to wrong them back, in deed or in words.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-08","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Crito 48b","q":["What we should value most","is not living, but living well."],"c":["Socrates reminds Crito of a principle they had agreed on many times before. The length of a life is not what matters most; its quality is. A short life lived well is better than a long life lived badly."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that makes this day well lived, not just lived.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-09","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Phaedo 118a","q":["Crito, we owe a cock to Asclepius.","Pay it, and do not forget."],"c":["These were Socrates' last words, spoken after he had drunk the hemlock. Asclepius was the god of healing, and a cock was offered to him by those who had recovered from illness. Some readers think Socrates meant that death was a cure for the illness of life; others that it was a simple act of piety. Either way, he died thinking of a debt to be paid."],"e":{"when":"399 BC · Prison, Athens","title":"The death of Socrates","text":"On his last day, Socrates spent hours discussing the immortality of the soul with his friends. He bathed, said goodbye to his family, and drank the hemlock calmly. When his friends wept, he scolded them gently: he had sent the women away to avoid such scenes. He walked about until his legs felt heavy, then lay down."},"p":"Is there a small debt or promise you have forgotten? Settle it today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-10","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Phaedo 67e","q":["Those who practise philosophy rightly","are practising for dying."],"c":["On his last day, Socrates explains to his friends why he is not afraid. Philosophy, he says, trains the mind to free itself from the demands of the body. A life spent loosening those bonds is a preparation for death, so the philosopher should not fear it when it comes."],"e":null,"p":"Loosen your grip on one bodily comfort today, and notice that you remain yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-11","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Gorgias 469c","q":["If I had to choose between doing wrong and suffering wrong,","I would choose to suffer it rather than to do it."],"c":["In the Gorgias, the young Polus is astonished: surely the tyrant who can do whatever he likes is happy? Socrates replies that doing injustice is the greatest harm, because it damages the soul. Being wronged only damages what is outside us."],"e":{"when":"406 BC · The Assembly, Athens","title":"The trial of the generals","text":"After the naval victory at Arginusae, a storm prevented the rescue of shipwrecked sailors, and the Assembly wanted to try the eight commanders together and illegally. Socrates, by lot presiding officer that day, refused to put the motion to a vote, despite threats of arrest. The generals were condemned anyway, and the Athenians later regretted it."},"p":"If you face a choice today between doing something wrong and accepting a loss, take the loss.","s":"Arginusae: Plato, Apology 32b; Xenophon, Hellenica 1.7.","t":"","id":"q-socrates-12","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Theaetetus 155d","q":["This feeling of wonder is the mark of a philosopher.","Philosophy has no other beginning."],"c":["Socrates says this to the young mathematician Theaetetus, who has confessed that some puzzles make him dizzy. Socrates is delighted. Being puzzled, he says, is not a sign of failure but the start of all real thinking."],"e":null,"p":"Let something puzzle you today. Instead of looking up the answer straight away, wonder about it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-13","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Phaedrus 279b","q":["Dear Pan and all you other gods of this place, grant that I may become beautiful within,","and that all I have outside may be in harmony with what is inside."],"c":["At the end of the Phaedrus, Socrates offers this prayer by a river outside Athens. He asks not for wealth or success but for inner beauty, and for his outer life to match it. He goes on to ask that he may consider the wise person rich, and have only as much gold as a self-controlled person could carry."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself today where your outer life and inner values do not match. Bring one small thing into harmony.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-14","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Xenophon, Memorabilia 1.6.10","q":["To need nothing is divine;","to need as little as possible is nearest to the divine."],"c":["Socrates said this to the sophist Antiphon, who mocked him for his poor clothes, bare feet and simple food. Antiphon thought happiness lay in luxury. Socrates replied that the fewer needs we have, the freer we are, and the closer we come to the gods, who need nothing."],"e":null,"p":"Do without one thing today that you usually think you need.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-15","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 2.25","q":["How many things I can do without!"],"c":["Socrates often walked through the Athenian market, where all kinds of goods were displayed for sale. Instead of wishing for them, he would say this to himself. The market became a lesson in freedom rather than desire."],"e":null,"p":"Next time you shop or scroll through things to buy, say to yourself: \"How many things I can do without.\"","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-16","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 28e","q":["Wherever someone has taken up their post, believing it best, or been placed there by their commander,","there, I think, they must remain and face the danger, without counting the cost in death or anything else, rather than disgrace."],"c":["Socrates reminds the jury that he served as a soldier and held his post when ordered. He will hold the post his conscience has given him in the same way, even at the cost of his life."],"e":{"when":"432 and 424 BC · Potidaea and Delium","title":"Socrates the soldier","text":"At the siege of Potidaea, Socrates saved the wounded Alcibiades and gave the prize for bravery to him. In the retreat after the Athenian defeat at Delium, he walked calmly through the chaos, looking around so steadily that no enemy dared to attack him or those near him. Alcibiades later told both stories with admiration."},"p":"Where is your post today, at work, at home, among friends? Hold it well, even if it gets uncomfortable.","s":"Military stories: Plato, Symposium 219e–221c.","t":"","id":"q-socrates-17","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Plato, Apology 32d","q":["I showed, not in words but in deeds, that I did not care about death in the least.","My whole concern was to do nothing unjust or unholy."],"c":["Socrates describes how he refused an order from the Thirty, the oligarchs who seized power in Athens. He did not argue; he simply went home. He would probably have been killed if the regime had not fallen soon afterwards."],"e":{"when":"404 BC · Athens","title":"The arrest of Leon of Salamis","text":"The Thirty Tyrants tried to implicate as many citizens as possible in their crimes. They ordered Socrates and four other men to arrest Leon of Salamis, an innocent man, so he could be executed. The other four went. Socrates went home. Leon was killed anyway."},"p":"If you are asked today to go along with something wrong, quietly do not.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-18","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Cicero, On Ends 2.90","q":["Hunger is the best sauce."],"c":["Cicero reports that Socrates was once seen walking hard until evening. When asked why, he said he was building up an appetite for dinner. Simple food tastes best when we are truly hungry; the best pleasures often come from need, not from luxury."],"e":null,"p":"Let yourself get properly hungry before your next meal, and notice how good simple food tastes.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-19","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Xenophon, Symposium 2.10","q":["People who want to become good horsemen choose the most spirited horses.","I wanted to live among people, so I married her, knowing that if I could get along with her, I could get along with anyone."],"c":["At a dinner party, a friend asked Socrates why he had married Xanthippe, known as the most difficult woman in Athens. Socrates answered with this joke, comparing marriage to training. It is unfair to Xanthippe, but it shows Socrates' view of difficulty: a hard companion is a teacher."],"e":null,"p":"With one difficult person today, think of yourself as in training. What are they teaching you?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-socrates-20","a":"socrates"},{"ref":"Republic 7.515c","q":["Such people would believe","that the truth is nothing but the shadows."],"c":["In the allegory of the cave, Plato imagines prisoners chained since birth, facing a wall. Behind them a fire throws the shadows of objects on that wall, and the prisoners take those shadows for reality. When one of them is freed and climbs into the sunlight, he is at first blinded and then begins to see things as they are. If he goes back to tell the others, they laugh at him, and might even kill him."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself what \"shadows\" you might be mistaking for reality today: a headline, a first impression, a rumour.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-01","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 5.473c","q":["Until philosophers rule as kings, or those who are now kings and rulers genuinely philosophise,","there will be no end to the troubles of cities, nor, I think, of the human race."],"c":["Plato puts this into Socrates' mouth as the most shocking idea in the Republic. He knew how absurd it would sound, and has Socrates expect a wave of laughter. But he meant it: power without wisdom brings ruin, and wisdom without power changes nothing."],"e":{"when":"367 BC · Syracuse, Sicily","title":"The philosopher and the tyrant","text":"After the death of Dionysius I, Plato's friend Dion invited him to Syracuse to educate the new ruler, Dionysius II, as a philosopher-king. It went badly: court intrigue grew, Dion was exiled, and Plato was kept in Syracuse almost as a prisoner. He tried again in 361 BC, with no better result."},"p":"If you lead anything today, a team, a family, a project, ask what a wise ruler, not a powerful one, would do.","s":"Syracuse: Plato, Seventh Letter; Plutarch, Life of Dion.","t":"","id":"q-plato-02","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 4.443d","q":["The just person sets their own house in order, is master of themselves,","brings themselves into order and becomes their own friend."],"c":["For Plato, justice is not first of all about laws and courts, but about the inner order of the soul. When reason leads, courage supports it, and desire follows, a person is at peace with themselves. Only someone who is just within can act justly in the world."],"e":null,"p":"Bring one corner of your life into order today: a drawer, an inbox, a promise. Notice how it helps the rest.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-03","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 1.347c","q":["The greatest penalty for refusing to rule","is to be ruled by someone worse."],"c":["Socrates argues that good people do not want power for money or honour. Why then would they ever take on the burden? Because if they refuse, people worse than them will rule instead. Responsibility sometimes means accepting a job you would rather avoid."],"e":null,"p":"Is there a responsibility you have been avoiding because it is unpleasant? Consider who will take it if you don't.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-04","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 2.377a","q":["The beginning is the most important part of any work,","especially with anything young and tender."],"c":["Plato argues that the stories children hear shape their character for life. What we take in early becomes part of us. The same is true of any project: the first steps set its direction."],"e":null,"p":"Start one task today with special care. Get the beginning right.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-05","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 7.536e","q":["Nothing taught by force stays in the mind.","So do not train children by force, but by play."],"c":["Plato argues that learning forced on people does not last. Bodily exercises done under compulsion do the body no harm, but forced learning does not take root in the soul. Let children learn through play, and you will also discover what each is naturally suited for."],"e":{"when":"c. 387 BC · Athens","title":"The founding of the Academy","text":"Plato bought land near the grove of the hero Akademos, about a mile outside the city walls, and began to teach there. Later tradition says the entrance bore the words \"Let no one ignorant of geometry enter\". The school taught mathematics, astronomy and philosophy for nearly three centuries, and Aristotle studied there for twenty years."},"p":"Learn something today because you are curious, not because you must. Notice how much more stays.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-06","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Laws 1.626e","q":["To conquer oneself is the first and best of all victories.","To be defeated by oneself is the most shameful and the worst of all defeats."],"c":["The Laws is Plato's last and longest work, a conversation between three old men walking on Crete. At the very start the Athenian stranger suggests that the real battle is not between cities, but within each person."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one small battle with yourself today and win it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-07","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Symposium 211d","q":["If there is any point in life worth living, it is here:","in the contemplation of beauty itself."],"c":["In the Symposium, Socrates repeats what he learned from the wise woman Diotima. Love leads us up a ladder: from one beautiful body to all beautiful bodies, then to beautiful souls, laws, knowledge, and finally to beauty itself. Every love we feel is a step toward something higher."],"e":{"when":"416 BC · Athens","title":"Agathon's victory party","text":"The Symposium is set at a drinking party held by the tragic poet Agathon to celebrate his first victory in the drama competition. The guests, including Aristophanes and Socrates, agree to drink moderately and take turns giving speeches in praise of love. The party ends when the drunken Alcibiades bursts in."},"p":"Notice something beautiful today, and let it lead your thoughts to something higher.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-08","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Phaedrus 246a","q":["Let the soul be likened to the combined power of a winged pair of horses and their charioteer."],"c":["Plato's image: reason is a charioteer driving two horses. One is noble and obedient; the other is wild and pulls toward pleasure. A good life is not one without desire, but one where reason holds the reins and keeps both horses moving together."],"e":null,"p":"When a strong desire pulls at you today, picture your hands on the reins. Steer, don't let it drag you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-09","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 3.401d","q":["Rhythm and harmony sink deepest into the soul","and take hold of it most powerfully."],"c":["Plato thought music shaped character more than any other part of education. What we listen to over and over influences how we feel and think. Good music brings grace to the soul; harsh or careless music brings the opposite."],"e":null,"p":"Choose deliberately what you listen to today, and notice how it shapes your mood.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-10","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Laws 7.803c","q":["A human being has been made as a kind of plaything of God, and this is truly the best thing about us.","So every man and woman should spend life playing the finest games."],"c":["The old Athenian in the Laws suggests that human affairs are not worth extreme seriousness. Only God is truly serious. We should live with a kind of lightness, devoting ourselves to the best activities, worship, song and dance, and the education of the young, as if they were games."],"e":null,"p":"Do one of your duties today with a lighter heart, as if it were a game worth playing well.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-11","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Seventh Letter 341c","q":["It cannot be put into words like other subjects,","but after long living together with the thing itself, it is born in the soul suddenly, like a light kindled from a leaping spark."],"c":["In this letter, written late in life, Plato explains why he never wrote a book setting out his deepest views. Understanding of the most important things cannot be handed over in writing. It comes through long conversation and shared life, suddenly, like a spark."],"e":null,"p":"Spend time today in real conversation with someone about something that matters, without hurrying to a conclusion.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-12","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Theaetetus 176b","q":["We should try to escape from here to there as quickly as we can.","And escape means becoming as like God as possible; and to become like God is to become just and holy, with wisdom."],"c":["Socrates explains to the geometer Theodorus why evil can never disappear completely from the world. The answer is not to run away from life, but to become more just, holy and wise within it. That is the only real escape."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one way to be a little more just today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-13","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 9.592b","q":["Perhaps there is a pattern of it laid up in heaven for anyone who wishes to see it,","and, seeing it, to found a city within themselves."],"c":["At the end of the Republic's long description of the ideal city, Glaucon objects that such a city exists nowhere on earth. Socrates agrees, but says it does not matter. The pattern can still guide anyone who wishes to live by it, building the ideal city within their own soul."],"e":null,"p":"Think of one ideal you hold that the world doesn't live up to. Live by it yourself today anyway.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-14","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Laws 5.731e","q":["The greatest of all evils is one that most people have from birth:","excessive love of oneself."],"c":["The Athenian stranger warns that self-love blinds us. We judge our own actions too generously and others too harshly. The person who loves themselves too much cannot see the just, the good and the beautiful clearly. True self-respect means following what is better, not simply what is ours."],"e":null,"p":"When you judge someone today, imagine judging yourself for the same thing with the same strictness.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-15","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Phaedrus 275a","q":["This invention will produce forgetfulness in the minds of those who learn it,","because they will not use their memory."],"c":["Socrates tells an Egyptian story. The god Theuth invents writing and offers it to King Thamus as a remedy for memory. The king answers that writing will do the opposite: people will rely on written signs instead of their own minds and appear wise without being so. The same debate surrounds every new technology since."],"e":null,"p":"Remember one thing today without writing it down or looking it up.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-16","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 2.369b","q":["A city comes into being because none of us is self-sufficient,","and each of us needs many things."],"c":["Plato starts his search for justice by imagining how a city begins. No one can be farmer, builder, weaver and shoemaker all at once. We need one another. Human society is born from mutual need and mutual help."],"e":null,"p":"Thank one person today whose work you depend on but rarely notice.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-17","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Laches 192c","q":["Courage seems to be a kind of endurance of the soul.","But not every endurance: only endurance joined with wisdom."],"c":["In the Laches, Socrates and two Athenian generals try to define courage. The general Laches suggests endurance. Socrates points out that stubborn endurance in a foolish cause is not courage. True courage knows what it is enduring for."],"e":null,"p":"Before you push through something difficult today, ask: is this worth enduring?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-18","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Euthyphro 10a","q":["Is the holy loved by the gods because it is holy,","or is it holy because it is loved by the gods?"],"c":["Socrates meets the confident priest Euthyphro on the way to court, where Euthyphro is about to prosecute his own father. He asks him what holiness is. This famous question asks whether things are good because an authority says so, or whether the authority approves them because they are good."],"e":null,"p":"Ask of one rule you follow today: is it right because it is the rule, or is it the rule because it is right?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-19","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 1.329c","q":["Most gladly have I escaped from it,","as if from a raging and savage master."],"c":["Old Cephalus quotes the poet Sophocles, who was asked whether he still enjoyed the pleasures of love. Sophocles answered that he was glad to be free of them. Cephalus adds that old age brings great peace and freedom from such passions, for those with a calm and contented character."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one craving that does not have the hold on you it used to. Be grateful for the freedom.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-20","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Symposium 215b","q":["He is just like those statues of Silenus in the sculptors' shops:","when you open them up, they have images of the gods inside."],"c":["The drunken Alcibiades compares Socrates to small statues of the satyr Silenus, ugly on the outside, which opened up to reveal figures of gods. Socrates looked comical, Alcibiades says, but inside he was full of wisdom and beauty."],"e":null,"p":"Look past someone's appearance today. What might be inside?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-21","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 10.617e","q":["Responsibility lies with the one who chooses.","God is not to blame."],"c":["At the end of the Republic, Plato tells the myth of Er, a soldier who returned from the dead. He saw souls choosing their next lives from a set of lots. Some chose wisely, others greedily, and later regretted it. This is the message spoken to them before they choose."],"e":null,"p":"When you make a choice today, own it fully. No blaming fate, others or circumstances.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-22","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Seventh Letter 324d","q":["I saw that these men in a short time made the former government look like a golden age."],"c":["In the Seventh Letter, Plato explains why he turned away from politics as a young man. His own relatives were among the Thirty Tyrants who seized Athens in 404 BC. Their reign of terror, and then the execution of Socrates by the restored democracy, convinced him that no city could be well governed until philosophy and power were joined."],"e":{"when":"404–403 BC · Athens","title":"The Thirty Tyrants","text":"After Athens lost the Peloponnesian War, a pro-Spartan oligarchy of thirty men took power, among them Plato's cousin Critias and his uncle Charmides. They executed around 1,500 people and seized property before being overthrown within a year. Plato, then in his early twenties, watched it all."},"p":"Look at a situation you once thought was bad. Did what replaced it turn out better or worse? What does that teach?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-23","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Republic 2.360c","q":["No one is just willingly, but only under compulsion.","Whoever could do wrong without being seen would do it."],"c":["Glaucon, Plato's brother, challenges Socrates with the story of the ring of Gyges. A shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible, and uses it to seduce the queen, kill the king and take the throne. Wouldn't anyone do the same? The rest of the Republic is Plato's answer: justice is worth having for its own sake, even if no one ever sees it."],"e":null,"p":"Do one right thing today that no one will ever know about.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-24","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Meno 80a","q":["You are like the flat torpedo fish of the sea:","whoever touches it is numbed, and you have done the same to me."],"c":["Meno, a confident young aristocrat, complains that Socrates' questions have left him unable to say what virtue is, though he had given many speeches on it before. Socrates answers that he numbs others only because he is numb himself. Confusion is uncomfortable, but it is the beginning of real inquiry."],"e":null,"p":"When something you thought you understood stops making sense today, stay with the confusion for a while.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plato-25","a":"plato"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 1.7, 1098a","q":["One swallow does not make a spring, nor does one fine day.","In the same way, one day or a short time does not make a person blessed and happy."],"c":["Aristotle defines happiness as the activity of the soul in accordance with virtue, and then adds: \"in a complete life\". A single good deed or a lucky week is not enough. Flourishing is something that shows itself over the whole course of a life."],"e":null,"p":"Do something good today, knowing it doesn't make a summer, but that every summer is made of such days.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-01","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 2.1, 1103b","q":["We become just by doing just acts,","self-controlled by doing self-controlled acts, and brave by doing brave acts."],"c":["Virtue, for Aristotle, is not something we are born with or learn from books. It is like a skill. Builders become builders by building, and harpists by playing the harp. In the same way, character is built by repeated action, until doing the right thing becomes natural."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one virtue you want to grow. Perform one act of it today, however small.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-02","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 2.6, 1106b","q":["Virtue is a mean between two vices,","one of excess and one of deficiency."],"c":["Courage lies between cowardice and recklessness; generosity between stinginess and extravagance; proper pride between self-deprecation and vanity. The mean is not a mathematical midpoint but what is right for this person, in this situation. Hitting it is hard, Aristotle admits: there are many ways to miss a target, only one way to hit it."],"e":null,"p":"In one situation today, ask: what would too much look like here, what would too little look like, and where is the right point?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-03","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 2.9, 1109a","q":["Anyone can get angry, that is easy.","But to be angry with the right person, to the right degree, at the right time, for the right reason and in the right way, that is not easy."],"c":["Aristotle did not think anger was always wrong. Someone who is never angry, even at injustice, has a fault too. The difficult skill is to feel the right emotions in the right measure. That is why, he says, goodness is rare, praiseworthy and fine."],"e":null,"p":"If you get angry today, check it against the five tests: the right person, degree, time, reason and way.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-04","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 1.6, 1096a","q":["It would seem better, indeed necessary, to give up even what is dear to us for the sake of the truth.","Both are dear, but piety requires us to honour the truth above our friends."],"c":["Aristotle writes this as he begins to criticise Plato's theory of the Forms. Plato was his teacher for twenty years and remained dear to him. But friendship cannot outrank truth. Later this became the saying \"Plato is dear, but truth is dearer\"."],"e":{"when":"347 BC · Athens","title":"Plato's death","text":"When Plato died, leadership of the Academy passed to his nephew Speusippus rather than to Aristotle, then thirty-seven. Aristotle left Athens soon after, for the court of Hermias of Atarneus at Assos, where he began twelve years of travel, research and teaching before returning to found his own school."},"p":"Disagree respectfully today with someone you admire, if the truth requires it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-05","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 8.1, 1155a","q":["No one would choose to live without friends,","even if they had all other good things."],"c":["Aristotle devotes two of the ten books of his Ethics to friendship, more than to any other single topic. The rich and powerful need friends most of all, he says, for what is the use of prosperity without the chance to do good, and to whom can they do good if not to friends?"],"e":{"when":"341 BC · Atarneus, Asia Minor","title":"The death of Hermias","text":"Hermias, ruler of Atarneus and Assos, had welcomed Aristotle and given him his niece Pythias in marriage. The Persians captured Hermias by treachery, and put him to death. Aristotle wrote a hymn in his memory and set up a statue to him at Delphi, honouring a friendship that cost Hermias his life."},"p":"Do something today that a real friend would do, without waiting to be asked.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-06","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 8.3, 1156b","q":["The wish for friendship comes quickly,","but friendship does not. As the saying goes, people cannot know each other until they have eaten salt together."],"c":["Aristotle distinguishes between friendships based on usefulness, on pleasure and on character. Only the last kind lasts, and it takes time. You need to have shared many meals, the proverbial \"measure of salt\", to really know and trust another person."],"e":null,"p":"Spend unhurried time today with someone you want to know better.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-07","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 10.7, 1177b","q":["We must not follow those who advise us, being human, to think only of human things, and being mortal, of mortal things.","We must, as far as we can, make ourselves immortal, and strain every nerve to live by the best thing in us."],"c":["At the end of the Ethics, Aristotle argues that the highest happiness lies in contemplation, the activity of the mind seeking truth. It is the most divine part of us. Even if small in bulk, he says, it surpasses everything else in power and worth."],"e":null,"p":"Spend some time today thinking about something larger than your daily concerns.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-08","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 1.3, 1094b","q":["It is the mark of an educated mind to look for precision in each kind of thing","only so far as the nature of the subject allows."],"c":["Aristotle warns at the start of his Ethics that questions about how to live cannot be answered with mathematical precision. To demand exact proofs from an orator, or to accept merely probable arguments from a mathematician, would be equally foolish. Good judgment knows what kind of certainty each question can give."],"e":null,"p":"When facing a question today, ask what kind of answer it can actually have: exact, probable or a matter of judgment.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-09","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Politics 1.2, 1253a","q":["The human being is by nature a political animal.","Whoever cannot live in a community, or has no need to because they are self-sufficient, is either a beast or a god."],"c":["For Aristotle, people are not isolated individuals who decide to form societies. We are made for community. We have speech, which allows us to discuss what is just and unjust, good and bad. The city exists not just so we can live, but so we can live well."],"e":{"when":"343 BC · Mieza, Macedonia","title":"The tutor of Alexander","text":"King Philip II invited Aristotle to teach his thirteen-year-old son, Alexander. Lessons took place at the sanctuary of the Nymphs at Mieza, away from the court. Aristotle taught him politics, ethics, medicine and poetry; Alexander is said to have later carried an Iliad annotated by his teacher on his campaigns."},"p":"Take part in your community today in some small way: a conversation, a vote, a helping hand.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-10","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Metaphysics 1.1, 980a","q":["All human beings by nature desire to know."],"c":["These are the opening words of the Metaphysics. Aristotle notes that we enjoy our senses even when they are not useful, and above all sight, because it shows us the differences between things. Curiosity is not a luxury but part of what we are."],"e":{"when":"335 BC · Athens","title":"The founding of the Lyceum","text":"Back in Athens, Aristotle began teaching in the Lyceum, a gymnasium dedicated to Apollo. He taught while walking in its covered walkways, so his students were called Peripatetics, the walkers. The school collected books, specimens and records of 158 Greek constitutions, an early model of organised research."},"p":"Follow one question you are curious about today just for the pleasure of knowing.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-11","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Rhetoric 1.2, 1356a","q":["Character may almost be called","the most effective means of persuasion."],"c":["Aristotle describes three means of persuasion: logical argument, appealing to the listener's emotions, and the character of the speaker. Of these, he says, the most powerful is often character. We believe good people more readily, and on doubtful matters completely."],"e":null,"p":"Before trying to convince anyone today, ask whether your actions have earned their trust.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-12","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Poetics 9, 1451b","q":["Poetry is more philosophical and more serious than history,","for poetry speaks of the universal, history of the particular."],"c":["History tells us what Alcibiades did and suffered. Poetry shows what kind of person would do or say what kind of thing, by probability or necessity. By telling us about types and patterns, stories teach us about human life in general."],"e":null,"p":"Read or listen to one story today and ask what it teaches about people in general.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-13","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 3.7, 1115b","q":["The brave person fears and endures the right things, for the right reason,","in the right way and at the right time."],"c":["For Aristotle, courage is not the absence of fear. Someone who fears nothing is not brave but reckless, or insane. The brave person feels fear, but in proportion, and acts well in spite of it, for the sake of what is noble."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one fear today. Ask whether it is the right fear, and then act well in spite of it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-14","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 5.18","q":["The roots of education are bitter,","but the fruit is sweet."],"c":["Diogenes Laertius records this among Aristotle's sayings. Learning anything worthwhile involves effort, frustration and discipline at first. But the result, understanding and skill, is a lasting reward."],"e":null,"p":"Stick with one difficult learning task today a little longer than you want to.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-15","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 5.20","q":["What is a friend?","A single soul living in two bodies."],"c":["Asked to define a friend, Aristotle is said to have given this answer. It echoes his view in the Ethics that a true friend is \"another self\", someone whose good we want for their own sake, as we want our own."],"e":null,"p":"Tell a close friend today what they mean to you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-16","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 5.20","q":["To do without being commanded","what others do only from fear of the law."],"c":["Asked what he had gained from philosophy, Aristotle answered in this way. Laws compel people to behave from the outside. Philosophy aims to produce people who behave well from within, because they understand why."],"e":null,"p":"Do something right today that no rule requires.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-17","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 5.18","q":["Hope is a waking dream."],"c":["Asked what hope is, Aristotle is said to have answered with this short phrase. Hope can carry us through hardship, but it can also replace action with imagination. A good life needs both dreams and wakefulness."],"e":null,"p":"Turn one hope into one concrete step today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-18","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 5.19","q":["The educated differ from the uneducated","as much as the living from the dead."],"c":["Diogenes Laertius records this sharp saying. For Aristotle, education is not a decoration but what brings the human capacities to life. A mind that is never trained remains, in a sense, asleep."],"e":null,"p":"Feed your mind today with something more substantial than news or entertainment.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-19","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Aelian, Varia Historia 3.36","q":["I will not allow the Athenians","to sin twice against philosophy."],"c":["Aristotle reportedly said this when he left Athens, charged with impiety after Alexander's death. He was thinking of Socrates, executed on a similar charge in 399 BC. Unlike Socrates, he chose to leave rather than face the trial."],"e":{"when":"323 BC · Athens and Chalcis","title":"Flight from Athens","text":"When news came that Alexander had died in Babylon, anti-Macedonian anger exploded in Athens. Aristotle, long associated with the Macedonian court, was charged with impiety for a hymn he had written to his friend Hermias. He moved to his mother's estate at Chalcis on Euboea and died there the following year."},"p":"Know when to stand your ground and when to step aside. Ask which this situation needs.","s":"Saying reported by Aelian, Varia Historia 3.36, and in the ancient lives of Aristotle.","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-20","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Politics 8.3, 1337b","q":["Nature itself requires that we be able not only to work well,","but also to use leisure well."],"c":["Aristotle argues that education should prepare people for leisure as well as for work. We work in order to have leisure, just as we make war in order to have peace. How we use our free time says a great deal about who we are."],"e":null,"p":"Use some free time today on something that genuinely enriches you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-21","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 2.1, 1103b","q":["It makes no small difference whether we form habits of one kind or another from our very youth.","It makes a very great difference, or rather all the difference."],"c":["Since character is built through habit, the habits we form early shape us for life. This is why Aristotle thought education and upbringing matter so much, and why it is never too late to start forming better habits."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one small habit you repeat every day. Is it shaping you in the direction you want?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-22","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 1.10, 1100b","q":["Even in misfortune, nobility shines through","when a person bears many great misfortunes calmly, not from insensitivity but from greatness of soul."],"c":["Aristotle discusses the case of King Priam of Troy, who was prosperous and then lost everything. Can such a person be called happy? Not blessed, perhaps. But someone of good character will never become wretched, because they will never do hateful things, and they will bear misfortune with dignity."],"e":null,"p":"If something goes badly today, bear it in a way you would be proud of later.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-23","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"On the Parts of Animals 1.5, 645a","q":["In all natural things there is something marvellous."],"c":["Aristotle defends the study of animals, even humble and unattractive ones. He recalls Heraclitus, who invited visitors into his kitchen saying \"there are gods here too\". Every living thing, studied closely, reveals the order and beauty of nature."],"e":{"when":"c. 345 BC · Lesbos","title":"The lagoon at Pyrrha","text":"On Lesbos, with his student Theophrastus, Aristotle made detailed observations of marine life in the island's great lagoon. He described the reproduction of the octopus, the embryo of the dogfish shark and much more, some of it not confirmed again until the nineteenth century. It is often called the birth of biology."},"p":"Look closely at something living today, a plant, an insect, a bird, and find what is marvellous in it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-24","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Nicomachean Ethics 9.8, 1169a","q":["The good person will give up wealth, honours and every contested good to gain nobility for themselves.","They would rather have a short period of intense pleasure than a long one of mild enjoyment, and one great and noble action than many trivial ones."],"c":["Aristotle argues that true self-love is not selfish. The person who loves themselves rightly wants the best for their own character, and will therefore act generously and bravely, even at personal cost. Selfishness grabs money and pleasure; true self-love grabs nobility."],"e":null,"p":"Give up something small today for the sake of doing something fine.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-aristotle-25","a":"aristotle"},{"ref":"Fragment B91 (Plutarch, On the E at Delphi 392b)","q":["You cannot step twice into the same river."],"c":["This is the most famous saying attributed to Heraclitus, though it comes to us in later paraphrases. Another fragment (B12) says: \"On those who step into the same rivers, different and different waters flow.\" The river stays the same river precisely because its water keeps changing. So do we."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one thing today that seems the same as always but has quietly changed. Including you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-01","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B119 (Stobaeus 4.40.23)","q":["A person's character","is their destiny."],"c":["In Greek this is just three words: ēthos anthrōpōi daimōn. The daimon was a guardian spirit that people believed shaped their fate. Heraclitus says the real force that shapes a life is a person's own character. What we become is not handed to us from outside; it grows from who we are."],"e":null,"p":"Look at one pattern in your life. Ask what part of your character keeps producing it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-02","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B1 (Sextus Empiricus, Against the Logicians 1.132)","q":["Though this logos holds always, people fail to understand it, both before hearing it and when they have first heard it.","Though all things come to pass in accordance with it, they are like people without experience."],"c":["These are the opening lines of Heraclitus' book. The logos is the rational order that runs through everything: the pattern behind change. Most people, he says, live as if asleep, unaware of the order around them. The Stoics took over his idea of the logos as the foundation of their whole philosophy."],"e":{"when":"c. 500 BC · Temple of Artemis, Ephesus","title":"The book in the temple","text":"Heraclitus is said to have deposited his single book in the great temple of Artemis at Ephesus, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world. He wrote it deliberately in an obscure style, some said, so that only the capable would approach it. The book is lost; we have only the fragments quoted by others."},"p":"Look for the pattern beneath one event that seems random today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-03","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B30 (Clement, Miscellanies 5.104)","q":["This world, the same for all, no god or human made.","It always was and is and will be: an ever-living fire, kindling in measures and going out in measures."],"c":["Heraclitus saw the world not as a fixed object but as a process, like a flame that keeps its shape while its fuel constantly changes. The Stoics took this idea and made fire the basic element of their physics."],"e":null,"p":"Watch a flame or running water for a minute today. Remember that you, too, are a pattern in constant motion.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-04","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B60 (Hippolytus, Refutation 9.10.4)","q":["The road up and the road down","are one and the same."],"c":["Heraclitus loved to show how opposites are connected. The same path leads up or down depending on where you stand. Difficulty and ease, beginning and end, are often two views of the same thing."],"e":null,"p":"Look at a problem today from the opposite direction. What does it look like from there?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-05","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B101 (Plutarch, Against Colotes 1118c)","q":["I searched myself."],"c":["In Greek just two words: edizēsamēn emeōuton. Heraclitus did not learn from teachers; he looked inside. Plutarch connected this with the Delphic command \"know yourself\". Self-examination, for Heraclitus, was the first step to understanding the world."],"e":null,"p":"Spend ten minutes today in honest self-search. What do you find?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-06","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B40 (Diogenes Laertius, Lives 9.1)","q":["Learning many things does not teach understanding.","Otherwise it would have taught Hesiod and Pythagoras, and Xenophanes and Hecataeus too."],"c":["Heraclitus mocked the famous learned men of his time. Collecting facts is not the same as understanding. The one who sees how things fit together is wiser than the one who knows a thousand separate things."],"e":null,"p":"Take one thing you already know and spend time understanding it more deeply, instead of learning something new.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-07","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B53 (Hippolytus, Refutation 9.9.4)","q":["Conflict is the father of all things and king of all.","Some it shows as gods, others as humans; some it makes slaves, others free."],"c":["Heraclitus saw tension and opposition as the forces that make and reveal everything. Without struggle, nothing would show what it is. Elsewhere he says that justice itself is strife (B80). Difficulty is not a flaw in the world; it is how the world works."],"e":null,"p":"Look at a conflict you are in today. What is it revealing about you, or the other person?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-08","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B51 (Hippolytus, Refutation 9.9.2)","q":["They do not understand how what is at odds agrees with itself:","it is a harmony of opposing tensions, as in the bow and the lyre."],"c":["A bow works because the string pulls one way and the wood the other. A lyre makes music through strings held in tension. Heraclitus saw the same in life: balance comes from opposing forces held together, not from the absence of tension."],"e":null,"p":"Notice two pulls in your life that seem opposed. Ask how their tension might be useful.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-09","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B88 (Plutarch, Consolation to Apollonius 106e)","q":["The same thing is in us: living and dead, waking and sleeping, young and old.","For these, changed around, are those, and those, changed around again, are these."],"c":["Heraclitus insists that opposites are not separate things but stages in one process. We are always becoming. Seeing this can soften our fear of change and loss."],"e":null,"p":"Think of one change you are afraid of. Remember that you have already changed many times and remained yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-10","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B89 (Plutarch, On Superstition 166c)","q":["Those who are awake share one common world.","But each sleeper turns away into a private world of their own."],"c":["Heraclitus contrasts the awake person, who sees the common order of things, with the sleeper, lost in private dreams. Marcus Aurelius echoes him: \"We should not act and speak like people asleep.\" To be truly awake is to see clearly what is shared."],"e":null,"p":"When you catch yourself lost in private worries today, wake up to what is actually in front of you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-11","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B123 (Themistius, Orations 5.69b)","q":["Nature loves to hide."],"c":["The deeper structure of things is not on the surface. It must be searched for, with patience. This short saying has inspired scientists and philosophers for centuries."],"e":null,"p":"Look beneath the surface of one thing today: a conversation, a habit, a natural object.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-12","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B18 (Clement, Miscellanies 2.17)","q":["If you do not expect the unexpected, you will not find it,","for it is hard to find and difficult to reach."],"c":["Heraclitus suggests that discovery requires openness. If you only look for what you already expect, you will never see anything new. The unexpected rewards those who are prepared to look for it."],"e":null,"p":"Go into one familiar situation today expecting to be surprised.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-13","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B110 (Stobaeus 3.1.176)","q":["It is not better for people","to get all they want."],"c":["Heraclitus saw that getting everything we desire would not make us happy. Desire satisfied too easily loses its value, and some wants are better unmet."],"e":null,"p":"Let one want go unmet today, on purpose. Notice that you are still fine.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-14","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B85 (Plutarch, Life of Coriolanus 22)","q":["It is hard to fight against anger.","Whatever it wants, it buys at the price of the soul."],"c":["The Greek word thumos covers anger, passion and strong desire. Heraclitus saw it as a costly master. Every time we give in, it takes something from our soul in payment. Plutarch quoted this line to describe the Roman general Coriolanus, whose rage destroyed him."],"e":null,"p":"Before acting on a strong emotion today, ask what it will cost you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-15","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B43 (Diogenes Laertius, Lives 9.2)","q":["Arrogance must be put out","more urgently than a house on fire."],"c":["For Heraclitus, insolence and arrogance (hubris) were the most dangerous of faults. A fire burns one house; arrogance can burn a whole life and a whole city. It must be stopped quickly before it spreads."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one moment of arrogance in yourself today, and put it out at once.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-16","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B44 (Diogenes Laertius, Lives 9.2)","q":["The people must fight for their law","as for their city wall."],"c":["For a Greek city, the wall was its life: without it, the enemy walked in. Heraclitus says laws are just as important. A community without the rule of law is defenceless."],"e":null,"p":"Defend one fair rule today, at work, at home or in public, even when it would be easier to let it go.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-17","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B78 (Origen, Against Celsus 6.12)","q":["Human nature has no insight,","but divine nature has."],"c":["Heraclitus was humble about human understanding. Our knowledge is limited and partial. Elsewhere he says that the wisest person, compared with God, looks like an ape (B83). Wisdom begins by recognising how little we know."],"e":null,"p":"In one decision today, remind yourself that you see only part of the picture.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-18","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B116 (Stobaeus 3.5.6)","q":["It belongs to all people","to know themselves and to think well."],"c":["Despite his scorn for the crowd, Heraclitus believed that everyone has the capacity for self-knowledge and good thinking. It is not reserved for the few. The trouble is that most people do not use it."],"e":null,"p":"Use your capacity for good thinking today on one question you usually avoid.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-19","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B112 (Stobaeus 3.1.178)","q":["Thinking well is the greatest virtue, and wisdom is to say what is true","and to act according to nature, listening to it."],"c":["Here Heraclitus combines thought, speech and action. Wisdom is not just knowing, but speaking truthfully and acting in harmony with the order of things. The Stoics would say almost exactly the same."],"e":null,"p":"Today, make sure that what you think, what you say and what you do line up in one thing.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-20","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B49a (Heraclitus Homericus, Homeric Questions 24)","q":["We step and do not step into the same rivers;","we are and we are not."],"c":["In this version of the river saying, Heraclitus applies the idea directly to us. We are the same person from day to day, and yet we are constantly changing. Identity is not something fixed but something that flows."],"e":null,"p":"Think about one way you have changed in the past year. Welcome it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-21","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B94 (Plutarch, On Exile 604a)","q":["The sun will not overstep its measures.","If it does, the Furies, servants of Justice, will find it out."],"c":["Even the sun, Heraclitus says, must keep within limits. The universe is governed by measure and balance, and whatever oversteps its place will be brought back. For the Stoics, this became the idea of a natural law that applies to all things."],"e":null,"p":"Where are you overstepping your measure today: in work, food, words? Pull back a little.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-22","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 9.3","q":["Why are you surprised, you scoundrels?","Isn't it better to do this than to take part in politics with you?"],"c":["Diogenes Laertius tells that Heraclitus withdrew to the temple of Artemis and played dice with children there. When the Ephesians gathered to stare, he answered them with these words. Disgusted with his city, he preferred children's games to their politics."],"e":{"when":"c. 500 BC · Ephesus","title":"The exile of Hermodorus","text":"The Ephesians banished Heraclitus' friend Hermodorus, saying that no one among them should be better than the rest. Heraclitus responded that all adult Ephesians should hang themselves and leave the city to the boys. Hermodorus is said to have gone to Rome and helped the Romans draft their earliest code of law."},"p":"If you are tired of a pointless contest today, step out of it without guilt.","s":"Hermodorus: Heraclitus B121; Pliny, Natural History 34.21.","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-23","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals 1.5, 645a","q":["Come in. There are gods here too."],"c":["Aristotle tells that visitors once came to see Heraclitus and found him warming himself by the kitchen stove. They hesitated, embarrassed to find the famous philosopher in such a humble place. He told them to come in. The divine is not only in temples but everywhere, even by the oven."],"e":null,"p":"Find something sacred, or simply wonderful, in an ordinary place today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-24","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Fragment B111 (Stobaeus 3.1.177)","q":["Disease makes health pleasant and good, hunger makes fullness,","weariness makes rest."],"c":["Heraclitus saw that we only understand good things through their opposites. Health is sweet because we know sickness. Rest is precious because we know tiredness. Hardships are not just obstacles; they give meaning to good things."],"e":null,"p":"After any discomfort today, take a moment to enjoy its opposite fully.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-heraclitus-25","a":"heraclitus"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 1","q":["Accept everything just the way it is."],"c":["The Dokkōdō, \"The Way of Walking Alone\", is a list of twenty-one short precepts that Musashi wrote a week before his death. Its first line could have been written by Epictetus. Acceptance is not giving up; it is the starting point for clear action, because we cannot act well on a situation we refuse to see."],"e":{"when":"May 1645 · Kumamoto","title":"The last week","text":"Knowing he was dying, Musashi gave away his possessions, wrote the Dokkōdō and handed it, together with The Book of Five Rings, to his student Terao Magonojō. He died on 13 June 1645, aged about sixty-one. Legend says he was buried in full armour."},"p":"Choose one thing today that you have been resisting, and accept it exactly as it is. Then decide what to do.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-01","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 4","q":["Think lightly of yourself","and deeply of the world."],"c":["Musashi warns against self-importance. A person preoccupied with themselves misses what is going on around them. Taking yourself lightly frees your attention for what matters."],"e":null,"p":"In one moment today when you feel offended or important, turn your attention outward instead.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-02","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 6","q":["Do not regret what you have done."],"c":["For Musashi, regret wastes the energy needed for the next action. This does not mean ignoring mistakes; he studied his defeats and errors carefully. It means not carrying them as weight. Learn the lesson, then put the burden down."],"e":null,"p":"Take one past mistake you keep replaying. Name the lesson, and let the rest go.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-03","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 7","q":["Never be jealous."],"c":["Jealousy fixes our attention on what others have instead of on our own path. Musashi, who walked his own way all his life, saw it as a distraction from the work of self-improvement."],"e":null,"p":"When you notice envy today, turn it into a question: what is one thing I can do on my own path?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-04","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 9","q":["Resentment and complaint are fitting","neither for yourself nor for others."],"c":["Musashi lived a hard life: no lord for most of it, little money, constant danger. He gives himself no room to complain. Complaint changes nothing and poisons the mind."],"e":null,"p":"Go one full day without complaining, aloud or in your head.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-05","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 14","q":["Do not keep possessions","you no longer need."],"c":["Musashi lived simply and travelled light. Possessions, he believed, tie us down and occupy the mind. In his last days he gave away everything he owned."],"e":null,"p":"Give away or throw out one thing today that you no longer need.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-06","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 17","q":["Do not fear death."],"c":["Musashi faced death in dozens of duels from the age of thirteen. He came to see the fear of death as something that weakens judgment in the moment it is needed most. The Stoics and the samurai agreed: a mind free from the fear of death is free."],"e":{"when":"1596 · Harima Province","title":"The first duel","text":"According to the opening of The Book of Five Rings, Musashi fought his first duel at thirteen, against Arima Kihei, a samurai of the Shintō-ryū school who had posted a public challenge. The boy accepted, and killed him. It was the first of more than sixty duels he claimed never to have lost."},"p":"When fear rises today, ask what you would do if you were not afraid. Then do it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-07","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 19","q":["Respect the Buddha and the gods","without counting on their help."],"c":["Musashi honoured the gods but relied on his own effort. Another story tells that before a duel he stopped at a shrine to pray for victory, then turned away, realising that he should depend on himself alone. Reverence and self-reliance can go together."],"e":null,"p":"Do your part today as if everything depends on you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-08","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 21","q":["Never stray from the Way."],"c":["This is the last of Musashi's twenty-one precepts. The Way, for him, was the path of strategy and self-discipline he had followed all his life. It meant constant training, clear perception and self-reliance, in war and in every art."],"e":null,"p":"Name your own Way in one sentence. Take one step on it today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-09","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"Dokkōdō, precept 12","q":["Be indifferent","to where you live."],"c":["Musashi spent most of his life wandering, without a fixed home or a permanent lord. He learned to be comfortable anywhere. In his last years he lived in a mountain cave. Like the Stoic citizen of the world, he did not tie his peace of mind to a place."],"e":{"when":"1643 · Reigandō, near Kumamoto","title":"The cave","text":"In his sixtieth year, Musashi withdrew to Reigandō, the \"Spirit Rock Cave\", in the mountains west of Kumamoto, to write The Book of Five Rings. He began writing on the tenth day of the tenth month, after praying to Heaven and to Kannon. The cave can still be visited today."},"p":"Find calm today wherever you are, without needing a better place.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-10","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["Do not think dishonestly.","The Way is in training."],"c":["These are the first two of Musashi's nine principles for those who want to learn his strategy. Honesty with oneself comes first, because self-deception ruins judgment. Then training: the Way is not learned from books but through constant practice."],"e":null,"p":"Be completely honest with yourself about one thing today, then train it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-11","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["Perceive those things which cannot be seen."],"c":["Another of Musashi's nine principles. The skilled strategist notices what others miss: intentions, rhythms, the moment before something happens. This comes only through long practice and careful attention."],"e":null,"p":"In one conversation today, pay attention to what is not said.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-12","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["Pay attention even to trifles.","Do nothing which is of no use."],"c":["These are the last two of the nine principles. Small details decide outcomes, and wasted effort weakens us. Musashi's way is economical: full attention, no wasted movement."],"e":null,"p":"Today, cut one useless activity and give that attention to a small detail that matters.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-13","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["From one thing, know ten thousand things."],"c":["Musashi believed that mastering one discipline deeply teaches principles that apply everywhere. The way of the sword taught him painting, sculpture, calligraphy and leadership. Deep understanding of one thing opens many doors."],"e":null,"p":"Take a principle from your work or hobby and apply it to another part of your life today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-14","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["The master carpenter uses his men according to their abilities.","He knows who is fit for the pillars, who for the floors, who for the rough work."],"c":["Musashi compares the strategist to a master builder who assigns each worker and each piece of wood to the place where it serves best. A good leader knows people's strengths and puts them where they can succeed."],"e":null,"p":"Recognise one person's real strength today and give them a chance to use it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-15","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["When I passed thirty, I looked back and saw that my victories had not come from mastery of strategy.","I went on studying morning and evening, and only at about fifty did I come to understand the Way."],"c":["Musashi admits that his early victories came from natural talent, luck or the weakness of his opponents. Undefeated, he kept training for another twenty years until he understood what he was doing. Success did not end his search."],"e":{"when":"1612 · Funashima (Ganryū-jima)","title":"The duel with Kojirō","text":"Musashi's most famous duel was fought against Sasaki Kojirō, known for his very long sword, on a small island in the strait between Honshu and Kyushu. As the story is usually told, Musashi arrived late, having carved a wooden sword from a spare oar on the boat, and killed Kojirō with a single blow. Many details are later legend."},"p":"Look at one thing you are good at and ask how much of it is real understanding. Keep learning.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-16","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Earth","q":["You should not have a favourite weapon.","Too much of one thing is as bad as not enough."],"c":["Musashi warns against over-reliance on any single tool or method. A strategist who has a favourite becomes predictable and is helpless when it fails. Flexibility is strength."],"e":null,"p":"Try a different approach to a familiar task today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-17","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Water","q":["Water adopts the shape of its vessel;","it is sometimes a trickle and sometimes a wild sea."],"c":["Musashi named the second book after water because the mind should be like it: flexible, clear and adaptable. A rigid mind breaks; a mind like water adjusts to every situation. Sun Tzu used the same image."],"e":null,"p":"Let your plan bend today to fit the situation, instead of forcing the situation into your plan.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-18","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Water","q":["In strategy, your state of mind must not be any different from normal.","Keep your mind broad and straight, neither too tense nor too relaxed."],"c":["Musashi says the mind should be the same in combat as in daily life: calm, open, balanced. Excitement and fear distort judgment. The trained mind does not change under pressure."],"e":null,"p":"In a stressful moment today, notice your state of mind and bring it back to normal.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-19","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Water","q":["Perception is strong, sight is weak.","See distant things as if they were close, and close things as if they were distant."],"c":["Musashi distinguishes two kinds of seeing: ken, ordinary looking at details, and kan, deeper perception of the whole situation. A good fighter does not fix their eyes on the opponent's sword, but takes in everything at once."],"e":null,"p":"Step back from one problem today and look at it as if from far away.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-20","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Water","q":["A thousand days of training to forge,","ten thousand days of training to polish."],"c":["Musashi uses the image of a sword blade, forged and then polished, for the training of a fighter. A thousand days is about three years; ten thousand days, about thirty. Real mastery takes a lifetime."],"e":null,"p":"Do one session of patient practice today, knowing that it is one of ten thousand.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-21","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Fire","q":["To become the enemy:","think yourself into the enemy's position."],"c":["Musashi teaches the strategist to see the situation through the opponent's eyes. What do they fear? What do they expect? Understanding the other side changes everything. Outside of combat, this is simply empathy."],"e":null,"p":"In one disagreement today, describe the situation from the other person's side.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-22","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Fire","q":["If you have tried something twice and it has failed, do not try it a third time.","If the enemy expects the mountain, attack like the sea; if they expect the sea, attack like the mountain."],"c":["Musashi calls this \"mountain and sea\". Repeating what has failed is stubbornness, not persistence. A wise strategist changes approach completely when the first one does not work."],"e":null,"p":"Is there something you keep trying in the same way? Try something completely different today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-23","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Fire","q":["Rat's head, ox's neck."],"c":["Musashi uses this odd phrase to describe two modes of attention. The rat's head is small, fine detail; the ox's neck is the large, strong picture. When you get lost in details, switch to the big picture, and back again. A good strategist moves between them."],"e":null,"p":"If you feel lost in details today, step back to the big picture, and vice versa.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-24","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Book of Five Rings, Void","q":["In the void is virtue, and no evil.","Wisdom has existence, principle has existence, the Way has existence, spirit is nothingness."],"c":["The last and shortest book of the Five Rings speaks of the void: the state of mind that is clear, empty of confusion and unattached. For Musashi, after a lifetime of training, the void is where true understanding lives."],"e":null,"p":"Sit in silence for five minutes today, letting your mind clear.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-miyamoto-musashi-25","a":"miyamoto-musashi"},{"ref":"The Art of War 1.1–2","q":["The art of war is of vital importance to the State.","It is a matter of life and death, a road either to safety or to ruin."],"c":["These are the opening words of the book. Sun Tzu does not glorify war; he treats it as a grave matter that must be studied carefully precisely because so much is at stake. Careless leaders lose nations."],"e":{"when":"1972 · Yinqueshan, Shandong","title":"The bamboo slips","text":"Workers digging near Linyi opened two Han dynasty tombs from around 140 BC. Inside were nearly five thousand bamboo strips covered with writing, including large parts of The Art of War and a long-lost work by the later strategist Sun Bin. The find proved that the text we read today was already established more than two thousand years ago."},"p":"Name the one decision in your life right now that is truly a \"road to safety or to ruin\". Give it the serious study it deserves.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-01","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 3.2","q":["To fight and conquer in all your battles is not supreme excellence;","supreme excellence consists in breaking the enemy's resistance without fighting."],"c":["This is Sun Tzu's most famous principle. Winning a battle costs lives, money and time, even for the winner. The best victory is one where the other side yields without a fight, through strategy, alliances or a position too strong to challenge."],"e":null,"p":"In one disagreement today, look for a way to resolve it without a fight.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-02","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 3.18","q":["If you know the enemy and know yourself, you need not fear the result of a hundred battles.","If you know yourself but not the enemy, for every victory gained you will also suffer a defeat."],"c":["Sun Tzu continues: if you know neither the enemy nor yourself, you will lose every battle. Knowledge, not force, decides outcomes. And notice the order: knowing yourself is as important as knowing the other side."],"e":{"when":"1910 · London","title":"Giles translates Sun Tzu","text":"Lionel Giles, a curator of Chinese books at the British Museum, published his translation of The Art of War with extensive notes, criticising an earlier English version for its mistakes. It became the standard English text for decades and is now in the public domain. The words on this page are his."},"p":"Before a meeting or challenge today, write down three honest facts about yourself and three about the other side.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-03","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 4.1–2","q":["The good fighters of old first put themselves beyond the possibility of defeat, and then waited for an opportunity of defeating the enemy.","To secure ourselves against defeat lies in our own hands, but the opportunity of defeating the enemy is provided by the enemy himself."],"c":["This passage is strikingly Stoic. Sun Tzu separates what is up to us, our own preparation and defence, from what is not, the enemy's mistakes. Make yourself solid first; then be ready when an opening appears."],"e":null,"p":"Strengthen one thing today that is fully in your hands, and stay alert for openings you cannot force.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-04","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 1.26","q":["Now the general who wins a battle makes many calculations in his temple ere the battle is fought.","The general who loses a battle makes but few calculations beforehand."],"c":["Before a campaign, generals in ancient China held councils in the ancestral temple to compare their strengths with the enemy's. Sun Tzu says that victory is decided there, by careful thinking, long before the armies meet."],"e":{"when":"1772 · Paris","title":"The Jesuit's translation","text":"Jean Joseph Marie Amiot, a French Jesuit missionary living at the Qing court in Beijing, sent home a French translation of The Art of War. It was the first version in a European language and was read in France in the years before the Revolution. Some later writers claimed Napoleon had studied it, though there is no proof."},"p":"Before your next important task, spend ten minutes planning it in detail.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-05","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 2.6","q":["There is no instance of a country having benefited from prolonged warfare."],"c":["Sun Tzu warns that long campaigns drain the treasury, exhaust the people and invite other enemies to take advantage. Speed and decisiveness are kinder in the end than drawn-out conflict. The same holds true of disputes in daily life."],"e":null,"p":"Is there a conflict in your life that has dragged on too long? Take a step to end it.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-06","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 4.13","q":["He wins his battles by making no mistakes."],"c":["Sun Tzu explains that the truly skilled general does not win spectacular victories that everyone praises. His wins look easy, because he has prepared so well that the outcome was never in doubt. Excellence often looks boring from the outside."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one task today and do it so carefully that nothing goes wrong.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-07","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 4.15","q":["In war the victorious strategist only seeks battle after the victory has been won,","whereas he who is destined to defeat first fights and afterwards looks for victory."],"c":["This line reverses the usual idea of battle. The winner wins in preparation and only then fights. The loser fights first and hopes to find a way to win along the way."],"e":null,"p":"Before you start something important today, ask whether you have already won it in preparation.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-08","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 5.13–15","q":["The quality of decision is like the well-timed swoop of a falcon which enables it to strike and destroy its victim.","Energy may be likened to the bending of a crossbow; decision, to the releasing of a trigger."],"c":["Sun Tzu distinguishes stored energy from the moment of action. A crossbow drawn too early wastes strength; released too late, the chance is gone. The skill is in building up force patiently and then acting at exactly the right moment."],"e":null,"p":"Identify one decision you have been holding back. If the moment is right, release the trigger today.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-09","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 6.31–32","q":["Water shapes its course according to the nature of the ground over which it flows.","Just as water retains no constant shape, so in warfare there are no constant conditions."],"c":["Water flows around obstacles and finds the weakest point. Sun Tzu urges the same flexibility: do not hold rigidly to one plan; read the situation and adapt. The commander who can change his tactics according to the enemy \"may be called a heaven-born captain\"."],"e":null,"p":"When a plan meets resistance today, flow around it instead of pushing harder.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-10","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 6.1–2","q":["Whoever is first in the field and awaits the coming of the enemy, will be fresh for the fight.","The clever combatant imposes his will on the enemy, but does not allow the enemy's will to be imposed on him."],"c":["Being early is an advantage in war and in life. The one who arrives first chooses the ground, rests and prepares. The one who arrives late must react to someone else's plan. Taking the initiative means setting the terms."],"e":null,"p":"Arrive early today at one appointment or task, and use the extra time to prepare.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-11","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 7.17–19","q":["Let your rapidity be that of the wind, your compactness that of the forest.","In raiding and plundering be like fire, in immovability like a mountain. Let your plans be dark and impenetrable as night, and when you move, fall like a thunderbolt."],"c":["Sun Tzu uses images from nature to describe the qualities of a good army: fast when it must move, solid when it must hold, fierce in attack, steady in defence. A good person, like a good army, needs different qualities for different moments."],"e":{"when":"16th century · Kai Province, Japan","title":"Fūrinkazan","text":"The Japanese warlord Takeda Shingen took four words from this passage, Wind, Forest, Fire, Mountain, and carried them on his battle standard. His banner with the motto \"Fūrinkazan\" became one of the most famous in Japanese history, a sign of how far Sun Tzu's ideas travelled."},"p":"Ask which quality today needs from you: wind, forest, fire or mountain.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-12","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 7.31","q":["To be near the goal while the enemy is still far from it, to wait at ease while the enemy is toiling and struggling,","to be well-fed while the enemy is famished: this is the art of husbanding one's strength."],"c":["Sun Tzu stresses the importance of conserving energy. Exhaustion defeats armies as surely as weapons. Rest, food and position are not luxuries but weapons in themselves."],"e":null,"p":"Protect your energy today. Rest, eat well and do not waste effort on what does not matter.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-13","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 8.11","q":["The art of war teaches us to rely not on the likelihood of the enemy's not coming, but on our own readiness to receive him;","not on the chance of his not attacking, but rather on the fact that we have made our position unassailable."],"c":["Hope is not a strategy. Sun Tzu says we should not count on trouble staying away, but on being ready when it comes. This is the military version of the Stoic practice of preparing in advance for hardship."],"e":null,"p":"Prepare today for one problem you are hoping will not happen.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-14","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 8.12","q":["There are five dangerous faults which may affect a general: recklessness, which leads to destruction; cowardice, which leads to capture;","a hasty temper, which can be provoked by insults; a delicacy of honor which is sensitive to shame; over-solicitude for his men, which exposes him to worry and trouble."],"c":["Sun Tzu lists five weaknesses that an enemy can exploit. Each is a virtue pushed too far or a passion left unchecked. A Stoic would recognise them all as errors of judgment that a trained mind can correct."],"e":null,"p":"Which of the five faults is closest to you? Watch for it today.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-15","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 9.43","q":["Soldiers must be treated in the first instance with humanity,","but kept under control by means of iron discipline."],"c":["Sun Tzu combines care and firmness. Soldiers punished before they are attached to their leader will be resentful; soldiers who are attached but never disciplined will be useless. Good leadership needs both."],"e":{"when":"c. 512 BC · Palace of Wu","title":"The drill of the palace women","text":"According to the historian Sima Qian, King Helü of Wu tested Sun Tzu by asking him to drill 180 palace women. When they laughed at his orders, Sun Tzu explained again; when they laughed again, he had the king's two favourite concubines, who led the companies, executed. After that, the women obeyed perfectly. The king, though grieving, appointed him general."},"p":"Be kind and firm with someone today: warm in manner, clear in expectations.","s":"Palace drill: Sima Qian, Records of the Grand Historian 65.","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-16","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 10.24","q":["The general who advances without coveting fame and retreats without fearing disgrace,","whose only thought is to protect his country and do good service for his sovereign, is the jewel of the kingdom."],"c":["Sun Tzu's ideal commander is free from vanity. He advances when it is right, not to win glory, and retreats when it is right, even if people call him a coward. His judgment is not bent by reputation."],"e":null,"p":"Make one decision today based on what is right, not on how it will look.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-17","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 10.25","q":["Regard your soldiers as your children, and they will follow you into the deepest valleys;","look upon them as your own beloved sons, and they will stand by you even unto death."],"c":["Sun Tzu adds a warning: if you are indulgent but cannot make your authority felt, they will be like spoilt children, useless for any practical purpose. Care and authority go together."],"e":null,"p":"Treat someone you lead or support today with the care you would give your own family.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-18","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 12.18–19","q":["No ruler should put troops into the field merely to gratify his own spleen; no general should fight a battle simply out of pique.","If it is to your advantage, make a forward move; if not, stay where you are."],"c":["Anger is a bad reason for any action. Sun Tzu warns rulers not to start wars, and generals not to fight battles, because they feel insulted. Act when it helps; otherwise, wait."],"e":null,"p":"Before acting on irritation today, ask whether it actually serves your goal.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-19","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 12.20–22","q":["Anger may in time change to gladness; vexation may be succeeded by content.","But a kingdom that has once been destroyed can never come again into being; nor can the dead ever be brought back to life."],"c":["Feelings pass, but some consequences are permanent. This is why the enlightened ruler is careful and the good general cautious. Do not let a passing mood make an irreversible decision."],"e":null,"p":"If you feel tempted to do something irreversible in anger today, wait until the feeling passes.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-20","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 13.4–6","q":["What enables the wise sovereign and the good general to strike and conquer, and achieve things beyond the reach of ordinary men, is foreknowledge.","Now this foreknowledge cannot be elicited from spirits; knowledge of the enemy's dispositions can only be obtained from other men."],"c":["In the last chapter, on spies, Sun Tzu insists that knowledge must come from real information, not from omens, guesswork or theory. Spending money on good information is far cheaper than losing a war through ignorance."],"e":null,"p":"Before making a decision today, find out one fact from someone who actually knows.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-21","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 3.17","q":["He will win who knows when to fight and when not to fight."],"c":["This is the first of Sun Tzu's five essentials for victory. Not every battle should be fought. Recognising when to engage and when to step back is itself a victory, and often the most important one."],"e":null,"p":"Choose one battle not to fight today.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-22","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 5.10–11","q":["In battle, there are not more than two methods of attack, the direct and the indirect; yet these two in combination give rise to an endless series of maneuvers.","The direct and the indirect lead on to each other in turn. It is like moving in a circle: you never come to an end."],"c":["Sun Tzu compares strategy to music, colour and taste: just five notes, five colours and five flavours, yet their combinations are endless. A few basic principles, combined creatively, produce limitless possibilities."],"e":null,"p":"Solve one problem today with an indirect approach instead of a head-on one.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-23","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 11.29–30","q":["The skillful tactician may be likened to the shuai-jan. Now the shuai-jan is a snake that is found in the Ch'ang mountains.","Strike at its head, and you will be attacked by its tail; strike at its tail, and you will be attacked by its head; strike at its middle, and you will be attacked by head and tail both."],"c":["Sun Tzu uses this image for a well-coordinated army in which every part supports the others. He adds that even enemies, like the men of Wu and Yue, will help each other when caught in the same boat in a storm. Unity is strength."],"e":null,"p":"Support a teammate or family member today without being asked.","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-24","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Art of War 1.18–19","q":["All warfare is based on deception.","When able to attack, we must seem unable; when using our forces, we must seem inactive."],"c":["Sun Tzu's most notorious line is a warning as much as advice. In conflict, things are often not what they seem. The Stoic lesson is to look beyond appearances, both in others and in our own first impressions."],"e":null,"p":"Look twice today at something that seems obvious. What might the appearance be hiding?","s":"","t":"Translation: Lionel Giles, 1910 (public domain).","id":"q-sun-tzu-25","a":"sun-tzu"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 25","q":["I hold it to be true that Fortune is the arbiter of one-half of our actions,","but that she still leaves us to direct the other half, or perhaps a little less."],"c":["Many in Machiavelli's time believed that everything was ruled by fate or God, so there was no point in trying. Machiavelli refuses that view \"not to extinguish our free will\". He gives fortune about half, and claims the other half for human skill and effort. A Stoic would draw the line in a different place, but would agree that what is ours must be fully used."],"e":{"when":"1513 · Sant'Andrea in Percussina","title":"The Prince is written","text":"Released after torture and stripped of office, Machiavelli retired to his small farm south of Florence. In a few months, in the evenings, he wrote The Prince and dedicated it to Lorenzo de' Medici, hoping to win back a post. It did not work. The book was published only in 1532, five years after his death."},"p":"Divide one situation today into Fortune's half and your half. Put all your effort into yours.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-01","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 25","q":["I compare her to one of those raging rivers, which when in flood overflows the plains, sweeping away trees and buildings …","and yet, though its nature be such, it does not follow therefore that men, when the weather becomes fair, shall not make provision, both with defences and barriers."],"c":["Fortune, Machiavelli says, shows her power where courage has not prepared to resist her. You cannot stop the flood once it comes. But in calm times you can build the dykes. Preparation done in good times decides how you fare in bad ones."],"e":null,"p":"While things are calm today, build one \"dyke\": a saving, a skill, a plan, a repaired relationship.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-02","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 17","q":["It may be answered that one should wish to be both, but, because it is difficult to unite them in one person,","it is much safer to be feared than loved, when, of the two, either must be dispensed with."],"c":["This is the most famous and most misunderstood line in The Prince. Machiavelli does not say fear is better than love. He says both are best, and if one must choose, fear is more reliable, because love depends on others' goodwill while fear depends on the ruler. Read alongside the Stoics, it raises the question of what we build our security on."],"e":null,"p":"Notice where you depend on others' goodwill today, and where you depend on your own steadiness.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-03","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 17","q":["A prince ought to inspire fear in such a way that, if he does not win love,","he avoids hatred."],"c":["Machiavelli immediately limits his own advice. Being feared is useful; being hated is fatal. And hatred, he notes, comes above all from taking what belongs to others. Firmness without injustice can be respected; injustice cannot."],"e":null,"p":"If you have to be firm with someone today, be firm without being unfair.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-04","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 18","q":["A prince … ought to choose the fox and the lion;","because the lion cannot defend himself against snares and the fox cannot defend himself against wolves."],"c":["Machiavelli says a leader needs two natures: the cunning of the fox to see traps and the strength of the lion to frighten enemies. Relying on just one is not enough. Read more gently, it is a reminder that strength without insight, or insight without strength, will fail."],"e":null,"p":"Ask which you need more of today: the fox's clear sight or the lion's courage.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-05","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 18","q":["Every one sees what you appear to be,","few really know what you are."],"c":["Machiavelli observes that people judge by appearances and by results. Few have the chance to know us closely. For a prince, this is a lesson in managing his image. For a Stoic, it is a reminder that reputation and character are different things, and only one of them is in our hands."],"e":null,"p":"Today, care more about what you are than what you appear to be.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-06","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 6","q":["There is nothing more difficult to take in hand, more perilous to conduct, or more uncertain in its success,","than to take the lead in the introduction of a new order of things."],"c":["Machiavelli explains why reformers struggle. Those who did well under the old system become enemies; those who might benefit from the new one are only lukewarm, because they do not yet believe in it. Anyone starting something new should expect resistance, and not be surprised by it."],"e":null,"p":"If you are trying to change something today, expect resistance, and plan for it calmly.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-07","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 6","q":["All armed prophets have conquered,","and the unarmed ones have been destroyed."],"c":["Machiavelli is thinking of Moses and Romulus on one side, and of the Florentine preacher Savonarola on the other. Ideas alone, he argues, do not hold when people stop believing; reformers need the means to carry their vision through."],"e":{"when":"23 May 1498 · Piazza della Signoria, Florence","title":"The fall of Savonarola","text":"For four years the Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florence with fiery preaching, bonfires of vanities and calls for moral reform. When his support collapsed, he was arrested, tortured and hanged and burned in the main square. Weeks later, the twenty-nine-year-old Machiavelli entered the service of the Republic."},"p":"For one goal you care about today, ask: do I have the means to carry this through, or only the wish?","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-08","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 22","q":["The first opinion which one forms of a prince, and of his understanding,","is by observing the men he has around him."],"c":["A leader is judged by the people they choose. Capable and loyal advisers show good judgment; poor ones show its absence. Seneca said something similar: choose your companions carefully, because character rubs off."],"e":null,"p":"Look at the people you spend most time with. What do they say about your judgment?","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-09","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 23","q":["There is no other way of guarding oneself from flatterers","except letting men understand that to tell you the truth does not offend you."],"c":["Courts are full of flatterers, Machiavelli observes, because people love to hear good things about themselves. The only defence is to make honesty safe. A wise leader chooses a few wise people, gives them freedom to speak the truth, and listens."],"e":null,"p":"Tell someone today that you want their honest opinion, and react well when you get it.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-10","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 14","q":["To exercise the intellect the prince should read histories, and study there the actions of illustrious men,","to see how they have borne themselves in war, to examine the causes of their victories and defeat, so as to avoid the latter and imitate the former."],"c":["Machiavelli recommends learning from the past. He mentions that Alexander imitated Achilles, Caesar imitated Alexander, and Scipio imitated Cyrus. Plutarch would have agreed: lives of great people are a school."],"e":null,"p":"Read about one person from history today and take one lesson from them.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-11","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 3","q":["They knew that war is not to be avoided,","but is only to be put off to the advantage of others."],"c":["Machiavelli praises the Romans for dealing with problems early, before they grew. Postponing a necessary conflict does not make it go away; it only gives the other side time to grow stronger. Small problems are easy to cure and hard to see; big ones are easy to see and hard to cure."],"e":null,"p":"Address one small problem today before it becomes a big one.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-12","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 15","q":["How one lives is so far distant from how one ought to live,","that he who neglects what is done for what ought to be done, sooner effects his ruin than his preservation."],"c":["This is the line that made Machiavelli notorious. He insists on looking at people as they really are, not as moralists imagine them. A Stoic would not accept his conclusion, but would agree with the first step: see the world as it is before deciding how to act in it."],"e":null,"p":"Look honestly at one situation today as it really is, not as you think it should be.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-13","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 21","q":["Irresolute princes, to avoid present dangers, generally follow the neutral path,","and are generally ruined."],"c":["Machiavelli argues that sitting on the fence in a conflict usually makes enemies on both sides. The winner will not thank you, and the loser will not protect you. Taking a clear position, even with risk, is usually wiser than hiding."],"e":null,"p":"If you have been avoiding taking a stand on something, decide where you stand today.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-14","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 13","q":["The wise prince has always avoided these arms and turned to his own;","and has been willing rather to lose with them than to conquer with the others."],"c":["Machiavelli distrusted mercenary and borrowed troops: unreliable in battle and dangerous in victory. A state should rely on its own citizens. For individuals, the lesson is to build your own strength rather than depend on borrowed help."],"e":{"when":"8 June 1509 · Pisa","title":"The militia takes Pisa","text":"For years Florence had failed to recapture Pisa with hired troops. Machiavelli persuaded the government to raise a militia of Florentine citizens and peasants, and organised it himself. In 1509, after a long blockade, Pisa surrendered. It was the high point of his career."},"p":"Strengthen one ability of your own today that you usually rely on others for.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-15","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 24","q":["It is a common defect in man","not to make any provision in the calm against the tempest."],"c":["Machiavelli writes about the Italian princes who lost their states. They did not lose them by bad luck, he says, but by idleness: in quiet times they never thought things could change. When bad times came, they thought of flight instead of defence."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today to prepare for a storm that isn't here yet.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-16","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 24","q":["That deliverance is of no avail which does not depend upon yourself;","those only are reliable, certain, and durable that depend on yourself and your valour."],"c":["This could have been written by Epictetus. Machiavelli's point is political: princes who wait to be rescued by others are lost. But the principle is universal. What depends on you is the only thing you can truly count on."],"e":null,"p":"Identify one thing you are waiting for someone else to fix. Take one step yourself.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-17","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 21","q":["Nothing makes a prince so much esteemed","as great enterprises and setting a fine example."],"c":["Machiavelli points to Ferdinand of Aragon, who rose from a minor king to the first king in Christendom through bold undertakings. Reputation is earned through action, not words, and through example more than command."],"e":null,"p":"Set a good example today in one thing others can see.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-18","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"The Prince, chapter 9","q":["A prince cannot rely upon what he observes in quiet times, when citizens have need of the state,","because then every one agrees with him … but in troubled times, when the state has need of its citizens, then he finds but few."],"c":["Loyalty is easy to promise when nothing is at stake. Machiavelli warns not to mistake fair-weather friends for real ones. True loyalty only shows in hard times, and a wise person builds relationships that will hold then."],"e":null,"p":"Be the one who shows up for someone in their trouble today.","s":"","t":"Translation: W. K. Marriott, 1908 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-19","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Discourses on Livy, Book I, chapter 3","q":["They who lay the foundations of a State and furnish it with laws must … assume that all men are bad,","and will always, when they have free field, give loose to their evil inclinations."],"c":["Machiavelli argues that good institutions must be built for people as they are, not as we wish they were. Rules should make it hard to do harm and easy to do right. In our own lives, we can design habits and surroundings the same way, so that good choices are easier."],"e":null,"p":"Change one thing in your surroundings today that makes a bad habit harder and a good one easier.","s":"","t":"Translation: Ninian Hill Thomson, 1883 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-20","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Discourses on Livy, Book III, chapter 9","q":["He will make fewest mistakes, and may expect to prosper most,","who, while following the course to which nature inclines him, finds, as I have said, his method of acting in accordance with the times in which he lives."],"c":["Machiavelli uses the example of Fabius Maximus, whose cautious delaying tactics saved Rome against Hannibal, but who would have failed in a different situation. Success comes from matching our nature to the moment. The hard part is that we rarely change our methods even when the times change."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself today whether your usual approach fits the situation, or only fits your habits.","s":"","t":"Translation: Ninian Hill Thomson, 1883 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-21","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Discourses on Livy, Book I, chapter 58","q":["That a People is wiser and more constant than a Prince."],"c":["This is the title of the chapter. Against all the historians who called the crowd fickle, Machiavelli argues that a people governed by laws judges better and is more consistent than a single ruler. The author of The Prince was also a convinced republican."],"e":null,"p":"Before a decision today, ask the people it affects what they think.","s":"","t":"Translation: Ninian Hill Thomson, 1883 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-22","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Discourses on Livy, Book I, chapter 10","q":["Titus, Nerva, Trajan, Hadrian, Antoninus and Marcus had no need of praetorian cohorts, or of countless legions to guard them,","but were defended by their own good lives, the good-will of their subjects, and the attachment of the senate."],"c":["Machiavelli contrasts the good emperors, including Marcus Aurelius, with tyrants like Caligula and Nero, who needed armies to protect them and were killed anyway. The safest defence for a ruler, he concludes, is to deserve the goodwill of the people."],"e":null,"p":"Act today in a way that earns goodwill rather than needing protection.","s":"","t":"Translation: Ninian Hill Thomson, 1883 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-23","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Discourses on Livy, Book III, chapter 31","q":["A great man is constantly the same through all vicissitudes of Fortune;","so that although she change, now exalting, now depressing, he remains unchanged."],"c":["Machiavelli quotes the Roman hero Camillus: \"My courage came not with my dictatorship nor went with my exile.\" Weak people, he says, are puffed up by success and broken by failure. Great people stay the same. It is the most Stoic page Machiavelli wrote."],"e":null,"p":"Whatever happens today, good or bad, try to be the same person at the end of the day as at the start.","s":"","t":"Translation: Ninian Hill Thomson, 1883 (public domain).","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-24","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Letter to Francesco Vettori, 10 December 1513","q":["When evening comes, I return home and go into my study. At the door I take off my everyday clothes, covered in mud and dirt, and put on the robes of court.","Decently dressed, I enter the ancient courts of the ancients, where, received kindly by them, I feed on the food that alone is mine."],"c":["Machiavelli describes his exile on the farm to his friend Vettori: snaring thrushes, arguing with woodcutters, playing cards at the inn. But in the evenings he talks with the great writers of the past, asks them questions and they answer. For four hours, he says, he feels no boredom, forgets every trouble, does not fear poverty and is not frightened by death."],"e":{"when":"1513 · Florence and Sant'Andrea","title":"Prison and exile","text":"In February 1513 Machiavelli was arrested as a suspected conspirator against the Medici and tortured with the strappado, hoisted by his bound wrists and dropped. He confessed nothing. Freed in a general amnesty when a Medici became Pope Leo X, he withdrew to his farm, where this letter and The Prince were written."},"p":"Tonight, give yourself one hour with a great book, with the same respect you would give a great guest.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-niccolo-machiavelli-25","a":"niccolo-machiavelli"},{"ref":"Letter to Menoeceus 125","q":["Death is nothing to us.","When we exist, death is not present; and when death is present, we do not exist."],"c":["Epicurus believed that most human misery comes from fear, and the greatest fear is of death. His answer is simple. All good and bad lie in sensation, and death is the end of sensation. Where death is, we are not. What is painless when it comes should not pain us in advance."],"e":null,"p":"When a fear of the end, of anything, appears today, remind yourself that it is the fear, not the end, that is hurting you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-01","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Philodemus, Against the Sophists 4.9–14","q":["God is not to be feared; death is not to be dreaded;","what is good is easy to get; what is terrible is easy to endure."],"c":["This four-part summary, later called the tetrapharmakos or \"four-part cure\", was used by Epicureans as a quick reminder of the whole philosophy. It was written down by the Epicurean Philodemus, whose library was buried by Vesuvius at Herculaneum and partly recovered from the charred scrolls."],"e":null,"p":"Repeat the four-part cure once today when something unsettles you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-02","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Letter to Menoeceus 132","q":["It is impossible to live pleasantly without living wisely, well and justly,","and impossible to live wisely, well and justly without living pleasantly."],"c":["Critics accused Epicurus of teaching selfish pleasure-seeking. This passage shows the opposite. For him, a truly pleasant life and a virtuous life are inseparable. The virtues are the means to a calm and happy life, and a calm and happy life is impossible without them."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one moment today where doing the right thing also brought you peace.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-03","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Letter to Menoeceus 131","q":["When we say that pleasure is the goal, we do not mean the pleasures of the dissolute or of the table,","but freedom from pain in the body and from disturbance in the soul."],"c":["Epicurus explicitly rejects the popular picture of an \"epicure\". Drinking parties, feasts and luxury do not make a pleasant life, he says, but sober reasoning, which examines what to choose and avoid and drives out the opinions that disturb the soul."],"e":null,"p":"Today, seek one moment of real calm rather than one moment of excitement.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-04","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Letter to Menoeceus 122","q":["Let no one delay the study of philosophy while young, nor grow weary of it when old.","For no one is too young or too old to secure the health of the soul."],"c":["Epicurus opens his letter to Menoeceus with this invitation. Philosophy is not a subject for specialists but medicine for everyone. The young need it to face the future without fear, the old to remain young in the good things they have."],"e":null,"p":"Whatever your age, spend some time today thinking about how to live well.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-05","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Principal Doctrines 27","q":["Of all the things that wisdom provides for the happiness of a whole life,","by far the greatest is friendship."],"c":["Epicurus built his whole community around friendship. In the Garden, people lived, ate and studied together. He believed that a life without friends, even with every comfort, would be poor and anxious, and that friendship was the surest protection against life's dangers."],"e":{"when":"c. 306 BC · Athens","title":"The Garden","text":"Epicurus bought a house with a garden just outside the walls of Athens for eighty minae and settled there with his friends. The Garden admitted women and slaves, which shocked contemporaries, and became the centre of a network of Epicurean communities across the Greek world. In his will he left it to his followers for ever."},"p":"Reach out today to a friend you haven't spoken to in a while.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-06","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 34","q":["We do not so much need the help of our friends","as the confidence that they will help us."],"c":["Epicurus saw that much of friendship's value lies in security. Even if we never call on them, knowing that someone would stand by us makes life calmer and braver."],"e":null,"p":"Let one friend know today that they can count on you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-07","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 35","q":["Do not spoil what you have by desiring what you do not have.","Remember that what you now have was once among the things you only hoped for."],"c":["Desire has a way of erasing gratitude. As soon as we have something, we start wanting the next thing. Epicurus suggests looking back: much of what we take for granted was once a dream."],"e":null,"p":"Name three things you have today that you once only hoped for.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-08","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 21.7","q":["If you want to make Pythocles rich,","do not add to his money, but take away from his desires."],"c":["Seneca quotes this advice that Epicurus gave about his young friend Pythocles. Wealth is a relationship between what you have and what you want. You can increase it by getting more, or more reliably by wanting less."],"e":null,"p":"Let go of one desire today, instead of trying to satisfy it.","s":"Quoted by Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 21.7.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-09","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 4.10","q":["Poverty brought into line with the law of nature","is great wealth."],"c":["Seneca loved this line and quoted it to Lucilius. Nature asks for little: food, water, shelter, friends. If we live by that measure, we are rich. If we live by the measure of opinion and fashion, we will never have enough."],"e":null,"p":"Spend one part of today using only what you really need.","s":"Quoted by Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 4.10; compare Vatican Sayings 25.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-10","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 9.20","q":["Whoever does not think what they have is more than enough","is unhappy, even if they are master of the whole world."],"c":["Another saying of Epicurus preserved by Seneca. Happiness depends less on the size of what we have than on our attitude toward it. Alexander conquered half the world and still wept that there were no more worlds to conquer."],"e":null,"p":"Say once today, about something you own: \"This is enough.\"","s":"Quoted by Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 9.20.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-11","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 52","q":["Friendship dances around the world,","calling on each of us to wake up to happiness."],"c":["One of the most poetic lines of Epicurus. He imagines friendship as a messenger travelling across the earth, inviting everyone to the blessed life. Happiness is not found alone but in shared life."],"e":null,"p":"Invite someone to share something good with you today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-12","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Principal Doctrines 15","q":["Natural wealth is limited and easy to obtain.","The wealth demanded by empty opinion goes on without end."],"c":["Epicurus divided desires into natural and necessary ones (food, shelter), natural but unnecessary ones (fine food), and empty ones (fame, power, luxury). Natural needs are easy to meet. Empty desires can never be satisfied, because they have no natural limit."],"e":null,"p":"Before you buy or chase something today, ask whether it is a natural need or an empty desire.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-13","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 10.11","q":["Send me a little pot of cheese,","so that I can have a feast whenever I like."],"c":["Epicurus wrote this to a friend. It shows how simple his idea of pleasure was. His daily diet was mostly bread and water, and a bit of cheese was a feast. A person who enjoys simple things is easy to please and hard to make miserable."],"e":null,"p":"Treat yourself today to one very simple pleasure, and enjoy it fully.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-14","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Diogenes Laertius, Lives 10.22","q":["On this blessed day, which is also the last of my life, I write to you.","My pains are extreme, but against them all I set the joy in my soul at the memory of our conversations."],"c":["These are the opening words of Epicurus' last letter, written to his friend Idomeneus. He asks him to look after the children of his friend Metrodorus, as worthy of his lifelong devotion to Epicurus and philosophy. Even dying in pain, he looked for the good and thought of others."],"e":{"when":"270 BC · Athens","title":"The death of Epicurus","text":"Epicurus died at seventy-two after fourteen days of severe pain from a kidney stone. According to one account, he had a warm bath, drank some unmixed wine, urged his friends to remember his teachings and died. His will provided for the Garden, his slaves' freedom and an annual feast in his memory."},"p":"If you are in pain or trouble today, call to mind one good memory with a friend and hold it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-15","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 11.8","q":["Choose some good person and keep them always before your eyes,","so that you live as if they were watching and act as if they could see."],"c":["Seneca, a Stoic, quotes this Epicurean advice with full approval. Having a model in mind, real or imagined, helps us behave better when no one is watching. Seneca suggests Cato or Laelius; any good person will do."],"e":null,"p":"Choose someone you admire. Go through today as if they were watching.","s":"Quoted by Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 11.8.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-16","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 28.9","q":["The knowledge of your wrongdoing","is the beginning of your healing."],"c":["Seneca quotes this line from Epicurus. We cannot correct a fault we do not see. Many people defend their mistakes instead of recognising them. Self-improvement begins when we are honest with ourselves."],"e":null,"p":"Admit one small fault to yourself today, without excuses.","s":"Quoted by Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 28.9.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-17","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 14","q":["We are born once and cannot be born twice, and we will not exist for all eternity.","Yet you, who are not master of tomorrow, keep putting off your happiness."],"c":["Epicurus sees how much of life we waste in postponement: we will be happy when we get the job, the house, the holiday. Life slips by in waiting. Joy has to be found now, because now is all we have."],"e":null,"p":"Stop waiting for one thing today. Find a reason to be content right now.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-18","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 41","q":["We must laugh and do philosophy and manage our households and our other affairs,","all at the same time."],"c":["Philosophy, for Epicurus, was not a separate activity done in a quiet room. It belonged in the middle of daily life, together with humour, work and family. A good life is not divided into compartments."],"e":null,"p":"Bring some philosophy and some laughter into your daily tasks today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-19","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 54","q":["We must not pretend to study philosophy, but really study it.","For we need not the appearance of health, but real health."],"c":["Epicurus saw philosophy as medicine for the soul. Showing off knowledge is like pretending to be healthy: it does nothing. What counts is the real change in how we live."],"e":null,"p":"Apply one idea you have read recently to an actual situation today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-20","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 66","q":["Let us show our feeling for friends who are suffering,","not by lamenting, but by caring for them."],"c":["When friends suffer, tears are not enough. Epicurus teaches that compassion should be active. Practical help and thoughtful attention mean more than sympathy alone."],"e":null,"p":"If someone you know is going through a hard time, do one practical thing for them today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-21","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Principal Doctrines 17","q":["The just person is the most free from disturbance;","the unjust person is full of the greatest disturbance."],"c":["Epicurus argued that justice is good for us, not just for others. The unjust person lives in fear of discovery and punishment, never at peace. The just person can sleep calmly."],"e":null,"p":"Clear up one small matter today where you have been less than fair.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-22","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Plutarch, Is \"Live Unnoticed\" Well Said? 1128c","q":["Live unnoticed."],"c":["In Greek, lathe biōsas. It was one of Epicurus' best-known maxims, so famous that Plutarch wrote a whole essay attacking it. Epicurus advised staying out of politics and the pursuit of fame. A quiet, private life with friends is safer and more peaceful than a life in the public eye."],"e":null,"p":"Do one good thing today without seeking any recognition for it.","s":"Maxim quoted and criticised by Plutarch in the essay of that title.","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-23","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Letter to Menoeceus 130","q":["We regard self-sufficiency as a great good,","so that we may be content with little, knowing that those who need luxury least enjoy it most."],"c":["Epicurus did not reject luxury entirely. But he argued that people who can live simply enjoy rich things more when they come, and are not hurt when they go. Simple living is not deprivation but freedom."],"e":null,"p":"Choose the simpler option once today, and notice whether you lose anything real.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-24","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Vatican Sayings 81","q":["The disturbance of the soul cannot be ended, nor real joy produced,","by the greatest wealth, nor by honour and respect from the crowd."],"c":["Epicurus saw that external success does not reach the source of anxiety. A troubled mind remains troubled in a palace. Only understanding, and the removal of false opinions, brings real peace."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one way you are trying to fix an inner problem with an outer solution today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-epicurus-25","a":"epicurus"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Listening to Lectures","q":["The mind does not need filling like a jar.","Like firewood, it needs only kindling, to set it burning with the desire to think for itself and to love the truth."],"c":["Plutarch wrote this essay for a young man starting to attend philosophy lectures. Listening well, he says, is half of learning. But the aim of education is not to pour facts into a student; it is to light a fire. This line, usually shortened to \"the mind is not a vessel to be filled but a fire to be kindled\", has been quoted by teachers ever since."],"e":null,"p":"Instead of absorbing more information today, take one idea and think it through for yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-01","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Alexander 1","q":["It is not always in the most famous deeds that virtue or vice is revealed.","Often a small thing, a word or a joke, shows character more clearly than battles where thousands fall."],"c":["Plutarch explains his method at the start of the Life of Alexander. He is not writing history but lives. Like a portrait painter who focuses on the face and eyes, he looks for the small signs that reveal the soul."],"e":null,"p":"Pay attention today to the small things you do when no one is impressed. They are your real portrait.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-02","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Pericles 1–2","q":["Virtue, by its very deeds, so moves us","that we admire the actions and at the same time want to imitate those who did them."],"c":["Plutarch wrote his Lives to give readers examples to follow. Admiring a fine sculpture doesn't make us want to be a sculptor, he says, but admiring a good action makes us want to act well. Good examples are contagious."],"e":null,"p":"Think of someone whose actions you admire. Imitate one of them today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-03","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Aemilius Paulus 1","q":["I began writing these Lives for the sake of others,","but I continue them for my own sake, using history as a mirror to arrange and adorn my own life."],"c":["Plutarch admits that his long work on famous lives became a personal exercise. Spending time with great people of the past, he says, is like living with them: we receive each as a guest and study who they were. It teaches us to drive away anything base from our own lives."],"e":null,"p":"Read about one life today, historical or personal, and ask what it shows you about your own.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-04","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Theseus 23","q":["The ship on which Theseus sailed was kept by the Athenians, who removed old planks as they decayed and put in new ones,","so that philosophers took it as an example in the argument about growth: some said it remained the same ship, others that it did not."],"c":["Plutarch records the famous puzzle known as the Ship of Theseus. If every part is gradually replaced, is it still the same ship? The question applies to us too: our bodies, habits and ideas change constantly. What makes us the same person?"],"e":null,"p":"Name one part of yourself you have replaced over the years. Are you still the same? What makes you so?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-05","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Tranquility of Mind","q":["Instead of always looking at those who are better off,","look also at those who are worse off, and you will find much to be grateful for."],"c":["Plutarch wrote this essay for his friend Paccius, a busy Roman official. He observes that people make themselves miserable by comparing upward. A prisoner envies the free; the free envy the rich; the rich envy kings. Turning the comparison around brings contentment."],"e":null,"p":"When you find yourself comparing upward today, make one comparison downward as well.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-06","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Tranquility of Mind","q":["The universe is a most holy temple,","and every day is a festival for a good person."],"c":["Plutarch ends his essay on tranquillity with this image, borrowed from a saying of Diogenes the Cynic. Life is like a great festival, and we are guests admitted to see the sun, moon and stars. Why spend it complaining?"],"e":null,"p":"Treat today as a festival. Find one moment to celebrate.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-07","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Moral Progress","q":["A sign of progress is when you are no longer ashamed to be corrected,","but rather glad of it, as a sick person is glad of a doctor."],"c":["Plutarch describes the signs that someone is really improving. One of the clearest is how they react to criticism. Beginners defend themselves; those making progress welcome correction because it helps them heal."],"e":null,"p":"Ask someone today for honest feedback, and thank them for it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-08","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On the Control of Anger","q":["Just as with a fever, it is easier to stop anger at its start.","When it is beginning, keep silent, and do not fan it with words."],"c":["Plutarch's essay on anger takes the form of a dialogue with a friend who has learned to control his temper. The key advice is to act early: anger feeds on speech. Silence starves it."],"e":null,"p":"The next time anger rises today, keep silent for a full minute before responding.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-09","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, How to Profit by One's Enemies","q":["How shall I defend myself against my enemy?","By being good and honourable yourself."],"c":["Plutarch quotes Diogenes the Cynic and builds an essay around the idea. An enemy is a free critic who watches everything you do. Instead of resenting them, use them: let their attention make you more careful, honest and self-controlled."],"e":null,"p":"Think of someone who criticises you. Use one of their criticisms to improve today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-10","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Talkativeness","q":["Often we regret having spoken,","but never having kept silent."],"c":["Plutarch's essay on talkativeness is full of stories of secrets betrayed and lives ruined by loose tongues. He quotes the poet Simonides, who said he had often regretted speaking but never silence."],"e":null,"p":"Hold back one comment today that you might regret.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-11","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, Consolation to His Wife","q":["Let us not forget the joy she brought us.","It would be unjust to remember only the grief and to cancel all the happiness she gave."],"c":["Plutarch wrote to his wife Timoxena after the death of their two-year-old daughter, also named Timoxena. He praises his wife's calm and asks her to remember their daughter with gratitude, not just pain. It is one of the most moving letters to survive from the ancient world."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 90 · Tanagra and Chaeronea","title":"The death of little Timoxena","text":"Plutarch was travelling when the news reached him that his little daughter had died. Their only daughter after four sons, she had been loved for her gentle nature: she asked her nurse to feed her dolls too. Plutarch's letter tells his wife that he has heard how she bore the loss with dignity and without empty display."},"p":"When you think of someone you've lost, choose to remember one joyful moment with them today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-12","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Exile","q":["By nature there is no such thing as a native country,","any more than there is by nature a house, a field or a forge."],"c":["Plutarch wrote this to a friend who had been exiled. Borders are drawn by people; the sky, the sun and the stars are the same everywhere. Socrates, he recalls, said that he was not an Athenian or a Greek, but a citizen of the world."],"e":null,"p":"Think of yourself today as a citizen of the world. How does that change one decision?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-13","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On the E at Delphi","q":["Know yourself.","Nothing in excess."],"c":["These two sayings were carved at the temple of Apollo at Delphi, where Plutarch served as priest. He discusses them, and the mysterious letter E also inscribed there, in several dialogues. For Plutarch they summed up the wisdom of Greece: self-knowledge and moderation."],"e":{"when":"c. AD 95–120 · Delphi","title":"Priest of Apollo","text":"For around thirty years Plutarch served as one of the two priests of Apollo at Delphi, the most famous oracle of the ancient world, then in decline. He helped restore the sanctuary, and the people of Delphi and Chaeronea set up a portrait of him in gratitude. The bust on his coin is thought to be it."},"p":"Spend a moment today on each of the Delphic sayings: one thing you know about yourself, and one thing you can do less of.","s":"The sayings are ancient Delphic maxims discussed by Plutarch, not his own coinage.","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-14","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Having Many Friends","q":["The pursuit of many friends prevents us","from making real friends at all."],"c":["Plutarch argues that true friendship requires time, attention and shared experience. If we spread ourselves too thin, we have many acquaintances and no friends. Better a few close friends than a crowd of half-friends."],"e":null,"p":"Put extra effort into one close friendship today instead of many shallow ones.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-15","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, How to Tell a Flatterer from a Friend","q":["I don't need a friend who changes when I change and nods when I nod.","My shadow does that much better."],"c":["Plutarch warns against flatterers, who agree with everything to win favour. A real friend sometimes disagrees, tells you the truth and stays the same when you change for the worse."],"e":null,"p":"Value one person today who disagrees with you. Tell them you appreciate their honesty.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-16","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Listening to Lectures","q":["Learn to listen, and you will profit even from those who speak badly."],"c":["Plutarch advises young listeners not to dismiss a speaker because of poor style. A good listener can find something worthwhile even in a weak lecture, by attending to the content, not the delivery."],"e":null,"p":"In a conversation today with someone who bores you, look for one useful thing they say.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-17","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, Political Precepts","q":["It is not the office that honours the man,","but the man who honours the office."],"c":["Plutarch tells the story of the Theban general Epaminondas. His enemies, to humiliate him, made him supervisor of street cleaning. Epaminondas took the job seriously and turned it into a respected position. Plutarch himself held humble offices in his home town and was mocked for supervising the dumping of rubble."],"e":null,"p":"Do a humble task today with the same seriousness you would bring to an important one.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-18","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Superstition","q":["The atheist thinks there are no gods;","the superstitious person wishes there were none, but believes against their will, out of fear."],"c":["Plutarch was a priest and believed in the gods. But he thought superstition was worse than atheism, because it made people live in constant fear of divine punishment. Real religion, he argued, should bring hope and joy."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one fear today that is based on superstition or habit rather than reason. Let it go.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-19","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Sertorius 16","q":["Perseverance is more powerful than violence.","Many things that cannot be overcome all at once yield when taken little by little."],"c":["To teach his soldiers patience, the Roman general Sertorius had two men try to pull out the tail of a horse. A strong man pulled at the whole tail at once and failed. A weak man pulled out the hairs one by one and succeeded. Plutarch tells the story and draws this lesson."],"e":null,"p":"Break one big problem into small parts today and pull out one hair.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-20","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Curiosity","q":["Why do you look so sharply at the faults of others,","and overlook your own?"],"c":["Plutarch's essay on curiosity targets the busybody who wants to know other people's secrets. He suggests turning that curiosity inward: there is plenty to investigate in our own lives, and the results are much more useful."],"e":null,"p":"Every time you notice someone else's fault today, look for one of your own.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-21","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Compliance","q":["Those who cannot say no","are led into many shameful things."],"c":["Plutarch's essay addresses excessive shyness, the fear of disappointing people. He gives practical advice: practise saying no in small matters, so that you can do it in large ones. Being unable to refuse is not kindness but weakness."],"e":null,"p":"Say no to one small request today that you would normally accept against your judgment.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-22","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Life of Demosthenes 2","q":["I live in a small city,","and I stay there willingly so that it does not become even smaller."],"c":["Plutarch explains why he chose to stay in his small hometown of Chaeronea, though he could have lived in Rome or Athens. It is a light remark, but it shows his loyalty to his community. He made it a centre of learning, and Chaeronea's fame today rests largely on him."],"e":null,"p":"Do something today to make your own community a little bigger in spirit.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-23","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Moralia, On Moral Virtue","q":["Character is formed by habit.","The ethical virtues are produced by reason working on our emotions through practice."],"c":["Plutarch follows Aristotle in this view: character (ēthos) gets its name from habit (ethos). We are not simply born good or bad. Through repeated action guided by reason, our emotions are shaped and become virtues."],"e":null,"p":"Repeat one good action today, consciously, as training.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-plutarch-24","a":"plutarch"},{"ref":"Analects 1.1","q":["Is it not a pleasure to learn something and practise it at the right time?","Is it not a joy to have friends come from far away? Is it not noble to remain unbothered when others do not recognise you?"],"c":["These are the first words of the Analects, the collection of Confucius' sayings put together by his disciples. They set out his whole programme in three lines: steady learning put into practice, friendship based on shared values, and a quiet independence from the need for recognition."],"e":null,"p":"Learn one thing today and use it before the day ends.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-01","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 2.4","q":["At fifteen I set my heart on learning. At thirty I stood firm. At forty I had no doubts. At fifty I knew the will of Heaven.","At sixty my ear was attuned. At seventy I could follow my heart's desire without overstepping what is right."],"c":["Confucius sums up his life in a few lines. The final stage is the most striking: after a lifetime of training, what he wants and what is right have become the same. For the Stoics too, the goal was a character so formed that doing right feels natural."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself where you are on your own path. What is the next stage you are working toward?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-02","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 2.17","q":["Shall I teach you what knowledge is?","When you know a thing, to recognise that you know it; when you do not know it, to recognise that you do not know it. That is knowledge."],"c":["Confucius says this to his disciple Zilu, who was brave but rash and inclined to claim more than he knew. The lesson is the same as Socrates': true knowledge begins with honesty about the limits of what we know."],"e":null,"p":"Say \"I don't know\" at least once today when it's true.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-03","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 15.24","q":["Is there one word that can guide a person throughout life?","\"Reciprocity\", perhaps: what you do not want for yourself, do not do to others."],"c":["Asked by his disciple Zigong for a single principle to live by, Confucius gave this answer. It is one of the earliest forms of what is now called the Golden Rule, stated five centuries before Christ."],"e":null,"p":"Before acting toward someone today, ask how you would feel on the receiving end.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-04","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 4.17","q":["When you see a person of worth, think of how to become their equal.","When you see someone unworthy, turn inward and examine yourself."],"c":["Confucius turns every encounter into a lesson. Good people show us what to aim for. Bad examples are not a reason to feel superior, but a mirror: do I have this fault too?"],"e":null,"p":"When someone irritates you today, ask whether you share any part of the fault you see.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-05","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 7.22","q":["When three people walk together, one of them is sure to be my teacher.","I pick out their good qualities and follow them, and their bad qualities and correct them in myself."],"c":["Confucius believed there is something to learn from everyone. The good show us what to imitate; the bad show us what to avoid. No encounter is wasted for someone who is willing to learn."],"e":null,"p":"In each conversation today, look for one thing to learn from the other person.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-06","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 9.28","q":["Only when the year grows cold","do we see that the pine and the cypress are the last to lose their leaves."],"c":["In good times, everyone looks strong. It is only in hardship that we see who truly has character, just as evergreens stand out only in winter. Difficulty reveals what easy times hide."],"e":{"when":"c. 489 BC · Between Chen and Cai","title":"Hunger on the road","text":"During his years of wandering, Confucius and his disciples were trapped between the states of Chen and Cai, surrounded by hostile forces. They ran out of food, and many fell ill. Confucius went on teaching, reciting and playing his lute. When the angry Zilu asked if a gentleman ever had to suffer such want, Confucius answered that a gentleman holds firm in hardship."},"p":"When something hard happens today, see it as the cold that shows your evergreen qualities.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-07","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 15.2","q":["The exemplary person holds firm in hardship.","The petty person in hardship loses all restraint."],"c":["Confucius gave this answer when his disciple Zilu, angry and hungry, asked whether a gentleman should ever be reduced to such need. Hardship comes to all. What differs is how we behave in it."],"e":null,"p":"When you feel pressed today, hold to your standards a little more firmly.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-08","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 4.14","q":["Do not worry about having no position; worry about whether you are fit to hold one.","Do not worry that no one knows you; seek to be worth knowing."],"c":["Confucius spent much of his life without the office he wanted. Instead of complaining, he focused on what was in his control: becoming capable. Recognition is not up to us; worthiness is."],"e":null,"p":"Spend effort today on becoming more capable, not on being noticed.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-09","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 15.21","q":["The exemplary person makes demands on themselves.","The petty person makes demands on others."],"c":["The junzi, the exemplary person, looks inward first when something goes wrong. The petty person blames others. It is the same distinction Epictetus made between the educated and the uneducated."],"e":null,"p":"When something goes wrong today, ask first what you could have done differently.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-10","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 2.15","q":["Learning without thinking is useless.","Thinking without learning is dangerous."],"c":["Confucius balances study and reflection. Reading and memorising without thinking leaves us confused. Thinking without studying what others have learned leaves us in danger of foolish mistakes."],"e":null,"p":"Today, take one thing you've read recently and spend ten minutes thinking about it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-11","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 9.17","q":["Standing by a river, the Master said:","\"It passes on like this, never ceasing, day or night.\""],"c":["This short scene has been interpreted in many ways: as a lament for passing time, as a symbol of tireless effort, as a reminder of constant change. Like Heraclitus at almost the same time on the other side of the world, Confucius saw in a river the image of life itself."],"e":null,"p":"Watch something that keeps moving today: water, traffic, clouds. Let it remind you that today, too, is passing.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-12","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 7.16","q":["Eating coarse rice, drinking water, using my bent arm for a pillow, there is still joy in these.","Wealth and honours gained unjustly are to me like floating clouds."],"c":["Confucius describes a simple life without regret. Joy does not depend on luxury; it depends on living rightly. Wealth gained wrongly is as unreal and passing as a cloud."],"e":null,"p":"Find joy in one simple thing today, and let go of one thing you want that isn't worth its price.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-13","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 6.11","q":["How worthy was Hui! A single bowl of rice, a ladle of water, living in a poor alley.","Others could not have borne the hardship, but Hui never let it spoil his joy."],"c":["Yan Hui was Confucius' favourite disciple, poor all his life but devoted to learning and virtue. Confucius praises him for finding contentment in the simplest conditions. It is the same freedom that Epicurus and Seneca praised."],"e":{"when":"c. 481 BC · Lu","title":"The death of Yan Hui","text":"Yan Hui died young, around the age of forty. Confucius wept bitterly and cried out: \"Alas! Heaven has destroyed me! Heaven has destroyed me!\" When disciples said he was grieving excessively, he answered: \"If I am not to grieve for this man, for whom should I grieve?\""},"p":"Be content today with what you have, as Hui was.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-14","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 12.1","q":["To master oneself and return to propriety is humaneness.","If for one day you master yourself and return to propriety, the whole world will turn to humaneness."],"c":["Asked by Yan Hui about ren, humaneness or goodness, Confucius gave this answer. Goodness starts with self-discipline. And it comes from oneself, not from others. When Hui asked for specifics, Confucius said: look at nothing, listen to nothing, say nothing and do nothing contrary to propriety."],"e":null,"p":"Master yourself in one small moment today, and act with care toward others.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-15","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 15.30","q":["To make a mistake and not correct it:","that is what is truly called a mistake."],"c":["Everyone makes mistakes. Confucius does not condemn them; he condemns not correcting them. Errors are part of learning. Refusing to admit and fix them is the real failure."],"e":null,"p":"Correct one mistake today instead of defending it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-16","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 1.8","q":["Hold faithfulness and sincerity as first principles.","When you have faults, do not be afraid to abandon them."],"c":["Confucius tells his disciples that they will not be respected if they are not serious, and that their learning will not be solid. Sincerity, loyalty and a willingness to change are the foundations of character."],"e":null,"p":"Let go of one fault today without being afraid of what it says about you.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-17","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 4.16","q":["The exemplary person understands what is right.","The petty person understands what is profitable."],"c":["Confucius often contrasts the junzi, the exemplary person, with the xiaoren, the petty person. The difference is in what they look at. One asks: is it right? The other asks: what do I gain?"],"e":null,"p":"In one choice today, ask \"is it right?\" before asking \"what's in it for me?\"","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-18","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 2.1","q":["One who governs through virtue","is like the North Star, which stays in its place while all the other stars pay homage to it."],"c":["Confucius believed that leaders should rule by moral example rather than by force or punishment. A virtuous leader stays steady, and others naturally orient themselves around them."],"e":{"when":"c. 500 BC · Jiagu","title":"The meeting at Jiagu","text":"According to tradition, Confucius accompanied the Duke of Lu to a peace meeting with the more powerful state of Qi. When Qi tried to intimidate the duke with armed dancers, Confucius insisted on proper ritual and rebuked the Qi ruler. Ashamed, Qi returned lands it had taken from Lu."},"p":"Lead by example today in one area where others watch you.","s":"Jiagu: Zuo Zhuan, Duke Ding 10; Sima Qian, Records of the Grand Historian 47.","t":"","id":"q-confucius-19","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 12.17","q":["To govern is to set things right.","If you lead by being upright, who will dare not to be upright?"],"c":["Asked by a powerful minister of Lu how to govern, Confucius played on the similarity of two Chinese words: zheng, \"to govern\", and zheng, \"to be upright\". Government begins with the ruler's own conduct."],"e":null,"p":"Before asking anyone else to do the right thing today, do it yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-20","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 13.3","q":["If names are not correct, language is not in accord with the truth of things.","If language is not in accord with the truth of things, affairs cannot be carried on to success."],"c":["Asked what he would do first if he governed, Confucius surprised his disciples: he would rectify the names. When words no longer match reality, when a ruler is not acting as a ruler or a father as a father, confusion spreads through everything."],"e":null,"p":"Call one thing by its honest name today, even if it's uncomfortable.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-21","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 7.8","q":["I do not open the way for those who are not eager to learn, nor help out those who are not anxious to explain themselves.","If I show one corner and they cannot come back with the other three, I do not repeat the lesson."],"c":["Confucius expected his students to think for themselves. A teacher can point the way, but the student has to walk it. Learning that is spoon-fed does not last."],"e":null,"p":"When you learn something today, try to work out the \"other three corners\" yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-22","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 14.27","q":["The exemplary person is ashamed","when their words exceed their deeds."],"c":["Confucius distrusted fine speech. Talking is easy; acting is hard. He wanted his students' actions to go beyond their words, not the other way round."],"e":null,"p":"Today, do more than you say.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-23","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 17.2","q":["By nature people are close to one another.","Through practice they grow far apart."],"c":["Confucius believed people are born much alike. What separates the good from the bad is not birth but habit and training. Like Aristotle and the Stoics, he saw character as something built through daily practice."],"e":null,"p":"Practise one good habit today, knowing it is shaping who you become.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-confucius-24","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"Analects 9.26","q":["The commander of three armies can be taken from them,","but the will of even a common person cannot be taken from them."],"c":["An army can lose its general, but no one can take away a person's resolve unless they give it up themselves. It is one of the most Stoic sentences in the Analects: our will is our own."],"e":{"when":"c. 496 BC · Kuang","title":"Mistaken for an enemy","text":"While travelling, Confucius was surrounded at Kuang by people who mistook him for Yang Hu, a hated official who had once oppressed them. He and his disciples were held for days. Confucius stayed calm, saying that if Heaven meant this culture to survive, the people of Kuang could do nothing to him."},"p":"Decide one thing today that no pressure will make you give up.","s":"Kuang: Analects 9.5.","t":"","id":"q-confucius-25","a":"confucius"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 276","q":["I want to learn more and more to see what is necessary in things as beautiful.","Then I shall be one of those who make things beautiful. Amor fati: let that be my love from now on."],"c":["Nietzsche wrote this at the start of Book 4 of The Gay Science, as a New Year's resolution for 1882. He adds that he does not want to wage war against what is ugly, or accuse, or even accuse the accusers. \"Looking away shall be my only negation.\" It is his version of the Stoic acceptance of fate, but warmer: not only to bear what happens, but to love it."],"e":null,"p":"Find one thing today that you cannot change, and look for what is beautiful in it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-01","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Ecce Homo, \"Why I Am So Clever\" 10","q":["My formula for greatness in a human being is amor fati:","that one wants nothing to be different, not forward, not backward, not in all eternity."],"c":["In Ecce Homo, his strange autobiography written just before his breakdown, Nietzsche goes further than the Stoics. Not merely to endure what is necessary, still less to hide from it, he says, but to love it."],"e":null,"p":"Look at one painful part of your past. Can you see how it helped make you who you are?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-02","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Twilight of the Idols, \"Maxims and Arrows\" 8","q":["From life's school of war:","what does not kill me makes me stronger."],"c":["This is one of the most quoted lines Nietzsche ever wrote, often used carelessly. For him it was personal. He lived with constant migraines, near-blindness and stomach illness, and turned the suffering into a discipline for his thinking."],"e":{"when":"1870 · Franco-Prussian War","title":"The medical orderly","text":"A Swiss citizen and Basel professor, Nietzsche volunteered as a medical orderly in the Prussian army. He spent days in a cattle car caring for wounded soldiers and contracted dysentery and diphtheria. His health never fully recovered, and the rest of his life was shaped by illness."},"p":"Name one hardship that left you stronger. Remember it the next time something hard comes.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-03","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Twilight of the Idols, \"Maxims and Arrows\" 12","q":["If we have our own why of life,","we can bear almost any how."],"c":["Nietzsche says that a person with a strong reason for living can endure almost any circumstance. Meaning makes hardship bearable. Without it, even comfort becomes unbearable."],"e":{"when":"1942–1945 · Nazi concentration camps","title":"Frankl's why","text":"The Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl survived Theresienstadt, Auschwitz and Dachau. In his book Man's Search for Meaning (1946), he quoted Nietzsche's line again and again: prisoners who still had a reason to live, a person waiting for them, a work to finish, were more likely to survive. The line became the motto of his logotherapy."},"p":"Write down your own \"why\" in one sentence. Read it when the \"how\" gets hard today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-04","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 290","q":["One thing is needful: to give style to one's character.","A great and rare art."],"c":["Nietzsche describes how a person can survey all the strengths and weaknesses of their nature and shape them into an artistic plan: adding a piece here, removing one there, until even the weaknesses delight the eye. Character is not simply given; it can be crafted, like a work of art."],"e":null,"p":"Pick one trait you don't like in yourself. How could you shape it into something that fits the whole?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-05","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 283","q":["The secret for harvesting the greatest fruitfulness and greatest enjoyment from existence is: live dangerously!","Build your cities on the slopes of Vesuvius! Send your ships into uncharted seas!"],"c":["Nietzsche writes for \"more courageous\" people who seek knowledge as an adventure. The comfortable life, he thinks, makes people small. Growth requires risk. It is not a call to recklessness but to courage."],"e":null,"p":"Take one small, deliberate risk today that could help you grow.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-06","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 341","q":["What if some day or night a demon were to steal into your loneliest loneliness and say to you:","\"This life as you now live it and have lived it, you will have to live once more and innumerable times more.\""],"c":["Nietzsche introduces his thought of the eternal recurrence. Would you curse the demon, or call him a god? The test is simple and terrifying: live in such a way that you could will every moment to return forever."],"e":{"when":"August 1881 · Sils Maria, Switzerland","title":"The rock at Surlej","text":"Walking beside Lake Silvaplana in the Upper Engadine, Nietzsche stopped by a huge pyramid-shaped rock near Surlej. There, he later wrote, the thought of eternal recurrence came to him, \"6,000 feet beyond man and time\". He spent seven summers in Sils Maria; his rented room is now a museum."},"p":"Live one hour today in a way you would be happy to repeat forever.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-07","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Beyond Good and Evil 146","q":["Whoever fights monsters should see to it that in the process they do not become a monster.","And if you gaze long into an abyss, the abyss also gazes into you."],"c":["Nietzsche warns that the struggle against something bad can change us into what we fight. Constant attention to darkness can darken us. Marcus Aurelius said the same in a different way: the best revenge is not to become like the wrongdoer."],"e":null,"p":"In a struggle today, check that you are not becoming what you oppose.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-08","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Beyond Good and Evil 9","q":["You want to live \"according to nature\"? O you noble Stoics, what deceptive words!","Imagine a being like nature, wasteful beyond measure, indifferent beyond measure, without purpose or consideration."],"c":["Nietzsche mocks the Stoic formula. Nature, he says, is not rational or kind; the Stoics read their own ideals into it and then claimed to follow it. It is a sharp challenge to the Stoics, and a useful one: are we following nature, or our own picture of it?"],"e":null,"p":"Examine one \"natural\" assumption you live by. Is it really nature, or a story you've told yourself?","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-09","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Prologue 5","q":["I say unto you: one must still have chaos in oneself","to be able to give birth to a dancing star."],"c":["Zarathustra speaks to a crowd in the marketplace. He warns that a time will come when people will no longer have chaos in them, and so will create nothing great. Inner turmoil, rightly used, is the raw material of creation."],"e":null,"p":"Instead of fighting some inner restlessness today, ask what it might help you create.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-10","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Prologue 4","q":["Man is a rope, stretched between animal and overman:","a rope over an abyss."],"c":["Nietzsche's Zarathustra sees humanity not as a finished product but as a bridge, something in transition. What is great in a human being is that they are a crossing, not a destination."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself today what you are crossing toward.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-11","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra, \"On Reading and Writing\"","q":["Of all that is written I love only what a person has written with their blood.","Write with blood, and you will learn that blood is spirit."],"c":["Nietzsche despised writing that was merely clever or correct. He valued writing that came from real experience and cost the writer something. The Meditations of Marcus Aurelius would have passed his test."],"e":null,"p":"Write a few honest lines today about something you have really lived.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-12","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Twilight of the Idols, \"Maxims and Arrows\" 33","q":["Without music","life would be a mistake."],"c":["Nietzsche was a passionate musician and composer, who played the piano daily and wrote songs and piano works. Music, for him, was the art closest to life itself. His friendship and later break with Richard Wagner shaped his whole philosophy."],"e":{"when":"1869–1872 · Tribschen, Lake Lucerne","title":"Visits to Wagner","text":"As a young professor in Basel, Nietzsche visited Richard Wagner and Cosima von Bülow at their villa at Tribschen more than twenty times. He was present when the Siegfried Idyll was first played on the staircase on Christmas morning 1870. He later called those days the best of his life, even after his bitter break with Wagner."},"p":"Listen to one piece of music today with full attention, doing nothing else.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-13","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Twilight of the Idols, \"Maxims and Arrows\" 34","q":["Only thoughts reached by walking","have any value."],"c":["Nietzsche walked for hours every day, often six to eight hours, with a notebook. He wrote most of his books from notes taken on these walks in the mountains and along the coast. He distrusted ideas that came from sitting still."],"e":null,"p":"Take a walk today without your phone, and see what thoughts come.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-14","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 270","q":["What does your conscience say?","\"You shall become who you are.\""],"c":["This short aphorism echoes the ancient Greek poet Pindar, and Nietzsche later used it as the subtitle of Ecce Homo: \"How One Becomes What One Is\". The task of life is not to become someone else, but to grow into the person you already are in potential."],"e":null,"p":"Do one thing today that is truly you, not what others expect.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-15","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Human, All Too Human I, 483","q":["Convictions are more dangerous enemies of truth","than lies."],"c":["A liar knows he is lying. A person with fixed convictions has stopped looking. Nietzsche argues that strong beliefs can block inquiry more effectively than deliberate falsehood."],"e":null,"p":"Hold one of your convictions more loosely today. Look for evidence against it.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-16","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Daybreak 573","q":["The snake that cannot shed its skin perishes.","So too with minds that are prevented from changing their opinions: they cease to be minds."],"c":["Nietzsche believed that growth requires letting go of old ideas. A mind that never changes is dead. Being willing to revise one's views is not weakness but life."],"e":null,"p":"Change your mind about one small thing today, if the evidence asks you to.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-17","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Antichrist 55","q":["The most common lie","is the one we tell ourselves."],"c":["Nietzsche notes that lying to others is rare compared with lying to oneself: not wanting to see something as it is. Self-deception is the foundation on which other deceptions are built. Marcus Aurelius made the same point when he wrote that he sought the truth, by which no one was ever harmed."],"e":null,"p":"Name one thing you have been avoiding seeing clearly about yourself.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-18","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Schopenhauer as Educator 1","q":["No one can build you the bridge on which you, and only you, must cross the river of life.","There may be countless paths and bridges and demigods ready to carry you across, but only at the price of yourself."],"c":["In this early essay Nietzsche urges young people to stop following others and find their own path. Teachers can show the way, but no one can walk it for you."],"e":null,"p":"Take one step today on a path only you can walk.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-19","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra, \"On the Spirit of Gravity\"","q":["\"This is my way; where is yours?\"","So I answered those who asked me \"the way\". For the way does not exist."],"c":["Zarathustra refuses to give his followers a single correct path. Each person must find their own. This is the opposite of a rule book: it is an invitation to self-responsibility."],"e":null,"p":"Instead of asking someone what to do today, decide your own way.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-20","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Twilight of the Idols, \"Maxims and Arrows\" 26","q":["I distrust all systematisers and avoid them.","The will to a system is a lack of integrity."],"c":["Nietzsche distrusted philosophers who forced everything into a tidy system. Reality is too complex, he thought, and the urge to make it fit shows that the thinker cares more about order than about truth. That is why he wrote in aphorisms."],"e":null,"p":"Notice one place where you are forcing a messy reality into a neat explanation. Let it stay messy.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-21","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"On the Use and Abuse of History for Life 1","q":["Consider the cattle, grazing as they pass you by: they do not know what yesterday or today is.","They leap about, eat, rest, digest and leap again, from morning to night, from day to day."],"c":["Nietzsche envies the animal's freedom from memory. Humans carry the past as a weight, and too much of it can crush life. A healthy person, he argues, knows how to forget as well as to remember."],"e":null,"p":"Let go of one memory today that is weighing on you without teaching you anything.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-22","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra, \"On Self-Overcoming\"","q":["And life itself told me this secret:","\"Behold,\" it said, \"I am that which must always overcome itself.\""],"c":["For Nietzsche, life is not a fixed state but continual growth. Everything alive pushes beyond itself. The best in a human being is their ability to overcome what they were."],"e":null,"p":"Overcome one small habit or fear today.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-23","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"The Gay Science 125","q":["God is dead. God remains dead. And we have killed him.","How shall we comfort ourselves, the murderers of all murderers?"],"c":["These words are spoken by a madman who runs into the marketplace with a lantern in broad daylight. The crowd laughs at him. Nietzsche is not celebrating; he is describing a crisis: if the old foundations of meaning are gone, what will people live by? His answer was that we must find or create values ourselves."],"e":null,"p":"Ask yourself today what your values really rest on.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-24","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"},{"ref":"Ecce Homo, \"Why I Am So Wise\" 6","q":["Freedom from resentment, understanding resentment:","who knows how much I ultimately owe to my long illness for this too!"],"c":["Nietzsche writes that resentment, the poisonous brooding over injuries, is the most harmful reaction of all, especially for someone weak or ill. He learned to avoid it as a kind of self-preservation, accepting even what was hard, like a Russian soldier who lies down in the snow without fighting it."],"e":{"when":"3 January 1889 · Turin","title":"The collapse","text":"Nietzsche suffered a complete mental breakdown in Turin. A famous but unverified story says it began when he threw his arms around a horse being beaten in the Piazza Carlo Alberto. He sent strange letters to friends, was brought back to Basel by his friend Franz Overbeck, and never recovered. He died in Weimar in 1900."},"p":"Let go of one resentment today, for your own sake.","s":"","t":"","id":"q-friedrich-nietzsche-25","a":"friedrich-nietzsche"}];
